var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Plankensteiner, D.; Hotter, C. and Ritsch, H. (2022). QuantumCumulants.jl: A Julia framework for generalized mean-field equations in open quantum systems. Quantum 6, 617.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Schrieffer-Wolff-Transformation","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"The Schrieffer-Wolff transformation is a perturbative unitary transformation that block-diagonalises a Hamiltonian order by order in a small parameter.  It is widely used to derive effective low-energy Hamiltonians –- for example the dispersive Hamiltonian of circuit QED from the Jaynes-Cummings model.\n\nGiven a Hamiltonian H = H_0 + V split into a solvable diagonal part H_0 and a small off-diagonal perturbation V, one seeks an anti-Hermitian generator S so that the transformed Hamiltonian tildeH = e^S H e^-S is block-diagonal. Expanding the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff series and choosing S order by order to cancel off-diagonal terms, the effective Hamiltonian becomes\n\nH_mathrmeff = H_0\n  + tfrac12S_1 V\n  + tfrac12S_2 V - tfrac124S_1S_1S_1V\n  + mathcalO(g^6)\n\nwhere S_n H_0 cancels the off-diagonal piece at order 2n-1 in the coupling g.\n\nIn this example we carry out the transformation symbolically with SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl, deriving both the dispersive shift chi (second order) and the Kerr nonlinearity K (fourth order).","title":"Schrieffer-Wolff Transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Setup","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"We consider a single cavity mode coupled to a two-level atom (Jaynes-Cummings model).  Working in the interaction picture with respect to the cavity frequency omega_0, the only energy scale in H_0 is the detuning Delta = omega_a - omega_0.  This eliminates omega_0 from all intermediate expressions and keeps the algebra clean.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nha = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\nh = hc ⊗ ha\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\n\nσge = Transition(h, :σ, 1, 2, 2)   # |g⟩⟨e|  (lowering)\nσeg = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1, 2)   # |e⟩⟨g|  (raising)\nσee = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2, 2)   # |e⟩⟨e|  (excited-state projector)\n\n@variables g Δ\n\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Num}:\n g\n Δ","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Jaynes-Cummings-Hamiltonian-(interaction-picture)","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"In the rotating-wave approximation the Hamiltonian splits into:\n\nH_0 = Delta eranglelangle e qquad\nV   = gbigl(a^dagger granglelangle e + a eranglelangle gbigr)\n\nH0 = Δ * σee\nV = g * (a' * σge + a * σeg)\n\ng * a * σ₂₁ + g * a' * σ₁₂","title":"Jaynes-Cummings Hamiltonian (interaction picture)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Second-order:-dispersive-shift","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"The first-order generator satisfies S_1 H_0 = -V.  For the Jaynes-Cummings interaction the solution is:\n\nS_1 = -fracgDeltabigl(a^dagger granglelangle e\n  - aeranglelangle gbigr)\n\nS1 = -(g / Δ) * (a' * σge - a * σeg)\n\n(g / Δ) * a * σ₂₁ - (g / Δ) * a' * σ₁₂\n\nWe can verify that the defining equation is satisfied exactly:\n\ncommutator(S1, H0) + V\n\n0\n\nThe second-order effective Hamiltonian is H_0 + tfrac12S_1 V:\n\nH_eff = H0 + commutator(S1, V) / 2\n\n((g^2) / Δ + Δ) * σ₂₂ - ((g^2) / Δ) * a' * a * σ₁₁ + ((g^2) / Δ) * a' * a * σ₂₂\n\nReading off the result, we identify the dispersive shift chi = g^2Delta: the cavity frequency shifts by pmchi depending on the qubit state (grangle vs erangle), enabling dispersive readout of superconducting qubits.","title":"Second order: dispersive shift"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Fourth-order:-Kerr-nonlinearity","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"At third order in g the transformed Hamiltonian acquires off-diagonal terms B equiv S_1 S_1 V:\n\nB = commutator(S1, commutator(S1, V))\n\n((-4(g^3)) / (Δ^2)) * a * σ₂₁ - ((4(g^3)) / (Δ^2)) * a' * σ₁₂ - ((4(g^3)) / (Δ^2)) * a' * a * a * σ₂₁ - ((4(g^3)) / (Δ^2)) * a' * a' * a * σ₁₂\n\nThese are cancelled by a second generator S_2 satisfying S_2 H_0 = -B3:\n\nS_2 = frac4g^33Delta^3bigl(\n  a^dagger granglelangle e\n  - a eranglelangle g\n  + a^dagger a^dagger a granglelangle e\n  - a^dagger a a eranglelangle g\nbigr)\n\nNote how S_2 contains terms with three ladder operators –- these generate photon-number-dependent corrections.\n\nS2 = ((4 // 3) * g^3 / Δ^3) * (a' * σge - a * σeg + a' * a' * a * σge - a' * a * a * σeg)\n\n(((-4//3)*(g^3)) / (Δ^3)) * a * σ₂₁ + (((4//3)*(g^3)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * σ₁₂ - (((4//3)*(g^3)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * a * σ₂₁ + (((4//3)*(g^3)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * σ₁₂\n\nThe fourth-order diagonal correction involves two pieces:\n\ncomm_S2_V = commutator(S2, V)\n\n(((-8//3)*(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * σ₂₂ + (((8//3)*(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₁₁ - ((8(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₂₂ + (((8//3)*(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₁₁ - (((8//3)*(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₂₂\n\nC = commutator(S1, B)\n\n((-8(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * σ₂₂ + ((8(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₁₁ - ((24(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₂₂ + ((8(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₁₁ - ((8(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₂₂\n\nThese combine into the fourth-order effective Hamiltonian via H_mathrmeff^(4) = H_mathrmeff^(2) + tfrac12S_2 V - tfrac124S_1 B:\n\nH_eff_4 = H_eff + comm_S2_V // 2 - C // 24\n\n((g^2) / Δ + (-(g^4)) / (Δ^3) + Δ) * σ₂₂ + ((-(g^2)) / Δ + (g^4) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₁₁ + ((g^2) / Δ + (-3(g^4)) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a * σ₂₂ + ((g^4) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₁₁ - ((g^4) / (Δ^3)) * a' * a' * a * a * σ₂₂\n\nWhile the symbolic prefactors are not automatically simplified by the CAS, the operator structure is manifest.  Collecting like terms analytically, one obtains:\n\nH_mathrmeff = Bigl(Delta + fracg^2Delta - fracg^4Delta^3Bigr)\n  eranglelangle e\n  + chi_mathrmeff a^dagger abigl(eranglelangle e - granglelangle gbigr)\n  + K (a^dagger)^2 a^2bigl(granglelangle g - eranglelangle ebigr)\n  + textconst\n\nwith the effective dispersive shift chi_mathrmeff = g^2Delta - g^4Delta^3 and the Kerr nonlinearity K = g^4Delta^3. The Kerr term (a^dagger)^2 a^2 = hatn(hatn-1) introduces an anharmonic, photon-number-dependent level spacing and represents the leading nonlinearity in dispersive circuit QED.","title":"Fourth order: Kerr nonlinearity"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/schrieffer_wolff/#Numerical-verification","page":"Schrieffer–Wolff Transformation","text":"We verify the symbolic effective Hamiltonians against the exact Jaynes-Cummings eigenvalues using numeric_average and substitute. For each Fock state n grangle we compute the expectation value langle ng H_mathrmeff ngrangle.  Since the Schrieffer-Wolff transformation block-diagonalises the Hamiltonian, this directly gives the dressed-state energy at each order.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\ng_val, Δ_val = 0.3, 2.0\nn_max = 8\nns = 0:n_max\nsubs = Dict(g => g_val, Δ => Δ_val)\n\nb_cav = FockBasis(n_max)\nb_atom = NLevelBasis(2)\n\nH_eff_num = substitute(H_eff, subs)\nH_eff_4_num = substitute(H_eff_4, subs)\n\nE_sw2 = Float64[]\nE_sw4 = Float64[]\nfor n in ns\n    ψ = fockstate(b_cav, n) ⊗ nlevelstate(b_atom, 1)   # |n, g⟩\n    push!(E_sw2, real(numeric_average(H_eff_num, ψ)))\n    push!(E_sw4, real(numeric_average(H_eff_4_num, ψ)))\nend\n\n# Exact JC eigenvalue for the |n, g⟩-like dressed state (interaction picture):\n# E_g(n) = Δ/2 - √(Δ²/4 + g²n)  for n ≥ 1, and E_g(0) = 0.\nE_exact = [n == 0 ? 0.0 : Δ_val / 2 - sqrt(Δ_val^2 / 4 + g_val^2 * n) for n in ns]\n\nfig = plot(; xlabel = \"photon number n\", ylabel = \"energy error (rotating frame)\", title = \"Schrieffer-Wolff accuracy: g/Δ = $(g_val / Δ_val)\", margin = 5Plots.mm)\nplot!(fig, collect(ns), E_exact .- E_sw2; label = L\"\\mathrm{SW}_2\\ error\", marker = :circle, linestyle = :solid)\nplot!(fig, collect(ns), E_exact .- E_sw4; label = L\"\\mathrm{SW}_4\\ error\", marker = :diamond, linestyle = :solid)\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Operator-functions","page":"Operator functions","text":"Some Hamiltonians are not polynomials in the ladder operators. A trapped ion driven by a laser picks up the phase of the light at its position, and its motion enters through the displacement e^ieta(a + a^dagger) with the Lamb–Dicke parameter eta,\n\nH = nu a^dagger a + fracOmega2 left( sigma^21 e^ieta(a + a^dagger) + sigma^12 e^-ieta(a + a^dagger) right)\n\nFunctions of operators such as this exponential, or the cosine potential of a Josephson junction, are kept as formal functions. They take part in the algebra without being expanded, and a Taylor expansion to a chosen order turns them into polynomials when needed.\n\nPages = [\"operator_functions.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Operator functions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Formal-functions","page":"Operator functions","text":"The exponential e^iA of a Hermitian operator is written expim, which also writes unit phases of numbers. With it the trapped-ion Hamiltonian reads as on paper.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:motion) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:ion, 2)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\nσ(k, l) = Transition(h, :σ, k, l)\n@variables η Ω ν\n\nH = ν * a' * a + Ω / 2 * (σ(2, 1) * expim(η * (a + a')) + σ(1, 2) * expim(-η * (a + a')))\nν * a' * a + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₁₂ * exp(im*(-η * a - η * a')) + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₂₁ * exp(im*(η * a + η * a'))\n\nSine and cosine of operators are formal in the same way. There is no exp of an operator; e^iA is written expim(A).\n\nA formal function keeps its place in a product. It commutes with operators on other subsystems but not with operators it shares a subsystem with.\n\na * cos(a + a') == cos(a + a') * a, σ(2, 1) * cos(a + a') == cos(a + a') * σ(2, 1)\n(false, true)\n\nNo identities between functions are applied, not even sin^2 + cos^2 = 1 or e^iA e^-iA = 1. Where such an identity is needed, it is applied by hand.\n\nexpim(η * (a + a')) * expim(η * (a + a'))'\nexp(im*(η * a + η * a')) * exp(im*(-η * a - η * a'))\n\nAn expression that contains a formal function has its own type, which holds it as written rather than in canonical form.","title":"Formal functions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Exact-operations","page":"Operator functions","text":"Operations that are exact without an expansion pass through the functions to their arguments. These are the adjoint, substitute, change_index, normal_order, simplify, expand, expand_completeness, assume_distinct_index and the conjugate of a unitary transformation. The Hamiltonian above is Hermitian as a whole.\n\nH' == H\ntrue\n\nSubstitution reaches inside the functions, so the Lamb–Dicke parameter can be set to zero to recover the bare two-level drive.\n\nsubstitute(H, Dict(η => 0))\n(\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₁₂ + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₂₁ + ν * a' * a)\n\nA unitary transformation acts on the argument of the function, so the result is exact and needs no Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff series.\n\n@variables θ\nR = Rotation(a, θ)\nconjugate(cos(a + a'), R) == cos(conjugate(a + a', R))\ntrue\n\nA commutator with a formal function stays formal, since evaluating it would need the series.","title":"Exact operations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Taylor-expansion","page":"Operator functions","text":"For small eta the exponentials are expanded. To first order the drive acquires the sidebands a sigma^21 and a^dagger sigma^21, which couple the internal state to the motion.\n\ntaylor(H, 0:1)\n\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₁₂ + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω * σ₂₁ + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω*η)im * a * σ₁₂ + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω*η)im * a * σ₂₁ + ν * a' * a + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω*η)im * a' * σ₁₂ + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*Ω*η)im * a' * σ₂₁\n\nThe operator method of taylor takes an operator expression and an order range. It is distinct from the scalar method taylor(f, x, 0:n) of Symbolics, which expands in a variable x.","title":"Taylor expansion"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Numerical-conversion","page":"Operator functions","text":"A formal function has no numerical form of its own. to_numeric, numeric_average, expect and average throw an ArgumentError on it rather than pick an expansion order silently, so the expression is lowered first.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase\nbackend = QuantumOpticsBackend()\nparameter = Dict(η => 0.1, Ω => 1.0, ν => 1.0)\nHn = to_numeric(taylor(H, 0:2), h, (10, 2); backend, parameter)\nsize(Hn)\n(22, 22)\n\nMatrix functions evaluated directly on the truncated space are not available yet (#268). A symbolic Σ over a formal function and conversion between normal and symmetric order with normal_to_symmetric and symmetric_to_normal are not supported either; both need the polynomial from taylor. Perturbative frame changes such as Schrieffer–Wolff or van Vleck transformations are a separate problem of effective Hamiltonians and are not part of the formal functions.","title":"Numerical conversion"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/operator_functions/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expim-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}","page":"Operator functions","text":"expim(A)\n\nExponential of im times a Hermitian operator expression, kept as a formal function.\n\nThe result is the unitary e^iA. A must be Hermitian as written, meaning that A - A' simplifies to zero; parameters in it must therefore be real. Otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. No series is expanded until taylor is called, and products such as e^iA e^-iA are not combined. To change the frame of an expression by such a unitary, use a UnitaryTransform, which applies e^iA X e^-iA exactly.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> U = expim(θ * a' * a)\nexp(im*(θ * a' * a))\n\njulia> taylor(U, 0:1)\n1 + θim * a' * a\n\njulia> expim(a)\nERROR: ArgumentError: `expim(::QField)` requires a provably Hermitian argument\n[...]\n\nSee also taylor, UnitaryTransform, expim(::Real).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expim"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Base.sin-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}","page":"Operator functions","text":"sin(A)\n\nSine of an operator expression, kept as a formal function.\n\nNo series is expanded. The function enters sums and products like any other operator, and keeps its position relative to operators on the same subsystem. A may be any operator expression. Use taylor to turn the function into a polynomial.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> sin(a + a') * a\nsin(a + a') * a\n\njulia> taylor(sin(a + a'), 0:3)\n1//2 * a + 1//2 * a' - 1//6 * a * a * a - 1//2 * a' * a * a - 1//2 * a' * a' * a - 1//6 * a' * a' * a'\n\nSee also cos, expim, taylor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.sin"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Base.cos-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}","page":"Operator functions","text":"cos(A)\n\nCosine of an operator expression, kept as a formal function.\n\nNo series is expanded. The function enters sums and products like any other operator, and keeps its position relative to operators on the same subsystem; a commutator with it stays formal. A may be any operator expression. Use taylor to turn the function into a polynomial.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> commutator(cos(a + a'), a)\n-a * cos(a + a') + cos(a + a') * a\n\njulia> taylor(cos(a + a'), 0:2)\n1//2 - 1//2 * a * a - a' * a - 1//2 * a' * a'\n\nSee also sin, expim, taylor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.cos"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/operator_functions/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QExpr","page":"Operator functions","text":"QExpr <: QField\n\nType of operator expressions that contain formal functions such as sin, cos or expim.\n\nPolynomials in the operators are kept in canonical form, with products reordered and like terms collected. An expression containing a formal function cannot be brought into that form, so it is held as written: products keep the order of factors on a shared subsystem, factors on different subsystems are sorted, and products are not multiplied out over sums. Exact operations such as adjoint, substitute or simplify act on the arguments of the functions. taylor turns a QExpr back into an ordinary polynomial. The type is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: QExpr\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> x = (a + a') * cos(a' * a);\n\njulia> x isa QExpr, taylor(x, 0:0) isa QExpr\n(true, false)\n\njulia> x'\ncos(a' * a) * (a + a')\n\nSee also sin, cos, expim, taylor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QExpr"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/operator_functions/#Symbolics.taylor","page":"Operator functions","text":"taylor(expr, 0:n)\n\nReplace every formal operator function in expr by its Taylor polynomial up to order n.\n\nThe coefficients are exact and the result is a polynomial in canonical form; an expression without formal functions is returned unchanged. Each function is truncated on its own, so the order is not a total degree of the result: sin(A)^2 truncated at order 1 is A^2, and sin(A)^2 + cos(A)^2 truncated at any order is not 1. A function inside another function throws an ArgumentError; lower the inner part first. So does a function of an argument containing a Σ, since a power such as (sum_i A_i)^2 would need fresh summation indices. The range must start at 0.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> A = a + a';\n\njulia> taylor(sin(A)^2, 0:1)\n1 + a * a + 2 * a' * a + a' * a'\n\njulia> taylor(sin(A)^2, 0:1) == A^2\ntrue\n\nSee also sin, cos, expim.\n\n\n\n\n\ntaylor(X::SuperExpr, ns)\ntaylor(L::Lindbladian{QExpr}, ns)\n\nLower every formal side of a map to its Maclaurin polynomial through order n, for ns = 0:n.\n\nA SuperExpr becomes a SuperOperator and a Lindbladian{QExpr} a polynomial Lindbladian. Each formal function is expanded as for an operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Symbolics.taylor"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Comparison-with-other-packages","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"Several packages, across several languages, do symbolic second-quantized operator algebra. This page benchmarks SecondQuantizedAlgebra (SQA) head-to-head against four of them on a compact suite of practical tasks: building Hamiltonians, deriving Heisenberg equations of motion, nested commutators, operator powers, normal ordering, multi-mode chains, symbolic indexed sums, and mean-field expectation values.\n\nPackage Language Canonicalization Bosons Two-level / spin Symbolic coefficients Indexed sums (symbolic N) Symbolic ⟨·⟩\nSecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl Julia eager ✓ ✓ (N-level, Pauli, spin-S) ✓ ✓ ✓\nQuantumAlgebra.jl Julia lazy (normal_form) ✓ ✓ (spin-½) ✓ ✓ ✓\nSymPy (sympy.physics.quantum) Python lazy (normal_ordered_form) ✓ ✓ (Pauli) ✓ ✗ ✗\nOpenFermion (BosonOperator) Python lazy (normal_ordered) ✓ ✗ (separate qubit type) ✗ (numeric only) ✗ ✗\nsneg Mathematica eager (nc) ✓ ✓ (fermionic mode) ✓ ✗ ✗\n\nThe suite definition, canonical benchmark keys, and the runner scripts live in the repository under benchmark/comparison/; see Reproducing below.","title":"Comparison with other packages"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Methodology","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"note: Fairness contract\nThe packages have different evaluation strategies, so a naive comparison is misleading. Every row times each package producing the same canonical (normal-ordered) result from the same physical input, written idiomatically in that package.Eager packages (SQA, sneg) canonicalize on every multiplication, so the operation itself is timed.\nLazy packages (QuantumAlgebra, SymPy, OpenFermion) keep products symbolic until a normalization call, so the arithmetic is timed together with the normalization that reaches the canonical form the eager packages hand you for free.\nProducts of three or more factors use the eager workflow on the lazy side too: normalize after each multiply rather than expanding the whole product first. Timing a lazy package on the fully expanded product would penalize a workflow choice, not an algorithm (see the ergonomics note below).Before timing, every script checks textbook identities in its own package (a a = 1 + aa, a a a = aa a + 2a, two-level completeness σσ + σσ = 1). A failing check aborts that package's run. Any benchmark whose single trial evaluation exceeds 3 s is reported as capped instead of timed.\n\nFurther caveats, each disclosed rather than silently absorbed:\n\nCross-language ratios are order-of-magnitude indicators. Every harness gives each scenario the same wall-clock budget (up to ~10 s) in a single process and reports the minimum per-call time (Julia via BenchmarkTools, Python via pyperf, Mathematica via a calibrated timing loop); fast scenarios stop early once ~50 samples are collected, and only scenarios slower than a few hundred ms per call use the full budget. Harness overhead and warm-up behavior still differ across runtimes, so read 90× as \"roughly two orders of magnitude\", not as a precise figure. The Julia-vs-Julia column is precise.\nOpenFermion uses numeric coefficients (ω = 1.0, J = 0.5, U = 0.25) because BosonOperator does not accept symbols. That is less work than the symbolic-coefficient arithmetic every other package performs, so OpenFermion's numbers are, if anything, flattered. It also cannot mix bosons with qubit operators in one expression, so the Jaynes–Cummings rows are not expressible.\nSymPy needs a small disclosed helper. normal_ordered_form does not know that boson and Pauli operators act on different Hilbert spaces (and hence commute), so mixed products fail to merge out of the box. The benchmark script includes a ~15-line helper that sorts boson factors before Pauli factors in each product; its cost is included in SymPy's timed path.\nsneg encodes the two-level atom as a single fermionic mode. For one site the fermion algebra (f f + ff = 1, f² = 0) is isomorphic to the σ algebra and bosons commute with fermions, so all Jaynes–Cummings results are identical to a spin encoding; this is also sneg's idiomatic usage in quantum impurity physics.\nDifferent canonical bases. QuantumAlgebra expands σσ into σˣσʸσᶻ form and SymPy works in its Pauli basis, whereas SQA keeps the transition form. Results are physically equivalent but not term-by-term identical.","title":"Methodology"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Results","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"Single Linux workstation, 2026-07-21: SQA 0.10.0 and QuantumAlgebra 1.6.0 on Julia 1.12.6, SymPy 1.14.0 and OpenFermion 1.8.1 on Python 3.14.6, sneg 2.0.21 on Mathematica 14.2. The SQA column is absolute time; every other cell shows that package's time with its ratio to SQA in parentheses, so (9.1×) means 9.1× slower than SQA. n/a means the scenario is not expressible in that package.\n\nBenchmark SQA QuantumAlgebra.jl SymPy OpenFermion sneg\nJaynes–Cummings: build H 1.500 μs 19.040 μs (13×) 73.943 μs (49×) n/a 186.711 μs (120×)\nJaynes–Cummings: H² 5.140 μs 220.801 μs (43×) 463.839 μs (90×) n/a 5.251 ms (1000×)\nHeisenberg equation: [H, a] 1.710 μs 20.900 μs (12×) 266.917 μs (160×) n/a 1.605 ms (940×)\nNested [H, [H, … σ⁻]] depth 2 7.700 μs 126.820 μs (16×) 781.343 μs (100×) n/a 8.472 ms (1100×)\nNested [H, [H, … σ⁻]] depth 4 61.240 μs 1.166 ms (19×) 5.402 ms (88×) n/a 102.622 ms (1700×)\nNested [H, [H, … σ⁻]] depth 6 232.321 μs 5.690 ms (24×) 382.454 ms (1600×) n/a 672.748 ms (2900×)\nNormal-order (a·a†)⁴ 5.020 μs 29.221 μs (5.8×) 1.044 ms (210×) 188.194 μs (37×) 6.054 ms (1200×)\nNormal-order (a·a†)⁸ 40.350 μs 163.951 μs (4.1×) 9.967 ms (250×) 1.174 ms (29×) 75.820 ms (1900×)\nBose–Hubbard (M = 8): build H 30.240 μs 78.550 μs (2.6×) 712.174 μs (24×) 145.807 μs (4.8×) 2.990 ms (99×)\nBose–Hubbard (M = 8): H² 436.901 μs 1.428 ms (3.3×) 3.229 s (7400×) 3.609 ms (8.3×) 2.472 s (5700×)\nTavis–Cummings Σᵢ: build H 1.400 μs 11.070 μs (7.9×) n/a n/a n/a\nTavis–Cummings: [H, σ⁺ⱼσ⁻ⱼ] 30.000 μs 354.721 μs (12×) n/a n/a n/a\nMean-field ⟨H⟩ 39.241 μs 1.360 μs (0.035×) n/a n/a n/a\n\n(Image: Scaling comparison)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Reading-the-results","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"SQA leads every operator-algebra row, against every package. The closest competitor throughout is QuantumAlgebra, which shares SQA's Julia substrate and a specialized bosonic normal-ordering engine. The general-purpose CAS approaches (SymPy, Mathematica-based sneg) sit well behind on the same physics.\nThe lead widens exactly where real derivations spend their time. Nested commutators (the core of Heisenberg / Schrieffer–Wolff / cumulant derivations) are where the gap grows fastest with depth, as the top-left panel shows. Eager canonicalization keeps every intermediate compact, so the cost compounds more slowly.\nOperator powers separate width from depth. The top-right panel sweeps Hⁿ (an eager fold, same contract as the table): a single wide product-and-collect rather than a deep recursion, and the place where general-CAS normal ordering degrades fastest while SQA and QuantumAlgebra stay near log-linear. It is the product-expansion counterpart of the nested-commutator panel and stresses like-term collection instead of cancellation.\nChain length probes system size, not expression size. The bottom-right panel builds the Bose–Hubbard Hamiltonian on M = 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 modes; unlike the other three sweeps it grows the number of Hilbert-space factors rather than the depth or width of a single expression. The Hamiltonian has O(M) terms, so every package scales near-linearly and the panel becomes a constant-factor race where the field bunches closest together. Building a Hamiltonian is the one axis with the least canonicalization work per term.\nOpenFermion is the strongest non-Julia contender on pure bosonic work, while computing with plain numeric coefficients, a strictly easier task than the symbolic arithmetic every other column performs.\nThe one row SQA loses is measuring different objects. QuantumAlgebra's expval tags operators inside its own term type (a near-trivial field move), whereas SQA's average materializes a Symbolics.jl Num expression (splitting coefficients into real and imaginary parts and building a CAS term tree). That object is what feeds substitute, simplify, numeric conversion, and ModelingToolkit downstream, so the extra cost buys the bridge into the Julia symbolics ecosystem.\nSymbolic indexed sums are a two-package race. Only SQA and QuantumAlgebra can express Σᵢ gᵢ(aσᵢ + aσᵢ) with a symbolic atom number N, and the H σⱼσⱼ diagonal split is the operation that turns such sums into mesoscopic equations of motion.","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Ergonomics:-eager-by-default-vs-the-lazy-product-footgun","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"The contract above uses the eager workflow for lazy packages. Their default lazy workflow has a sharp footgun on powers, building the full product before canonicalizing: with QuantumAlgebra, normal_form(H⁴) for the Jaynes–Cummings H costs about 86 ms and 2.2 M allocations lazily versus 2.3 ms and 84 k allocations eagerly, a roughly 37× time difference from one workflow choice. SymPy's Bose–Hubbard H² row above is the same effect in a different package. SQA canonicalizes on every *, so this cliff does not exist for its users.","title":"Ergonomics: eager by default vs the lazy-product footgun"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Reproducing","page":"Comparison with other packages","text":"Each language has a runner script in benchmark/comparison/ that validates the known-answer identities, runs its scenarios, and writes one results/<package>.json per package (the Julia script covers both SQA and QuantumAlgebra in one run); make_table.jl merges whatever JSONs exist into the table and figure above. The scenario definitions and canonical keys live in benchmark/comparison/BENCHMARKS.md.\n\n# Julia (SQA + QuantumAlgebra)\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/comparison/julia_bench.jl\n\n# Python (each needs: pip install sympy openfermion pyperf)\npython benchmark/comparison/sympy_bench.py\npython benchmark/comparison/openfermion_bench.py\n\n# Mathematica (needs sneg installed)\nwolframscript -f benchmark/comparison/sneg_bench.wls\n\n# Merge into the docs table + figure\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/comparison/make_table.jl","title":"Reproducing"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"We use units with hbar = 1. Consider a cavity mode on resonance with a two-level atom, described by the Jaynes–Cummings Hamiltonian\n\nH = omega a^dagger a + omega sigma^22 + g left( a^dagger sigma^12 + a sigma^21 right)\n\nThe cavity lives on a FockSpace and the atom on an NLevelSpace; their tensor product ⊗ is the space of the full system. Each space in the tensor product represents one subsystem. In the constructors below, the final argument selects that subsystem: 1 for the cavity and 2 for the atom. Thus the annihilation operator a acts on the cavity, while the transitions sigma^ij = iranglelangle j act on the atom. The couplings are ordinary Symbolics variables.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2)\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\n@variables ω g\n\nH = ω * a' * a + ω * σ(2, 2) + g * (a' * σ(1, 2) + a * σ(2, 1))\nω * σ₂₂ + g * a * σ₂₁ + ω * a' * a + g * a' * σ₁₂\n\nProducts apply the local algebraic identities as they are formed: ladder operators come out normal ordered, and transitions on the same atom compose.\n\na * a', σ(1, 2) * σ(2, 1), σ(2, 1) * σ(2, 1)\n(1 + a' * a, σ₁₁, 0)\n\nHere sigma^11 = 1ranglelangle 1 projects onto the atomic ground state, and sigma^22 = 2ranglelangle 2 onto the excited state. Since these are the only two states, their projectors satisfy sigma^11 + sigma^22 = 1. The algebra keeps sigma^11 as a single operator; call expand_completeness to replace it using this identity.\n\nexpand_completeness(σ(1, 1))\n1 - σ₂₂\n\nBecause every product is already in canonical form, a commutator reduces to a subtraction, and the Heisenberg equation dota = iH a is one line.\n\n1im * commutator(H, a)\n(-ω)im * a + (-g)im * σ₁₂\n\nTaking expectation values with average turns the operator equation for the atomic coherence into an equation for c-numbers, the starting point of any mean-field or cumulant treatment.\n\naverage(1im * commutator(H, σ(1, 2)))\n⟨\u001b[34ma * σ₂₂\u001b[39m⟩*g*im - ⟨\u001b[34ma * σ₁₁\u001b[39m⟩*g*im - ⟨\u001b[34mσ₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*im*ω\n\nTo compute with the model, to_numeric builds its matrix through QuantumOptics on a truncated basis, here with cavity Fock states from zero to ten photons.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase\nusing LinearAlgebra: eigvals, Hermitian\n\nbackend = QuantumOpticsBackend()\nparameter = Dict(ω => 1.0, g => 0.1)\nHn = to_numeric(H, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter)\neigvals(Hermitian(Matrix(dense(Hn).data)))[1:5]\n5-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.0\n 0.8999999999999988\n 1.0999999999999983\n 1.858578643762691\n 2.14142135623731\n\nAbove the ground state the spectrum splits into doublets at omega pm g and 2omega pm sqrt2 g, the vacuum Rabi splitting.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Unitary-transformations","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"A change of frame replaces every operator X by U^dagger X U for a unitary U. Rotating frames remove fast oscillations, displacements move a driven mode to its steady state, and squeezing or Bogoliubov transformations diagonalize quadratic Hamiltonians. For the transformations that keep the operator algebra linear, the image of every operator is known in closed form, so the new Hamiltonian follows exactly, without a Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff series. Consider a Kerr oscillator driven at frequency omega_d,\n\nH = omega a^dagger a + K a^dagger 2 a^2 + eta e^-iomega_d t a^dagger + eta^* e^iomega_d t a\n\nPages = [\"unitary_transformations.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Unitary transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Rotating-frames","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"The drive oscillates at omega_d. In the frame rotating at the drive frequency, Rotation(a, ω_d * t, t), the drive becomes static and the mode frequency is replaced by the detuning omega - omega_d.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\na = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a)\n@variables ω ω_d K t η::Number\n\nH = ω * a' * a + K * a' * a' * a * a + η * expim(-ω_d * t) * a' + conj(η) * expim(ω_d * t) * a\nH_rot = transform(H, Rotation(a, ω_d * t, t))\nconj(η) * a + η * a' + (ω - ω_d) * a' * a + K * a' * a' * a * a\n\nAn observable is transformed with conjugate, which gives U^dagger X U. A Hamiltonian in a frame that moves in time also picks up the term i(partial_t U^dagger)U, so it is transformed with transform. Here that term subtracts omega_d a^dagger a.\n\ngauge_term(Rotation(a, ω_d * t, t))\n-ω_d * a' * a\n\nRotatingFrame builds the same frame from the Hamiltonian it rotates with, which is convenient when that Hamiltonian involves several subsystems.\n\ntransform(H, RotatingFrame(ω_d * a' * a, t)) == H_rot\ntrue\n\nRotation is one of several named constructors, which together cover the standard frames of quantum optics:\n\nSystem Constructor Action\nMode Displace(a, α) a mapsto a + alpha\nMode Rotation(a, θ) a mapsto e^-itheta a\nMode Squeeze(a, r, ϕ) a mapsto cosh r a + e^iphi sinh r a^dagger\nTwo modes Rotation(a, b, θ) beam splitter\nTwo modes Squeeze(a, b, r) two-mode squeezing\nSeveral modes Bogoliubov(modes, S) general linear mixing of a and a^dagger\nQuadratures Displace(x, p, dx, dp) x mapsto x + dx, p mapsto p + dp\nQuadratures Rotation(x, p, θ) rotation in phase space\nQuadratures Squeeze(x, p, r) x mapsto e^r x, p mapsto e^-r p\nSpin or Pauli Rotation(S, axis, θ) rotation about an axis\nFinite-level system Rotation(σ, W) change of basis by a unitary matrix W\nAny RotatingFrame(H0, t) interaction picture of H_0\nDriven mode DisplacementFrame(a, H) displacement to the steady state\n\nDisplace, Rotation, Squeeze and DisplacementFrame take a final time variable t when their parameters depend on time. The time variable is a real symbolic variable, and without it no time-derivative term is produced, so transform then coincides with conjugate.","title":"Rotating frames"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Displacements","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"A strong drive pushes the mode to a large coherent amplitude alpha, and the fluctuations around it are described in a displaced frame. Written with the detuning Delta = omega - omega_d, the displaced Hamiltonian shows how the Kerr term acts on the fluctuations: a frequency shift 4Kalpha^2, squeezing terms Kalpha^2 a^dagger 2, and a linear term that vanishes when alpha solves the classical steady-state equation Deltaalpha + 2Kalpha^2alpha + eta = 0.\n\n@variables Δ α::Number\nH_det = substitute(H_rot, Dict(ω => ω_d + Δ))\ntransform(H_det, Displace(a, α))\nconj(η)*α + conj(α)*η + conj(α)*Δ*α + K*(conj(α)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*(α^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m) + (conj(η) + conj(α)*Δ + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mK*(conj(α)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*α) * a + (η + Δ*α + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mK*conj(α)*(α^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)) * a' + K*(conj(α)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m) * a * a + (Δ + \u001b[34m4\u001b[39mK*conj(α)*α) * a' * a + K*(α^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m) * a' * a' + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mK*conj(α) * a' * a * a + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mK*α * a' * a' * a + K * a' * a' * a * a\n\nDisplacementFrame finds the displacement from the linear part of a Hamiltonian, here alpha = -etaDelta. It removes the drive exactly when K = 0, and leaves the Kerr correction 2Kalpha^2alpha as a residual drive.\n\nD = DisplacementFrame(a, Δ * a' * a + η * a' + conj(η) * a)\nconjugate(a, D)\n(-η) / Δ + a","title":"Displacements"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Squeezing-and-Bogoliubov-transformations","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Around the steady state, the quadratic part of the displaced Hamiltonian has the form delta a^dagger a + frackappa2(a^dagger 2 + a^2), with delta = Delta + 4Kalpha^2 and kappa = 2Kalpha^2 for real alpha. A transformation that mixes annihilation and creation operators diagonalizes it. Under Squeeze, the coefficient of a^2 becomes frac12(kappacosh 2r + deltasinh 2r), which vanishes for tanh 2r = -kappadelta.\n\n@variables δ κ r\nconjugate(δ * a' * a + κ / 2 * (a' * a' + a * a), Squeeze(a, r))\ncosh(r)*sinh(r)*κ + (sinh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*δ + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*(cosh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*κ + cosh(r)*sinh(r)*δ + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*(sinh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*κ) * a * a + ((cosh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*δ + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mcosh(r)*sinh(r)*κ + (sinh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*δ) * a' * a + (\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*(cosh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*κ + cosh(r)*sinh(r)*δ + \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*(sinh(r)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*κ) * a' * a'\n\nBogoliubov does the same for any number of modes with a given matrix.","title":"Squeezing and Bogoliubov transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Composition-and-inversion","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Transformations compose with *, the left one applied first, and are inverted with inv, which needs no inverse rules from the caller. The time-derivative terms compose in the same order as the operator maps, so a product of frames gives the same Hamiltonian as the two frame changes applied one after the other. The rotating and displaced frames combine into one.\n\nU = Rotation(a, ω_d * t, t) * D\ntransform(substitute(H, Dict(ω => ω_d + Δ)), U) == transform(H_det, D)\ntrue\n\nApplying a transformation and then its inverse gives back the original expression.\n\nX = a' * a + K * a' * a' * a * a\nconjugate(conjugate(X, D), inv(D)) == X\ntrue\n\nParameters of a transformation are set with substitute, so a symbolic frame is built once and evaluated later. Time-dependent transformations in one product must share the same time variable.\n\nconjugate(a, substitute(Squeeze(a, r), Dict(r => 0))) == a\ntrue\n\ngenerators lists the operators a transformation acts on.\n\ngenerators(U)\n2-element Vector{Op}:\n a\n a'","title":"Composition and inversion"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Spins-and-finite-level-systems","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"A spin or a set of Pauli matrices is rotated about one of its axes. A rotation about the y axis mixes S_z with S_x.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nSz = Spin(SpinSpace(:S), :S, 3)\n@variables θ\nconjugate(Sz, Rotation(Sz, 2, θ))\n-sin(θ) * Sx + cos(θ) * Sz\n\nThe levels of a finite-level system change to a new basis by a unitary matrix. A real rotation by theta mixes the two levels of an atom.\n\nh = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\nσ = Transition(h, :σ, :g, :e)\n\nW = [cos(θ) -sin(θ); sin(θ) cos(θ)]\nconjugate(σ, Rotation(σ, W))\ncos(θ)*sin(θ) * σ₁₁ + cos(θ)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m * σ₁₂ - sin(θ)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m * σ₂₁ - cos(θ)*sin(θ) * σ₂₂\n\nThe matrix must be unitary, which is not checked. With a time-dependent matrix, Rotation(σ, W, t) carries the gauge term i dot W^dagger W.","title":"Spins and finite-level systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#General-generators","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"A transformation can also be given by its Hermitian generator G as U = e^-itheta G. The image of each operator is found by closing the commutators with G and exponentiating the resulting matrix exactly. The tunnelling term a^dagger b + b^dagger a between two modes generates a beam splitter with a phase.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:a) ⊗ FockSpace(:b)\na = Destroy(h, :a, 1)\nb = Destroy(h, :b, 2)\n@variables θ\n\nconjugate(a, UnitaryTransform(a' * b + b' * a, θ))\ncos(θ) * a + (-sin(θ))im * b\n\nThe exponential is exact only for numeric rates, so a generator such as g(a^dagger b + b^dagger a) with a symbolic g throws an ArgumentError; it is written as UnitaryTransform(a' * b + b' * a, g * θ), or with a named constructor.\n\nFormal operator functions are transformed through their arguments, so the result stays exact.\n\nconjugate(cos(a + a'), Rotation(a, θ))\ncos(exp(-im*θ) * a + exp(im*θ) * a')","title":"General generators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#Reading-displacement-amplitudes","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"displacement(U, a) returns the c-number part of conjugate(a, U), i.e. the displacement amplitude of a Fock or phase-space frame.","title":"Reading displacement amplitudes"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.conjugate","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"conjugate(A, U::UnitaryTransform)\nconjugate(L, U::UnitaryTransform)\n\nImage of an operator expression under a unitary transformation.\n\nThe result is U^dagger A U, with every operator replaced by its exact image; no series is truncated. Formal operator functions are transformed through their arguments. For a Lindbladian or a superoperator L, every operator it contains is conjugated, so a channel mathcalDJ becomes mathcalDU^dagger J U. For a Hamiltonian in a time-dependent frame use transform, which adds the term from the motion of the frame.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables α::Number;\n\njulia> conjugate(a' * a, Displace(a, α))\nconj(α)*α + conj(α) * a + α * a' + a' * a\n\nSee also transform, UnitaryTransform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.conjugate"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transform","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"transform(H, U::UnitaryTransform)\ntransform(L, U::UnitaryTransform)\n\nHamiltonian in the frame of a unitary transformation.\n\nThe result is U^dagger H U + i (partial_t U^dagger) U. The second term, returned by gauge_term, comes from the motion of a time-dependent frame. For a static transformation it vanishes and transform agrees with conjugate. For a Lindbladian L, the Hamiltonian is transformed and every jump is conjugated.\n\nQuantumOpticsBase also exports a transform. With both packages loaded, import the name explicitly, using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: transform, or qualify it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω g t;\n\njulia> transform(ω * a' * a + g * (a + a'), Rotation(a, ω * t, t))\nexp(-im*t*ω)*g * a + exp(im*t*ω)*g * a'\n\nSee also conjugate, gauge_term, RotatingFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transform"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.gauge_term","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"gauge_term(U::UnitaryTransform)\n\nTerm that the motion of a time-dependent frame adds to a Hamiltonian.\n\nIt is i (partial_t U^dagger) U, zero for a static transformation. transform adds it automatically.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω t;\n\njulia> gauge_term(Rotation(a, ω * t, t))\n-ω * a' * a\n\nSee also transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.gauge_term"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.RotatingFrame","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"RotatingFrame(H0, t)\n\nFrame rotating with a time-independent Hamiltonian H0, the interaction picture.\n\nThe transformation is U = e^-i H_0 t. With transform, a Hamiltonian H_0 + V becomes U^dagger V U, since the motion of the frame cancels H0. H0 must be Hermitian and of a form that UnitaryTransform can exponentiate.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω g t;\n\njulia> transform(ω * a' * a + g * (a + a'), RotatingFrame(ω * a' * a, t))\nexp(-im*t*ω)*g * a + exp(im*t*ω)*g * a'\n\nSee also transform, Rotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.RotatingFrame"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Rotation","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Rotation(a, θ)\nRotation(a, b, θ)\nRotation(x, p, θ)\nRotation(S, axis::Int, θ)\nRotation(σ, W::AbstractMatrix)\nRotation(a, θ, t)\nRotation(a, b, θ, t)\nRotation(x, p, θ, t)\nRotation(S, axis::Int, θ, t)\nRotation(σ, W::AbstractMatrix, t)\n\nRotation of a mode, of a pair of modes or quadratures, of a spin, or of the levels of a finite-level system.\n\nRotation(a, θ) turns the phase of a mode, a mapsto e^-itheta a.\nRotation(a, b, θ) mixes two modes as a beam splitter, a mapsto costheta a + sintheta b and b mapsto -sintheta a + costheta b.\nRotation(x, p, θ) rotates a pair of quadratures in phase space in the same way.\nRotation(S, axis, θ) rotates a spin or Pauli triple by θ about axis (1, 2 or 3); S is any component of the triple.\nRotation(σ, W) changes the basis of a finite-level system, sigma^ij mapsto W^dagger sigma^ij W, acting on all transitions of the system; σ is any one of them. W is a square unitary matrix with one row per level; its size is checked, its unitarity is not.\n\nWith a final time variable t, the angle or matrix may depend on time and the transformation carries the gauge_term of the moving frame.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> conjugate(a, Rotation(a, θ))\nexp(-im*θ) * a\n\njulia> Sx = Spin(SpinSpace(:S), :S, 1);\n\njulia> conjugate(Sx, Rotation(Sx, 3, θ))\ncos(θ) * Sx - sin(θ) * Sy\n\nSee also RotatingFrame, Squeeze, Displace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Rotation"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.DisplacementFrame","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"DisplacementFrame(a, H)\nDisplacementFrame(x, p, H)\nDisplacementFrame(a, H, t)\nDisplacementFrame(x, p, H, t)\nDisplacementFrame(a, L)\nDisplacementFrame(a, L, t)\n\nDisplacement to the classical steady state of a driven mode.\n\nH is a Hamiltonian of one mode that is at most linear in the drive, such as Delta a^dagger a + eta a^dagger + eta^* a. The transformation displaces the mode by its steady-state amplitude, here -etaDelta, which removes the drive. The quadrature form accepts any Hermitian quadratic H. With a time variable t the drive may be a finite sum of phases expim(ω t), and the frame follows the steady driven response, without the transient free oscillation. A drive on resonance throws an ArgumentError, and symbolic denominators are kept as written.\n\nWith a damped linear Lindbladian L in place of H, the amplitude is the fixed point of the drift transpose(L)(a) = λ a + c, so loss and heating enter it as well.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables Δ η::Number;\n\njulia> H = Δ * a' * a + η * a' + conj(η) * a;\n\njulia> conjugate(H, DisplacementFrame(a, H))\n(-conj(η)*η) / Δ + Δ * a' * a\n\nSee also Displace, transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.DisplacementFrame"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Displace","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Displace(a, α)\nDisplace(x, p, dx, dp)\nDisplace(a, α, t)\nDisplace(x, p, dx, dp, t)\n\nDisplacement of a bosonic mode or of a pair of quadratures.\n\nThe mode a is shifted by the complex amplitude α, a mapsto a + alpha. A pair of quadratures is shifted by real amounts, x mapsto x + dx and p mapsto p + dp. With a final time variable t, the amplitudes may depend on time and the transformation carries the gauge_term of the moving frame.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables α::Number;\n\njulia> conjugate(a' * a, Displace(a, α))\nconj(α)*α + conj(α) * a + α * a' + a' * a\n\njulia> h = PhaseSpace(:mode); x = Position(h, :x); p = Momentum(h, :p);\n\njulia> @variables dx dp;\n\njulia> conjugate(x * p, Displace(x, p, dx, dp))\ndp*dx + dp * x + dx * p + x * p\n\nSee also DisplacementFrame, Rotation, Squeeze.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Displace"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Squeeze","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Squeeze(a, r, ϕ = 0)\nSqueeze(a, b, r)\nSqueeze(x, p, r)\nSqueeze(a, r, ϕ, t)\nSqueeze(a, b, r, t)\nSqueeze(x, p, r, t)\n\nSqueezing of a mode, of a pair of modes, or of a pair of quadratures.\n\nSqueeze(a, r, ϕ) squeezes one mode, a mapsto cosh r a + e^iphi sinh r a^dagger.\nSqueeze(a, b, r) squeezes two modes jointly, a mapsto cosh r a + sinh r b^dagger and the same with a and b exchanged.\nSqueeze(x, p, r) rescales a pair of quadratures, x mapsto e^r x and p mapsto e^-r p.\n\nWith a final time variable t, the parameters may depend on time and the transformation carries the gauge_term of the moving frame. A time-dependent single-mode squeeze always gives the phase, Squeeze(a, r, 0, t), since the third argument is ϕ.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables r;\n\njulia> conjugate(a, Squeeze(a, r))\ncosh(r) * a + sinh(r) * a'\n\njulia> conjugate(a, inv(Squeeze(a, r)))\ncosh(r) * a - sinh(r) * a'\n\nSee also Bogoliubov, Rotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Squeeze"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Bogoliubov","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"Bogoliubov(modes, S::AbstractMatrix)\nBogoliubov(modes, U::AbstractMatrix, V::AbstractMatrix)\n\nGeneral linear transformation of bosonic modes that mixes annihilation and creation operators.\n\nmodes is one annihilation operator, or a vector or tuple of them. The block form maps a mapsto U a + V a^dagger. The full form acts on (a_1 ldots a_N a_1^dagger ldots a_N^dagger) with the matrix S, which must have the block structure [U V; conj(V) conj(U)]. For the result to be unitary the blocks must preserve the commutation relations, S J S^dagger = J with J = mathrmdiag(1_N -1_N). This is the caller's responsibility, also after substitute: the modes and the matrix dimensions are checked, but the condition is not, and a numeric matrix is used as given, without tolerance or projection. For squeezing and beam splitters, Squeeze and Rotation satisfy it by construction.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables u::Number v::Number;\n\njulia> B = Bogoliubov(a, [u v; conj(v) conj(u)]);\n\njulia> conjugate(a, B)\nu * a + v * a'\n\njulia> conjugate(a, substitute(B, Dict(u => 5//3, v => 4//3)))\n5//3 * a + 4//3 * a'\n\nSee also Squeeze, Rotation, UnitaryTransform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Bogoliubov"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.generators","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"generators(U::UnitaryTransform)\n\nOperators that the transformation U acts on, in canonical order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:a) ⊗ FockSpace(:b);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) b::Destroy(h, 2);\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> generators(Rotation(a, b, θ))\n4-element Vector{Op}:\n a\n a'\n b\n b'\n\nSee also UnitaryTransform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.generators"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.UnitaryTransform","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"UnitaryTransform(G, θ)\ninv(U)\nU1 * U2\n\nExact change of frame generated by a Hermitian operator.\n\nUnitaryTransform(G, θ) is the transformation U = e^-itheta G. The image of each operator follows in closed form when the commutators with G close on a finite set of operators and their exponential is known exactly, as for displacements, phase rotations, beam splitters and squeezers with numeric rates; otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. The named constructors Displace, Rotation, Squeeze, Bogoliubov, RotatingFrame and DisplacementFrame cover the common frames, also with symbolic parameters.\n\ninv(U) is the inverse transformation, and in a product U1 * U2 the left factor is applied first. Parameters of a transformation are replaced with substitute.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> conjugate(a, UnitaryTransform(a' * a, θ))\nexp(-im*θ) * a\n\njulia> conjugate(a, Displace(a, 1//2) * Rotation(a, θ))\n1//2 + exp(-im*θ) * a\n\nSee also conjugate, transform, generators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.UnitaryTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/unitary_transformations/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.displacement","page":"Unitary transformations","text":"displacement(U::UnitaryTransform, o::Op)\n\nReturn the c-number part of conjugate(o, U) for a Fock or phase-space operator o: α for conjugate(a, U) = L(a) + α with L linear. For a moving frame the result is the time-dependent amplitude; a site U does not act on gives zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.displacement"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Dicke-Model-and-the-Superradiant-Transition","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"The Dicke model describes N two-level atoms collectively coupled to a single cavity mode, retaining the counter-rotating terms that the Tavis-Cummings rotating-wave approximation drops.  In the thermodynamic limit N to infty it exhibits a quantum phase transition from a normal phase with langle arangle = 0 to a superradiant phase with a macroscopically occupied cavity at the critical coupling lambda_c = sqrtomega_0omega_a2.\n\nIn the collective-spin form,\n\nH = omega_0 a^dagger a + omega_a S_z\n  + frac2lambdasqrtN(a + a^dagger)S_x\nqquad N = 2S\n\nthe 2sqrtN normalisation keeps lambda_c finite as Ntoinfty. This example walks through every step that turns the operator Hamiltonian into the textbook result: built-in SU(2) algebra, Heisenberg equations of motion, Holstein-Primakoff bosonisation in the normal phase, the resulting polariton spectrum from a symbolic Bogoliubov-de-Gennes determinant and again from a beamsplitter frame change, the critical coupling from gap closing, and a numerical comparison at finite S.","title":"Dicke Model and the Superradiant Transition"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Setup","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"We bundle the rescaled coupling g equiv 2lambdasqrtN into a single symbolic variable so the algebra stays uncluttered:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nhs = SpinSpace(:S)\nh = hc ⊗ hs\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\n\nSx = Spin(h, :S, 1)\nSy = Spin(h, :S, 2)\nSz = Spin(h, :S, 3)\n\n@variables ω₀ ωₐ g\n\nH = ω₀ * a' * a + ωₐ * Sz + g * (a + a') * Sx\n\nωₐ * Sz + g * a * Sx + ω₀ * a' * a + g * a' * Sx","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Built-in-angular-momentum-algebra","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"Every product is eagerly canonicalised, so the SU(2) commutators fall out directly from *:\n\ncommutator(Sx, Sy)\n\nim * Sz\n\ncommutator(Sy, Sz)\n\nim * Sx\n\ncommutator(Sz, Sx)\n\nim * Sy","title":"Built-in angular-momentum algebra"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Heisenberg-equations-of-motion","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"The dynamics follow from dotO = -iO H.  No manual product rule or commutator table is needed:\n\n-1im * commutator(a, H)\n\n(-ω₀)im * a + (-g)im * Sx\n\n-1im * commutator(Sx, H)\n\n-ωₐ * Sy\n\n-1im * commutator(Sy, H)\n\nωₐ * Sx - g * a * Sz - g * a' * Sz\n\n-1im * commutator(Sz, H)\n\ng * a * Sy + g * a' * Sy\n\nReading these off:\n\nbeginaligned\ndot a   = -iomega_0 a - i g S_x \ndot S_x = -omega_a S_y \ndot S_y = omega_a S_x - g(a + a^dagger) S_z \ndot S_z = g(a + a^dagger) S_y\nendaligned","title":"Heisenberg equations of motion"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Holstein-Primakoff-bosonisation-in-the-normal-phase","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"Around the normal-phase fixed point (all atoms in their ground state with langle S_zrangle = -S, and an empty cavity) we expand the spin in a new bosonic mode b via the Holstein-Primakoff transformation,\n\nS_+ approx sqrt2 S b quad\nS_- approx sqrt2 S b^dagger quad\nS_z = b^dagger b - S\n\nvalid for low excitation langle b^dagger b rangle ll 2 S.  Then S_x = (S_+ + S_-)2 approx sqrtS2(b + b^dagger) and inserting g = 2lambdasqrt2S into the Dicke Hamiltonian yields the polariton Hamiltonian\n\nH_mathrmpol\n= omega_0 a^dagger a + omega_a b^dagger b\n  + lambda(a + a^dagger)(b + b^dagger)\n\nwhich is quadratic in two bosonic modes, exactly the kind of model the two-mode Bogoliubov example solves.  We build it on a fresh two-mode Fock space:\n\nha2 = FockSpace(:a)\nhb2 = FockSpace(:b)\nh_pol = ha2 ⊗ hb2\n\n@qnumbers a_pol::Destroy(h_pol, 1)\n@qnumbers b_pol::Destroy(h_pol, 2)\n\n@variables λ\nH_pol = ω₀ * a_pol' * a_pol + ωₐ * b_pol' * b_pol +\n    λ * (a_pol + a_pol') * (b_pol + b_pol')\n\nλ * a_pol * b_pol + λ * a_pol * b_pol' + ω₀ * a_pol' * a_pol + λ * a_pol' * b_pol + λ * a_pol' * b_pol' + ωₐ * b_pol' * b_pol","title":"Holstein-Primakoff bosonisation in the normal phase"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Polariton-spectrum-from-a-Bogoliubov-de-Gennes-determinant","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"The Heisenberg equations for mathbfv = (a b a^dagger b^dagger)^T close into a linear system dotmathbf v = Mmathbf v.  Each row comes from a single symbolic commutator:\n\n-1im * commutator(a_pol, H_pol)\n\n(-ω₀)im * a_pol + (-λ)im * b_pol + (-λ)im * b_pol'\n\n-1im * commutator(b_pol, H_pol)\n\n(-λ)im * a_pol + (-λ)im * a_pol' + (-ωₐ)im * b_pol\n\nReading off the coefficients,\n\nM = ibeginpmatrix\n   -omega_0  -lambda  0  -lambda \n   -lambda  -omega_a  -lambda  0 \n    0  lambda  omega_0  lambda \n    lambda  0  lambda  omega_a\nendpmatrix\n\nThe polariton frequencies are the moduli of the eigenvalues of M.  Their characteristic polynomial is biquadratic and tractable symbolically:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra\n@variables x\nM = [\n    -1im * ω₀   -1im * λ     0          -1im * λ;\n    -1im * λ   -1im * ωₐ    -1im * λ       0;\n    0        1im * λ      1im * ω₀      1im * λ;\n    1im * λ    0          1im * λ       1im * ωₐ\n]\n\nsimplify(det(M - x * I))\n\n-((x^2)*λ - (x^2 + λ^2 - ω₀*ωₐ)*λ + λ^3 + λ*ω₀*ωₐ)*λ + ((x^2)*(ω₀ + ωₐ) - (x^2 + 2(λ^2) - ω₀*ωₐ)*ωₐ)*ω₀ - x*(-x*(x^2 + λ^2 - ω₀*ωₐ) + x*(λ^2) - x*(ω₀ + ωₐ)*ωₐ) + im*(-x*((x^2)*(ω₀ + ωₐ) - (x^2 + 2(λ^2) - ω₀*ωₐ)*ωₐ) - (-x*(x^2 + λ^2 - ω₀*ωₐ) + x*(λ^2) - x*(ω₀ + ωₐ)*ωₐ)*ω₀ - x*(λ^2)*(ω₀ + ωₐ) + (x*λ*ω₀ - x*λ*ωₐ)*λ)\n\nSo det(M - x I) = x^4 + (omega_a^2 + omega_0^2)x^2 + omega_aomega_0(omega_aomega_0 - 4lambda^2).\n\nSetting y = -x^2 (so x = pm ivarepsilon with varepsilon = sqrty) gives the polariton dispersion\n\nvarepsilon_pm^2\n= tfrac12Bigl\n   (omega_a^2 + omega_0^2)\n   mp sqrt(omega_a^2 - omega_0^2)^2 + 16lambda^2omega_aomega_0\nBigr","title":"Polariton spectrum from a Bogoliubov-de-Gennes determinant"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#The-same-dispersion-from-a-beamsplitter","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"On resonance, omega_a = omega_0, the two-mode problem factorises, and the transformation that factorises it is a beamsplitter: Rotation of two Fock modes acts as a mapsto costhetaa + sinthetab.\n\n@variables θ\nH_res = substitute(H_pol, Dict(ωₐ => ω₀))\n\nconjugate(H_res, Rotation(a_pol, b_pol, θ))\n\n-cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ * a_pol * a_pol + ((cos(θ)^2)*λ - (sin(θ)^2)*λ) * a_pol * b_pol + ((cos(θ)^2)*λ - (sin(θ)^2)*λ) * a_pol * b_pol' + (ω₀ - 2cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ) * a_pol' * a_pol - cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ * a_pol' * a_pol' + ((cos(θ)^2)*λ - (sin(θ)^2)*λ) * a_pol' * b_pol + ((cos(θ)^2)*λ - (sin(θ)^2)*λ) * a_pol' * b_pol' + cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ * b_pol * b_pol + (ω₀ + 2cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ) * b_pol' * b_pol + cos(θ)*sin(θ)*λ * b_pol' * b_pol'\n\nThe cross-mode coefficient is lambda(cos^2theta - sin^2theta) = lambdacos 2theta, which vanishes at theta = pi4.  There the normal modes c_mp = (a mp b)sqrt2 decouple into two single-mode squeezing Hamiltonians\n\nH_mp = (omega_0 mp lambda)c_mp^dagger c_mp\n  mp tfraclambda2bigl(c_mp^2 + c_mp^dagger 2bigr)\n\neach diagonalised by a single-mode Squeeze (see the optomechanics example) at\n\nvarepsilon_mp = sqrt(omega_0 mp lambda)^2 - lambda^2\n  = sqrtomega_0^2 mp 2lambdaomega_0\n\nwhich is the resonant limit of the dispersion above.","title":"The same dispersion from a beamsplitter"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Critical-coupling-from-gap-closing","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"Demanding varepsilon_- = 0 reduces to (omega_a^2 + omega_0^2)^2 = (omega_a^2 - omega_0^2)^2 + 16lambda^2omega_aomega_0, which simplifies to 4omega_a^2omega_0^2 = 16lambda^2omega_aomega_0 and hence\n\nboxedlambda_c = tfrac12sqrtomega_aomega_0\n\nFor lambda  lambda_c the lower polariton frequency becomes imaginary and the normal-phase Holstein-Primakoff vacuum is dynamically unstable. The macroscopic occupation of the cavity that defines the superradiant phase arises precisely to restabilise the spectrum around a shifted fixed point.","title":"Critical coupling from gap closing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/dicke/#Numerical-verification","page":"Dicke Superradiant Transition","text":"We diagonalise the finite-S Dicke Hamiltonian (S = 52, N = 5 atoms) using numeric_average and substitute, and compare both observables to the analytical predictions: the cavity occupation against the mean-field order parameter, and the gap of the first excited state against the lower polariton frequency varepsilon_-.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\nω₀_val, ωa_val = 1.0, 1.0\nλc = sqrt(ω₀_val * ωa_val) / 2\nS_val = 5 // 2\nN_val = 2 * float(S_val)\ng_of_λ(λ_val) = 2 * λ_val / sqrt(N_val)\n\nn_max = 30\nb_cav = FockBasis(n_max)\nb_spin = SpinBasis(S_val)\nb = b_cav ⊗ b_spin\n\n# Closed-form curves\nε_minus(λ_val) = sqrt(\n    max(\n        0.0,\n        0.5 * (\n            (ωa_val^2 + ω₀_val^2) -\n                sqrt((ωa_val^2 - ω₀_val^2)^2 + 16 * λ_val^2 * ωa_val * ω₀_val)\n        ),\n    ),\n)\nε_plus(λ_val) = sqrt(\n    0.5 * (\n        (ωa_val^2 + ω₀_val^2) +\n            sqrt((ωa_val^2 - ω₀_val^2)^2 + 16 * λ_val^2 * ωa_val * ω₀_val)\n    ),\n)\nn_mf(λ_val) = λ_val < λc ? 0.0 : (λ_val^2 / ω₀_val^2) * (1 - (λc / λ_val)^4)\n\nλs = range(0.0, 1.5 * λc, length = 30)\nn_over_N = Float64[]\ngap1 = Float64[]\ngap2 = Float64[]\nfor λ_val in λs\n    subs = Dict(ω₀ => ω₀_val, ωₐ => ωa_val, g => g_of_λ(λ_val))\n    H_op = dense(to_numeric(substitute(H, subs), b))\n    E = eigvals(Hermitian(H_op.data))\n    ψ0 = Ket(b, eigvecs(Hermitian(H_op.data))[:, 1])\n    push!(n_over_N, real(numeric_average(a' * a, ψ0)) / N_val)\n    push!(gap1, E[2] - E[1])\n    push!(gap2, E[3] - E[1])\nend\n\nfig = plot(; layout = (1, 2), size = (820, 360), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nscatter!(fig[1], collect(λs) ./ λc, n_over_N; label = \"finite S = $(S_val)\", marker = :circle)\nplot!(fig[1], collect(λs) ./ λc, n_mf.(λs); label = \"mean field\", linestyle = :dash)\nvline!(fig[1], [1.0]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dot, label = L\"\\lambda_c\")\nplot!(fig[1]; xlabel = L\"\\lambda / \\lambda_c\", ylabel = L\"\\langle a^\\dagger a \\rangle / N\", title = \"Superradiant order parameter\", legend = :topleft)\n\nscatter!(fig[2], collect(λs) ./ λc, gap1; label = L\"E_1 - E_0\", marker = :circle)\nscatter!(fig[2], collect(λs) ./ λc, gap2; label = L\"E_2 - E_0\", marker = :diamond)\nplot!(fig[2], collect(λs) ./ λc, ε_minus.(λs); label = L\"\\varepsilon_-\", linestyle = :dash)\nplot!(fig[2], collect(λs) ./ λc, ε_plus.(λs); label = L\"\\varepsilon_+\", linestyle = :dash)\nvline!(fig[2], [1.0]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dot, label = false)\nplot!(fig[2]; xlabel = L\"\\lambda / \\lambda_c\", ylabel = L\"\\Delta E / \\omega\", title = \"Polariton frequencies\", legend = :topright)\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nBoth panels tell the same story.  The finite-S cavity occupation (left) rounds the order-parameter onset on a scale set by 1S and approaches the mean-field curve quickly thereafter.  The right panel overlays the analytical polariton frequencies (dashed) on the numerical excitation gaps (markers): below lambda_c they coincide exactly, and the Holstein-Primakoff polariton spectrum is the low-lying excitation structure.  At lambda_c the lower branch varepsilon_- softens to zero, signalling the dynamical instability of the normal-phase vacuum. Above lambda_c the analytical varepsilon_- is undefined while E_1 - E_0 collapses into the parity-tunnel splitting (vanishing only as S to infty) and E_2 - E_0 reveals the re-opened polariton excitation around the displaced fixed point.  The entire story (SU(2) algebra, Heisenberg dynamics, polariton dispersion, and critical coupling) came out of a handful of commutator calls, a single det, and one beamsplitter.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/algebra/#Algebra-and-ordering","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"The Heisenberg equation of a Kerr oscillator, dot a = iH a with H = K a^dagger 2 a^2, asks to move an annihilation operator past two creation operators. Using a a^dagger = 1 gives dot a = -2iK a^dagger a^2; the same result comes out in normal order directly:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\na = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a)\n@variables K\n1im * commutator(K * a' * a' * a * a, a)\n(\u001b[34m-2\u001b[39mK)im * a' * a * a\n\nOperator expressions are kept in a canonical form at all times. Every product is reordered and reduced the moment it is formed, so like terms combine without further work.\n\nPages = [\"algebra.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Algebra and ordering"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/algebra/#Canonical-form","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"Each algebra brings its own rules, and all of them apply as soon as a product is formed.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 3) ⊗ PauliSpace(:qubit) ⊗ SpinSpace(:S) ⊗ PhaseSpace(:osc)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) b::Destroy(h, 1) x::Position(h) p::Momentum(h)\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\nτ(k) = Pauli(h, :τ, k)\nS(k) = Spin(h, :S, k)\n\nOperators of different subsystems are sorted by their position in the product space.\n\nx * a * σ(2, 1)\na * σ₂₁ * x\n\nEach bosonic mode is normal ordered, and modes with different names are grouped by name.\n\nb' * a' * b * a\na' * a * b' * b\n\nTransitions on the same system compose.\n\nσ(1, 2) * σ(2, 3)\nσ₁₃\n\nProducts of Pauli matrices reduce to a single Pauli matrix or a number.\n\nτ(1) * τ(2)\nim * τz\n\nSpin components are sorted by axis.\n\nS(2) * S(1)\n-im * Sz + Sx * Sy\n\nPosition stands to the left of momentum.\n\np * x\n-im + x * p\n\nnormal_order applies the same rules explicitly, for code that expects a separate ordering step.\n\nThe ground-state projector is the one deliberate exception. It stays as it is, since rewriting it through completeness would multiply the number of terms in dissipators and equations of motion.\n\nσ(1, 2) * σ(2, 1)\nσ₁₁\n\nCompleteness is applied only on request.\n\nexpand_completeness(σ(1, 2) * σ(2, 1))\n1 - σ₂₂ - σ₃₃\n\nAn expression in canonical form has its own type, which is what products, sums and commutators of operators return.","title":"Canonical form"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/algebra/#Commutators","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"Since both orderings of a product are already canonical, a commutator costs no more than two products and a subtraction. The Pauli matrices show both brackets at once: different components anticommute, and their commutator is the third component.\n\ncommutator(τ(1), τ(2)), anticommutator(τ(1), τ(2))\n(2im * τz, 0)\n\nFor a mode, the anticommutator of a and a^dagger is the symmetric combination that enters vacuum fluctuations.\n\nanticommutator(a, a')\n1 + 2 * a' * a","title":"Commutators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/algebra/#Finishing-coefficients","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"Coefficients are exact numbers or Symbolics expressions, as described in Parameters and coefficients. Polynomial coefficients cancel as terms combine, while identities between functions are applied only by simplify.\n\n@variables θ\nsimplify((sin(θ)^2 + cos(θ)^2) * a' * a)\na' * a\n\nexpand multiplies out products that sit inside functions of the coefficients, which are otherwise kept as written.\n\n@variables x y\nexpand(exp(x * (x + y)) * a)\nexp(x^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m + x*y) * a","title":"Finishing coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/algebra/#Symmetric-ordering","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"Phase-space methods such as the truncated Wigner approximation work with symmetrically ordered operators rather than normal-ordered ones. In symmetric order the photon number a^dagger a reads as (a^dagger a + a a^dagger)2 - 12, so the half quantum of vacuum noise becomes explicit.\n\nn_sym = normal_to_symmetric(a' * a)\n-1//2 + a' * a\n\nThe conversion is exact in both directions.\n\nsymmetric_to_normal(n_sym) == a' * a\ntrue","title":"Symmetric ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.normal_order","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"normal_order(expr::QField)\n\nBring expr into canonical, normal-ordered form.\n\nArithmetic already returns this form: *, +, -, ^, adjoint, commutator, Σ and substitute all canonicalize as they go, so normal_order leaves their results unchanged. It is there for code that expects an explicit ordering step. Formal operator functions such as cos or expim keep their form; only their polynomial arguments are ordered.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> a * a'\n1 + a' * a\n\njulia> normal_order(a * a')\n1 + a' * a\n\nSee also simplify, expand_completeness, normal_to_symmetric, symmetric_to_normal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.normal_order"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expand_completeness","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"expand_completeness(expr)\n\nEliminate every ground-state projector in expr through completeness.\n\nEach projector sigma^gg is replaced by 1 - sum_k neq g sigma^kk. Arithmetic never does this by itself, which keeps expressions short. Expand when the remaining projectors should be the independent variables, e.g. before deriving equations for their expectation values.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> σ11 = Transition(h, :σ, 1, 1);\n\njulia> expand_completeness(σ11)\n1 - σ₂₂\n\nSee also assume_distinct_index, normal_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expand_completeness"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"QAdd <: QField\n\nType of operator expressions that are polynomials in the operators, kept in canonical form.\n\nSums, products and commutators of operators return a QAdd, with every product ordered and reduced and like terms collected, so two equal polynomials compare equal. A single operator is an Op instead, and an expression that contains a formal function such as cos is a QExpr. length gives the number of terms, and sorted_arguments splits an expression into them. The type is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: QAdd\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> x = a * a'\n1 + a' * a\n\njulia> x isa QAdd, length(x)\n(true, 2)\n\nSee also QExpr, normal_order, sorted_arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.commutator","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"commutator(a, b)\n\nCommutator of a and b.\n\nReturns a b = ab - ba in canonical form. If either argument is a formal operator function, the result stays formal until lowered with taylor.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> commutator(a, a')\n1\n\njulia> commutator(a' * a, a)\n-a\n\nSee also anticommutator, normal_order, taylor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.commutator"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.anticommutator","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"anticommutator(a, b)\n\nAnticommutator of a and b.\n\nReturns a b = ab + ba in canonical form.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> anticommutator(a, a')\n1 + 2 * a' * a\n\nSee also commutator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.anticommutator"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/algebra/#SymbolicUtils.simplify-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QSym}","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"simplify(expr::QField)\n\nSimplify the scalar coefficients of expr symbolically.\n\nPolynomial coefficients already cancel as an expression is built. simplify goes further and applies symbolic identities, such as trigonometric ones, to every coefficient, then drops the terms that vanish. Because this step is expensive, it is best used once on a final result. Formal operator functions keep their form.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> @variables x;\n\njulia> expr = (sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2) * a' * a\n(cos(x)^2 + sin(x)^2) * a' * a\n\njulia> simplify(expr)\na' * a\n\nSee also normal_order, expand, expand_completeness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SymbolicUtils.simplify"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/algebra/#SymbolicUtils.expand-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"expand(expr::QField)\n\nMultiply out the scalar coefficients of expr.\n\nPolynomial coefficients are kept expanded already; expand also reaches products inside functions such as exp. Formal operator functions are not expanded.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> @variables x y;\n\njulia> expr = exp(x * (x + y)) * a;\n\njulia> expand(expr)\nexp(x^2 + x*y) * a\n\nSee also simplify, taylor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SymbolicUtils.expand"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.normal_to_symmetric","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"normal_to_symmetric(expr)\n\nRewrite a normal-ordered expression in symmetric (Weyl) order.\n\nThe result is written with the same monomials, but each now stands for its fully symmetrized product: a' * a reads as (a^dagger a + a a^dagger)2 and x * p as (xp + px)2. This is the form phase-space methods such as the truncated Wigner approximation work with. Bosonic modes and quadratures are converted; other operators pass through unchanged. The inverse is symmetric_to_normal.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> hf = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(hf);\n\njulia> normal_to_symmetric(a' * a)\n-1//2 + a' * a\n\njulia> hp = PhaseSpace(:osc);\n\njulia> @qnumbers x::Position(hp) p::Momentum(hp);\n\njulia> normal_to_symmetric(x * p)\n1//2im + x * p\n\nSee also symmetric_to_normal, normal_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.normal_to_symmetric"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/algebra/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.symmetric_to_normal","page":"Algebra and ordering","text":"symmetric_to_normal(expr)\n\nRewrite a symmetric (Weyl) ordered expression in normal order.\n\nEach monomial of expr is read as its symmetrized product, as returned by normal_to_symmetric, and expanded back into normal order. The two functions are exact inverses.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> hf = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(hf);\n\njulia> symmetric_to_normal(a' * a)\n1//2 + a' * a\n\njulia> normal_to_symmetric(symmetric_to_normal(a' * a))\na' * a\n\nSee also normal_to_symmetric, normal_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.symmetric_to_normal"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Many-body-systems","page":"Many-body systems","text":"An ensemble of identical atoms or an array of coupled modes is written once per site and summed. Consider N two-level atoms in a cavity, the Tavis–Cummings model\n\nH = Delta a^dagger a + sum_i=1^N g_i left( a^dagger sigma_i^12 + a sigma_i^21 right)\n\nThe site label i and the number of atoms N both stay symbolic, so equations are derived once for any N and any distribution of couplings. When the atoms are indistinguishable, collective operators describe the same ensemble without any site label.\n\nPages = [\"many_body.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Many-body systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Indices","page":"Many-body systems","text":"A site label is an Index. It belongs to one subsystem of the product space and runs over a range that may be a number or a symbol. For the Tavis–Cummings model the index runs over the atoms, the second factor.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n@variables N Δ\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atoms, 2)\ni = Index(h, :i, N, 2)\nj = Index(h, :j, N, 2)\n\nnote: Replaces `ClusterSpace`\nEarlier versions inherited ClusterSpace from QuantumCumulants.jl, which wrapped a fixed number of identical copies of a space. It has been removed, together with cluster_expand and insert_index, in favor of an Index with a symbolic range. The number of copies then stays symbolic, and the truncation order of a cumulant expansion is set in the solver rather than in the Hilbert space.","title":"Indices"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Indexed-operators-and-parameters","page":"Many-body systems","text":"An operator is placed on a site with IndexedOperator. Wrapping it in a function of the levels and the index keeps the notation close to sigma_i^kl.\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\nσ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m)\nσ(2, 1, i)\nσ_i₂₁\n\nOperators on different sites commute, while operators on the same site obey the local operator algebra. To retain individual atom labels, use indexed Transition operators as above and Σ to form their collective sums.\n\nA CollectiveTransition instead represents an entire ensemble; see Collective systems. An index on such an operator could label separate ensembles, each with its own collective algebra, but IndexedOperator does not currently support this. For a fixed number of ensembles, give each its own CollectiveNLevelSpace in a product space.\n\nSite-dependent parameters such as the couplings g_i are made with IndexedVariable, and pair parameters with DoubleIndexedVariable.\n\ng = IndexedVariable(:g, i)\ng(i)","title":"Indexed operators and parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Sums","page":"Many-body systems","text":"With these pieces the Hamiltonian is a single Σ.\n\nH = Δ * a' * a + Σ(g * (a' * σ(1, 2, i) + a * σ(2, 1, i)), i)\nΔ * a' * a + Σ(i=1:N) (g(i) * a * σ_i₂₁ + g(i) * a' * σ_i₁₂)\n\nA sum takes part in the algebra like any other expression. The Heisenberg equation of the cavity field collects the contribution of every atom.\n\n1im * commutator(H, a)\n(-Δ)im * a + Σ(i=1:N) (-g(i))im * σ_i₁₂","title":"Sums"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Coinciding-sites","page":"Many-body systems","text":"An index under a sum also runs over the site of every other index on the same subsystem. On that site the two operators act on one atom and compose, so the term is split off and the remaining sum excludes it.\n\nΣ(σ(2, 2, i), i) * σ(2, 1, j)\nσ_j₂₁ + Σ(i=1:N)(i≠j) σ_i₂₂ * σ_j₂₁\n\nA ground-state projector produced on the shared site stays as it is, as everywhere else.\n\nΣ(σ(1, 2, i), i) * σ(2, 1, j)\nσ_j₁₁ + Σ(i=1:N)(i≠j) σ_i₁₂ * σ_j₂₁","title":"Coinciding sites"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Free-indices","page":"Many-body systems","text":"Two indices that are not summed over may or may not label the same site. Their operators are therefore left in the order they were written, and no rule is applied between them.\n\nσ(2, 2, j) * σ(1, 2, i)\nσ_j₂₂ * σ_i₁₂\n\nWhen the indices are known to label different sites, assume_distinct_index says so, and the product is brought into canonical form.\n\nassume_distinct_index(σ(2, 2, j) * σ(1, 2, i), [(i, j)])\nσ_i₁₂ * σ_j₂₂","title":"Free indices"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Renaming-indices","page":"Many-body systems","text":"An equation derived for one site holds for every other site under a new index name, and a correlation between two sites is obtained from a single-site expression by renaming. change_index renames an index throughout and brings the result back into canonical form. The coherence of atom i becomes that of atom j.\n\nchange_index(a' * σ(1, 2, i), i, j)\na' * σ_j₁₂","title":"Renaming indices"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Inspecting-indices","page":"Many-body systems","text":"The indices of an expression and their properties are read off directly.\n\nget_indices(a' * σ(1, 2, i) * σ(2, 1, j))\n2-element Vector{Index}:\n i\n j\n\nindex_name(i), index_range(i)\n(:i, N)","title":"Inspecting indices"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Explicit-sites","page":"Many-body systems","text":"An index treats all of its sites alike. A lattice with nearest-neighbour couplings, open boundaries or a definite number of sites is written instead with one subsystem per site, as in the driven Bose–Hubbard chain of M coupled cavities\n\nH = sum_k=1^M left -Delta a_k^dagger a_k + fracU2 a_k^dagger a_k^dagger a_k a_k + Omega(a_k + a_k^dagger) right - J sum_k=1^M-1 left( a_k^dagger a_k+1 + a_k+1^dagger a_k right)\n\nEach site gets its own FockSpace, and both sums are a sum over a vector of terms.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n@variables Δ U Ω J\nM = 6\nhc = reduce(⊗, [FockSpace(Symbol(:c, k)) for k in 1:M])\na = [Destroy(hc, Symbol(:a, k), k) for k in 1:M]\n\nonsite = sum([-Δ * a[k]' * a[k] + (U / 2) * a[k]' * a[k]' * a[k] * a[k] + Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M])\nhopping = sum([-J * (a[k]' * a[k+1] + a[k+1]' * a[k]) for k in 1:(M-1)])\nH = onsite + hopping\nlength(H)\n34\n\nSumming a vector builds the result in place. A chain of + gives the same expression, but each + copies the sum accumulated so far, so n terms cost O(n^2) instead of O(n).\n\nterms = [Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M]\nsum(terms) == foldl(+, terms)\ntrue\n\nFor a few sites the difference is negligible. For long chains the copies outweigh the work of forming the terms themselves:\n\nSites M Terms Forming the terms foldl(+, terms) sum(terms)\n24 142 0.4 ms 0.18 ms 0.008 ms\n400 2398 6.3 ms 63 ms 0.22 ms\n\nwarning: Use brackets, not a bare generator or a `+=` loop\nThe fast path is taken by sum, reduce(+, …) and MutableArithmetics.operate(sum, …) over an array. A bare generator and an accumulation loop both fall back to repeated +:H = sum([Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M])            # fast\nH = sum(Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M)              # slow: generator\nH = 0; for k in 1:M; H += Ω * (a[k] + a[k]'); end     # slow: copies on every +=Wrapping the generator in brackets, or collecting the terms into a vector first, is enough. The extra vector of length M is negligible next to the quadratic saving.\n\nMutableArithmetics.@rewrite gives the same result, but its macro expansion adds overhead and it does not take the fast path for generators. It suits code that already composes a larger MutableArithmetics expression.\n\nimport MutableArithmetics as MA\nfast = sum([Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M])\nfast == MA.operate(sum, [Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M]) == MA.@rewrite(sum(Ω * (a[k] + a[k]') for k in 1:M))\n\nThe binary +, - and scalar * copy because the caller still holds both operands, so neither may be modified in place. Only a reduction over a whole collection can build its result in one buffer. The same holds wherever many terms are added up:\n\nequations of motion and cumulant systems, where each derived fracddtlangle O rangle is a sum of many contributions;\nensembles written with explicit sites, for instance collective operators S_pm = sum_i sigma_i^pm;\nLindbladians with one dissipator per channel;\ntruncated Magnus, Dyson or BCH expansions.\n\nProducts are not affected; their cost lies in the reordering itself. A symbolic Σ stays a single sum however many sites it covers, so the question does not arise there.","title":"Explicit sites"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/many_body/#Collective-systems","page":"Many-body systems","text":"Collective operators S^ij = sum_k sigma_k^ij act on the ensemble as a whole and form a closed algebra without individual site labels. This algebra holds on the full atomic Hilbert space as well as within its permutation-symmetric sector. The numerical example below chooses a basis for that sector; this restriction belongs to the numerical representation, not to the symbolic commutation relations. The two descriptions suit different problems:\n\n Indexed sites Collective operators\nOperators sum_k sigma_k^ij with Index and Σ CollectiveTransition S^ij\nNumber of atoms symbolic fixed by the numerical basis\nSite-dependent parameters yes no\nSite-resolved moments and correlations yes no\nNumerical basis full product space for a concrete N permutation-symmetric sector in the example below\n\nThey encode different physical roles and live on different Hilbert-space types, so they do not mix on one subsystem.\n\nThe number of systems enters only through the numerical basis. A bosonic many-body basis over an NLevelBasis with N particles is the permutation-symmetric sector, of dimension binomN+n-1n-1 for n levels, so N + 1 for two.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra, QuantumOpticsBase\nh = CollectiveNLevelSpace(:atoms, (:g, :e))\nS(k, l) = CollectiveTransition(h, :S, k, l)\n\nN = 10\nb1 = NLevelBasis(2)\nb = ManyBodyBasis(b1, bosonstates(b1, N))\nlength(b)\n11\n\nIn this basis the collective projectors add up to the number of atoms.\n\nto_numeric(S(:g, :g) + S(:e, :e), b) ≈ N * identityoperator(b)\ntrue\n\nFor two levels this is the collective spin of length N2. With e as the upper level, S^eg = S_+, S^ge = S_- and S_z = (S^ee - S^gg)2. In the ordering of the SpinBasis of QuantumOpticsBase, where level 1 is spin up, the same identification reads S^12 = S_+.\n\nget_sum_indices(expr) reads only the bound summation indices. It returns an empty vector for an unsummed expression.","title":"Collective systems"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Index","page":"Many-body systems","text":"Index(h::HilbertSpace, name::Symbol, range, space::HilbertSpace)\nIndex(h::HilbertSpace, name::Symbol, range, subsystem::Int)\ni(k::Integer)\n\nSymbolic label for one of many identical sites.\n\nAn index runs from 1 to range, an integer or a symbolic parameter such as N, over the factor of h given either by the space itself or by its position. Attached to operators with IndexedOperator and to parameters with IndexedVariable, it is summed over with Σ. Two indices are equal only if they have the same name, range and subsystem.\n\nCalling an index with an integer, i(3), gives the index of a concrete site. It is named i_3, keeps the range and subsystem of i, and addresses that site after a sum has been written out site by site, for instance in numerical conversion or in cumulant equations.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @variables N;\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> i = Index(h, :i, N, NLevelSpace(:atom, 2));\n\njulia> i == Index(h, :i, N, 2)\ntrue\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> IndexedOperator(σ, i), IndexedOperator(σ, i(3))\n(σ_i₂₁, σ_i_3₂₁)\n\nSee also IndexedOperator, Σ, index_name, index_range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Index"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.IndexedOperator","page":"Many-body systems","text":"IndexedOperator(op, i::Index)\n\nCopy of the operator op placed on site i.\n\nOperators on different sites act independently; operators on the same site follow the algebra of op. Any operator except a CollectiveTransition can be indexed.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> IndexedOperator(σ, i) * IndexedOperator(σ', i)\nσ_i₂₂\n\nSee also Index, Σ, operator_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.IndexedOperator"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.IndexedVariable","page":"Many-body systems","text":"IndexedVariable(name::Symbol, i::Index)\n\nSite-dependent parameter name(i), such as a local detuning or coupling.\n\nThe parameter is real. Under a sum it follows the index, so it is evaluated site by site once the sum is unrolled.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> g = IndexedVariable(:g, i)\ng(i)\n\njulia> Σ(g * IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1), i), i)\nΣ(i=1:10) g(i) * σ_i₂₁\n\nSee also DoubleIndexedVariable, Index, index_sym.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.IndexedVariable"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.DoubleIndexedVariable","page":"Many-body systems","text":"DoubleIndexedVariable(name::Symbol, i::Index, j::Index; identical = true)\n\nPair parameter name(i, j), such as a hopping amplitude or a dipole-dipole coupling.\n\nWith identical = false the parameter vanishes whenever both indices refer to the same site, so the diagonal terms of a double sum drop out.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> J = DoubleIndexedVariable(:J, i, j; identical = false);\n\njulia> Σ(J * σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), i, j)\nΣ(i=1:10)Σ(j=1:10)(i≠j) J(i, j) * σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₁₂\n\nSee also IndexedVariable, Σ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.DoubleIndexedVariable"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Σ","page":"Many-body systems","text":"Σ(expr, i::Index, [non_equal::Vector{Index}])\nΣ(expr, i::Index, j::Index, more::Index...)\n∑(expr, i, ...)\n\nSymbolic sum of expr over all sites of the index i.\n\nSeveral indices give a nested sum. The sum stays symbolic and follows the algebra like any other expression. Where its index meets another index on the same subsystem, the terms in which the two sites coincide are split off, so the remaining sum carries the constraint i ≠ j. The optional non_equal adds such constraints by hand, excluding those sites from the sum. A summand that does not depend on i is multiplied by the number of sites.\n\n∑ (typed \\sum<tab>) is the same function.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @variables N;\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, N, h); j = Index(h, :j, N, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> Σ(σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), i, j)\nΣ(j=1:N) σ_j₂₂ + Σ(i=1:N)Σ(j=1:N)(i≠j) σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₁₂\n\njulia> Σ(σ(2, 2, j), i, [j])\n(-1 + N) * σ_j₂₂\n\nSee also Index, IndexedOperator, assume_distinct_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Σ"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.assume_distinct_index","page":"Many-body systems","text":"assume_distinct_index(expr, pairs::Vector{Tuple{Index, Index}})\n\nTreat each pair of free indices in pairs as different sites.\n\nTwo free indices on the same subsystem may or may not refer to the same site, so their operators are left as they are. Outside a Σ, which settles this by itself, this function supplies the missing information: the operators are then sorted into canonical order, and ground-state projectors in the result are eliminated with expand_completeness.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> σ(2, 1, j) * σ(1, 2, i)\nσ_j₂₁ * σ_i₁₂\n\njulia> assume_distinct_index(σ(2, 1, j) * σ(1, 2, i), [(i, j)])\nσ_i₁₂ * σ_j₂₁\n\nSee also Σ, expand_completeness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.assume_distinct_index"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.change_index","page":"Many-body systems","text":"change_index(expr, from::Index, to::Index)\nchange_index(expr, pairs::AbstractDict{Index, Index})\n\nReplace the index from by to in operators and coefficients of expr.\n\nThe result is brought back into canonical form, so operators that now share a site compose, and a DoubleIndexedVariable declared with identical = false vanishes when its two indices become equal. The second form renames several indices at once, which makes swaps such as Dict(i => j, j => i) possible.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> change_index(σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), i, j)\nσ_j₂₂\n\njulia> change_index(σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), Dict(i => j, j => i))\nσ_j₂₁ * σ_i₁₂\n\nSee also get_indices, substitute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.change_index"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_indices","page":"Many-body systems","text":"get_indices(expr) -> Vector{Index}\n\nIndices occurring in expr, both summed and free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> get_indices(Σ(σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), i))\n2-element Vector{Index}:\n i\n j\n\nSee also change_index, has_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_index","page":"Many-body systems","text":"has_index(i::Index) -> Bool\n\nWhether i is an index rather than the placeholder carried by unindexed operators.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> has_index(operator_index(σ)), has_index(operator_index(IndexedOperator(σ, i)))\n(false, true)\n\nSee also operator_index, get_indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_index"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_name","page":"Many-body systems","text":"index_name(i::Index) -> Symbol\n\nName of the index i, as given at construction.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> index_name(i), index_name(i(3))\n(:i, :i_3)\n\nSee also index_range, index_sym, Index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_name"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_range","page":"Many-body systems","text":"index_range(i::Index)\n\nNumber of sites the index i runs over, as given at construction.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @variables N;\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, N, h);\n\njulia> index_range(i)\nN\n\nSee also index_name, Index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_range"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_slot","page":"Many-body systems","text":"index_slot(i::Index) -> Int\nindex_slot(x) -> Int\n\nSite number of a concrete index such as i(3), or 0 for a symbolic index.\n\nThe second form reads the site number from the symbolic variable of an index, as found in the coefficients of an expression.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> index_slot(i), index_slot(i(3))\n(0, 3)\n\nSee also Index, index_sym.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_slot"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_sym","page":"Many-body systems","text":"index_sym(i::Index) -> Num\n\nSymbolic variable that stands for the index i in coefficients.\n\nIndexed parameters such as g(i) are functions of this variable, so it is what to substitute when a coefficient is evaluated at a given site.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> g = IndexedVariable(:g, i);\n\njulia> substitute(g, Dict(index_sym(i) => 2))\ng(2)\n\nSee also IndexedVariable, index_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.index_sym"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.rename","page":"Many-body systems","text":"rename(i::Index, name::Symbol)\nrename(op::Op, name::Symbol)\n\nCopy of an index or operator under a new name, with everything else kept.\n\nFor an index this gives a second index over the same sites, for instance the partner index of a pair interaction. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: rename\n\njulia> @variables N;\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, N, h);\n\njulia> j = rename(i, :j)\nj\n\njulia> j == Index(h, :j, N, h)\ntrue\n\nSee also Index, change_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.rename"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.constraint_pairs","page":"Many-body systems","text":"constraint_pairs(expr) -> Vector{Tuple{Index, Index}}\n\nPairs of indices that expr requires to be distinct, collected over all its terms.\n\nThe function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: constraint_pairs\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> σ(k, l, m) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l), m);\n\njulia> constraint_pairs(Σ(σ(2, 1, i) * σ(1, 2, j), i))\n1-element Vector{Tuple{Index, Index}}:\n (i, j)\n\nSee also Σ, assume_distinct_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.constraint_pairs"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CollectiveNLevelSpace","page":"Many-body systems","text":"CollectiveNLevelSpace(name::Symbol, n::Int)\nCollectiveNLevelSpace(name::Symbol, levels::Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}})\n\nHilbert space of an ensemble of identical n-level systems, home of the CollectiveTransition operators.\n\nThe number of systems is not part of the space; it is fixed only by the basis chosen for numerics. Levels are numbered or named as for NLevelSpace. There is no ground state to eliminate, since the collective projectors add up to the number of systems rather than to one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = CollectiveNLevelSpace(:atoms, (:g, :e))\nℋ(atoms)\n\njulia> S = CollectiveTransition(h, :S, :e, :g);\n\njulia> commutator(S, S')\n-S₁₁ + S₂₂\n\nSee also CollectiveTransition, NLevelSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CollectiveNLevelSpace"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CollectiveTransition","page":"Many-body systems","text":"CollectiveTransition(h::CollectiveNLevelSpace, name::Symbol, i, j)\nCollectiveTransition(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, i, j, [subsystem::Int])\n\nCollective transition from level j to level i, summed over all systems of the ensemble.\n\nThe operator is S^ij = sum_k irangle_klangle j. Collective transitions obey S^ij S^kl = delta_jkS^il - delta_liS^kj and are normal ordered like ladder operators. Unlike a single Transition, a product of two of them does not compose, since it includes pairs of different systems. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the CollectiveNLevelSpace factor and may be omitted when there is only one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = CollectiveNLevelSpace(:atoms, (:g, :e));\n\njulia> @qnumbers S::CollectiveTransition(h, :e, :g);\n\njulia> S' * S\nS₁₁ - S₂₂ + S₂₁ * S₁₂\n\nSee also CollectiveNLevelSpace, Transition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CollectiveTransition"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/many_body/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}","page":"Many-body systems","text":"get_sum_indices(q::QAdd) -> Vector{Index}\nget_sum_indices(q::QSym) -> Vector{Index}\n\nThe summation indices bound by q, in the order of its sum scope. A QSym and an unsummed QAdd bind none. The result is a copy, so mutating it does not change q.\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:site);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> i = Index(h, :i, 3, h);\n\njulia> SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices(Σ(IndexedOperator(a, i), i)) == [i]\ntrue\n\nSee also get_indices, constraint_pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Two-Mode-Bogoliubov-Transformation","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"The two-mode squeezing Hamiltonian\n\nH = omegabigl(a^dagger a + b^dagger bbigr)\n  + kappabigl(a^dagger b^dagger + abbigr)\n\narises whenever a quadratic bosonic system mixes particles and holes: in parametric down-conversion, in the Bogoliubov-de-Gennes treatment of weakly interacting Bose-Einstein condensates, and in the linearised dynamics of any optomechanical or polariton system above its Hopf bifurcation.  A canonical transformation\n\nc = ua + vb^dagger qquad d = ub + va^dagger\n\nbrings H into diagonal form when u and v are chosen correctly. In this example we use SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl to drive this story end to end without ever resorting to a pen-and-paper expansion: symbolic commutators, Heisenberg dynamics, vacuum expectation values, EPR squeezing, and Weyl (Wigner) symbol.","title":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Setup","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"using SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nha = FockSpace(:a)\nhb = FockSpace(:b)\nh = ha ⊗ hb\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\n@qnumbers b::Destroy(h, 2)\n\n@variables ω κ u v\n\nH = ω * (a' * a + b' * b) + κ * (a' * b' + a * b)\n\nκ * a * b + ω * a' * a + κ * a' * b' + ω * b' * b","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Canonical-commutator-and-dispersion","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"Define the Bogoliubov modes and verify the commutator:\n\nc = u * a + v * b'\nd = u * b + v * a'\n\ncommutator(c, c')\n\nu^2 - (v^2)\n\nSo c c^dagger = u^2 - v^2: a proper bosonic mode requires u^2 - v^2 = 1.  The two new modes commute among themselves:\n\ncommutator(c, d), commutator(c, d')\n\n(0, 0)\n\nTry the ansatz H = varepsilon(c^dagger c + d^dagger d) + textconst. The package canonicalises c^dagger c + d^dagger d eagerly, returning a polynomial in the original a b:\n\nc' * c + d' * d\n\n2(v^2) + 2u*v * a * b + (u^2 + v^2) * a' * a + 2u*v * a' * b' + (u^2 + v^2) * b' * b\n\nMatching coefficients with H:\n\nvarepsilon(u^2 + v^2) = omega qquad 2varepsilonu v = kappa\nqquad u^2 - v^2 = 1\n\nSquaring and subtracting yields the Bogoliubov dispersion\n\nvarepsilon^2 = (omega - kappa)(omega + kappa) Longrightarrow\nvarepsilon = sqrtomega^2 - kappa^2\n\nvalid for kappa  omega.  Beyond this point the system is dynamically unstable.","title":"Canonical commutator and dispersion"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Heisenberg-dynamics:-the-BdG-matrix","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"An equivalent route is to derive the Heisenberg equations of motion and diagonalise the resulting linear system.  The commutator structure pairs a with b^dagger and b with a^dagger:\n\neq_a = -1im * commutator(a, H)\neq_bd = -1im * commutator(b', H)\n\nκim * a + ωim * b'\n\nReading off the coefficients, dot a = -iomegaa - ikappab^dagger and dot b^dagger = +ikappaa + iomegab^dagger, the closed (a b^dagger) block evolves under the Bogoliubov-de-Gennes matrix\n\nM = beginpmatrix -iomega  -ikappa  phantom-ikappa  phantom-iomega endpmatrix\n\nIts trace vanishes and its determinant is exactly omega^2 - kappa^2, both readable symbolically:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra\nM = [\n    -1im * ω   -1im * κ;\n    1im * κ    1im * ω\n]\ntr(M), simplify(det(M))\n\n(0, -(κ^2) + ω^2)\n\nThe characteristic equation lambda^2 + (omega^2 - kappa^2) = 0 gives the normal-mode frequencies lambda = pm ivarepsilon, recovering the dispersion derived algebraically above.","title":"Heisenberg dynamics: the BdG matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Vacuum-expectations-from-the-inverse-transformation","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"A different trick (also handled fully symbolically) gives the vacuum correlation functions of the Bogoliubov ground state without diagonalising anything numerically.  Treat the Bogoliubov modes as the fundamental Fock operators, and rebuild the physical operators on top of them via the inverse transformation a = u c - v d^dagger, b = u d - v c^dagger. Then the package's eager normal ordering does all of the work: any remaining c-number term in the canonical form is automatically the BdG vacuum expectation value.\n\nhc = FockSpace(:c)\nhd = FockSpace(:d)\nh_cd = hc ⊗ hd\n\n@qnumbers c_bog::Destroy(h_cd, 1)\n@qnumbers d_bog::Destroy(h_cd, 2)\n\na_phys = u * c_bog - v * d_bog'\nb_phys = u * d_bog - v * c_bog'\n\n-v * c_bog' + u * d_bog\n\nPhoton number in mode a:\n\na_phys' * a_phys\n\nv^2 - u*v * c_bog * d_bog + u^2 * c_bog' * c_bog - u*v * c_bog' * d_bog' + v^2 * d_bog' * d_bog\n\nThe constant term v^2 is langle a^dagger arangle in the BdG vacuum.  Squeezing populates the physical modes with v^2 = sinh^2 r photons per mode even though the Bogoliubov vacuum is empty.\n\na_phys * b_phys\n\n-u*v + u^2 * c_bog * d_bog - u*v * c_bog' * c_bog + v^2 * c_bog' * d_bog' - u*v * d_bog' * d_bog\n\nThe constant -uv is the pair correlation langle ab rangle = -u v = -tfrac12sinh(2 r), the hallmark of a two-mode squeezed state.","title":"Vacuum expectations from the inverse transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#EPR-quadratures-and-two-mode-squeezing","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"Define unnormalised quadratures q_j = a_j + a_j^dagger and p_j = -i(a_j - a_j^dagger) (single-mode vacuum variance is 1).  The symmetric and anti-symmetric EPR combinations are Q_pm = (q_a pm q_b)sqrt2 and P_pm = (p_a pm p_b)sqrt2. We omit the 1sqrt2 in code and reinstate it in the final reading.\n\nq_a = a_phys + a_phys'\nq_b = b_phys + b_phys'\np_a = -1im * (a_phys - a_phys')\np_b = -1im * (b_phys - b_phys')\n\nQ_plus = q_a + q_b\nP_plus = p_a + p_b\n\nQ_plus * Q_plus\n\n2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2) + (u^2 - 2u*v + v^2) * c_bog * c_bog + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog * d_bog + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog * d_bog' + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * c_bog + (u^2 - 2u*v + v^2) * c_bog' * c_bog' + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * d_bog + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * d_bog' + (u^2 - 2u*v + v^2) * d_bog * d_bog + (2(u^2) - 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * d_bog' * d_bog + (u^2 - 2u*v + v^2) * d_bog' * d_bog'\n\nP_plus * P_plus\n\n2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2) - (u^2 + 2u*v + v^2) * c_bog * c_bog - (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog * d_bog + (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog * d_bog' + (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * c_bog - (u^2 + 2u*v + v^2) * c_bog' * c_bog' + (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * d_bog - (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * c_bog' * d_bog' - (u^2 + 2u*v + v^2) * d_bog * d_bog + (2(u^2) + 4u*v + 2(v^2)) * d_bog' * d_bog - (u^2 + 2u*v + v^2) * d_bog' * d_bog'\n\nThe constant term of Q_+^2 is 2(u-v)^2 and of P_+^2 is 2(u+v)^2.  Dividing by the omitted normalisation factor 2:\n\nmathrmVar(Q_+) = (u - v)^2 = e^-2 r\nqquad\nmathrmVar(P_+) = (u + v)^2 = e^+2 r\n\nwhere u = cosh r, v = sinh r.  The product\n\nmathrmVar(Q_+)mathrmVar(P_+) = (u^2 - v^2)^2 = 1\n\nsaturates the Heisenberg bound, and one quadrature drops below the single-mode shot-noise level 1, the defining signature of two-mode squeezing.","title":"EPR quadratures and two-mode squeezing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Weyl-symbol-and-the-zero-point-energy","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"A perhaps less expected payoff is the explicit access to the Wigner-Weyl symbol via normal_to_symmetric.  Applied to the normal-ordered Hamiltonian:\n\nnormal_to_symmetric(H)\n\n-ω + κ * a * b + ω * a' * a + κ * a' * b' + ω * b' * b\n\nThe conversion adds a c-number -omega to the expression.  This is the zero-point energy that the normal-ordered Hamiltonian conceals: each bosonic mode contributes +frac12omega upon symmetric ordering, and the two-mode sum is omega.  Replacing the (now Weyl-ordered) operators by c-numbers gives the Wigner symbol\n\nH_W(alpha beta)\n= omegabigl(alpha^2 + beta^2bigr)\n  + kappabigl(alpha^*beta^* + alphabetabigr) - omega\n\nwhose constant -omega is Bogoliubov-invariant.  Exactly the same shift appears on the diagonalised form H = varepsilon(c^dagger c + d^dagger d) - 2varepsilon v^2:\n\n@variables ε\nH_diag = ε * (c_bog' * c_bog + d_bog' * d_bog) - 2 * ε * v^2\nnormal_to_symmetric(H_diag)\n\n-ε - 2(v^2)*ε + ε * c_bog' * c_bog + ε * d_bog' * d_bog\n\nUsing the matching condition varepsilon(u^2 + v^2) = omega together with u^2 - v^2 = 1, the Bogoliubov-frame constant -varepsilon(1 + 2 v^2) = -varepsilon(u^2 + v^2) = -omega coincides with the original.","title":"Weyl symbol and the zero-point energy"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bogoliubov/#Numerical-verification","page":"Two-Mode Bogoliubov Transformation","text":"We diagonalise H in a truncated Fock space and compare both the ground-state energy and the photon number langle a^dagger arangle to the closed-form predictions.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\nω_val = 1.0\nn_max = 18\nb_a_num = FockBasis(n_max)\nb_b_num = FockBasis(n_max)\nb_total = b_a_num ⊗ b_b_num\n\nE0_th(κ_val) = sqrt(ω_val^2 - κ_val^2) - ω_val\n# ⟨a†a⟩ = v² where 2v² = (ω - ε)/ε = ω/ε - 1\nn_th(κ_val) = (ω_val / sqrt(ω_val^2 - κ_val^2) - 1) / 2\n\nκs = range(0.0, 0.9 * ω_val, length = 25)\nE0_num = Float64[]\nn_num = Float64[]\nfor κ_val in κs\n    subs = Dict(ω => ω_val, κ => κ_val)\n    H_op = dense(to_numeric(substitute(H, subs), b_total))\n    F = eigen(Hermitian(H_op.data))\n    ψ = Ket(b_total, F.vectors[:, 1])\n    push!(E0_num, F.values[1])\n    push!(n_num, real(numeric_average(a' * a, ψ)))\nend\n\nfig = plot(; layout = (1, 2), size = (820, 360), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nscatter!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ ω_val, E0_num; label = \"diagonalisation\", marker = :circle)\nplot!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ ω_val, E0_th.(κs); label = L\"\\sqrt{\\omega^2-\\kappa^2}-\\omega\", linestyle = :dash)\nplot!(fig[1]; xlabel = L\"\\kappa / \\omega\", ylabel = L\"E_0 / \\omega\", title = \"Ground-state energy\", legend = :bottomleft)\n\nscatter!(fig[2], collect(κs) ./ ω_val, n_num; label = \"diagonalisation\", marker = :diamond)\nplot!(fig[2], collect(κs) ./ ω_val, n_th.(κs); label = L\"v^2\", linestyle = :dash)\nplot!(fig[2]; xlabel = L\"\\kappa / \\omega\", ylabel = L\"\\langle a^\\dagger a \\rangle\", title = L\"\\mathrm{Photon\\ number} = v^2\", legend = :topleft)\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nBoth observables match the closed-form predictions to truncation error: the entire Bogoliubov story (dispersion, vacuum correlations, EPR squeezing, and zero-point energy) emerged from a handful of symbolic manipulations carried out by the eager canonicalisation built into the package.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/tavis_cummings/#Exact-finite-N-Tavis–Cummings-model-in-the-symmetric-subspace","page":"Tavis–Cummings Model","text":"CollectiveTransition keeps the atomic Hilbert space polynomial in atom number while retaining exact finite-N dynamics. For two-level atoms the symmetric sector has dimension N + 1, instead of 2^N for the full product space.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nusing QuantumOpticsBase\n\nN = 20\nnphotons = 12\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nha = CollectiveNLevelSpace(:atoms, (:g, :e))\nh = hc ⊗ ha\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\nSge = CollectiveTransition(h, :S, :g, :e, 2)\nSeg = Sge'\nSee = CollectiveTransition(h, :S, :e, :e, 2)\nSgg = CollectiveTransition(h, :S, :g, :g, 2)\n\n@variables ωc ωa g\nH = ωc * a' * a + ωa * See + g * (a' * Sge + a * Seg)\n\nωa * S₂₂ + g * a * S₂₁ + ωc * a' * a + g * a' * S₁₂\n\nThe symbolic algebra applies the collective SU(2) commutator.\n\niszero(simplify(commutator(Sge, Seg) - Sgg + See))\n\ntrue\n\nFixing total atomic occupation to N constructs the symmetric sector.\n\nbc = FockBasis(nphotons)\nb1 = NLevelBasis(2)\nba = ManyBodyBasis(b1, bosonstates(b1, N))\nb = bc ⊗ ba\n\n[Fock(cutoff=12) ⊗ ManyBody(onebodybasis=NLevel(N=2), states:21)]\n\nSubstitute physical parameters and materialize the exact sparse Hamiltonian.\n\nHnum = to_numeric(H, b; parameter = Dict(ωc => 1.0, ωa => 1.0, g => 0.1))\n\n@assert length(ba) == N + 1\n@assert size(Hnum.data) == (length(b), length(b))\n\nDiagonal collective populations sum to N, not one.\n\nnumber_identity = to_numeric(Sgg + See, b)\n@assert number_identity ≈ N * one(b)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Exact finite-N Tavis–Cummings model in the symmetric subspace"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Two-Atom-Dipole-Dipole:-Bright-and-Dark-States","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Two identical two-level atoms in close proximity exchange virtual photons, giving rise to a dipole-dipole interaction that splits the single-excitation manifold into a symmetric (bright, superradiant) and antisymmetric (dark, subradiant) eigenstate.  This is the elementary building block of collective atomic physics and the simplest non-trivial setting for the package's index machinery: two indexed atomic operators on the same Hilbert subspace, kept distinct via assume_distinct_index.","title":"Two-Atom Dipole-Dipole: Bright and Dark States"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Setup","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"A single two-level Hilbert space, populated by two atoms via abstract indices i and j.  The convention (:g, :e) lets us name transitions by symbol rather than by integer level.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nha = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\n\ni = Index(ha, :i, 2, ha)\nj = Index(ha, :j, 2, ha)\n\nσge(idx) = IndexedOperator(Transition(ha, :σ, :g, :e), idx)\nσeg(idx) = IndexedOperator(Transition(ha, :σ, :e, :g), idx)\nσee(idx) = IndexedOperator(Transition(ha, :σ, :e, :e), idx)\n\n@variables ω J\n\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Num}:\n ω\n J","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Hamiltonian","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Two identical atoms at frequency omega exchange excitations through a coherent dipole-dipole coupling J:\n\nH = omega(sigma_i^ee + sigma_j^ee)\n  + J(sigma_i^eg sigma_j^ge + sigma_j^eg sigma_i^ge)\n\nBuilding it with free indices leaves the site relationship i = j vs i ≠ j ambiguous.  Calling assume_distinct_index records the pair (i, j) as a permanent inequality constraint and triggers re-canonicalisation, including completeness expansion of any ground-state projectors that emerge from the new constraint:\n\nH = ω * (σee(i) + σee(j)) + J * (σeg(i) * σge(j) + σeg(j) * σge(i))\nH = assume_distinct_index(H, [(i, j)])\n\nω * σ_i₂₂ + ω * σ_j₂₂ + J * σ_i₁₂ * σ_j₂₁ + J * σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₁₂","title":"Hamiltonian"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Symmetric-and-antisymmetric-raising-operators","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Define\n\nS_pm^dagger\n  = frac1sqrt2(sigma_i^eg pm sigma_j^eg)\n\nso S_+^dagger ggrangle is the bright single-excitation state and S_-^dagger ggrangle is the dark one.  In the package we drop the 1sqrt2 to keep symbolic expressions clean; only the eigenvalues matter.\n\nS_bright = assume_distinct_index(σeg(i) + σeg(j), [(i, j)])\nS_dark = assume_distinct_index(σeg(i) - σeg(j), [(i, j)])\n\nσ_i₂₁ - σ_j₂₁","title":"Symmetric and antisymmetric raising operators"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Diagonalising-the-single-excitation-manifold","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Acting on the ground state ggrangle we have Hggrangle = 0, so HS_pm^daggerggrangle = H S_pm^daggerggrangle.  Each commutator is a single line in the package, but the raw form mixes single-excitation and two-excitation operators because sigma^ee and sigma^eg don't quite commute even at distinct sites. expand_completeness projects to the sigma^ij  (ij) neq (gg) cup 1 basis and the structure becomes clear:\n\nexpand_completeness(commutator(H, S_bright))\n\n(J + ω) * σ_i₂₁ + (J + ω) * σ_j₂₁ - 2J * σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₂₂ - 2J * σ_i₂₂ * σ_j₂₁\n\nexpand_completeness(commutator(H, S_dark))\n\n(-J + ω) * σ_i₂₁ + (J - ω) * σ_j₂₁ + 2J * σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₂₂ - 2J * σ_i₂₂ * σ_j₂₁\n\nReading off the single-excitation parts,\n\nH S_+^dagger = (omega + J)S_+^dagger\n  - 2J(sigma_i^eesigma_j^eg + sigma_i^egsigma_j^ee) qquad\nH S_-^dagger = (omega - J)S_-^dagger\n  - 2J(sigma_i^eesigma_j^eg - sigma_i^egsigma_j^ee)\n\nThe residual sigma^eesigma^eg terms vanish identically on the ground state (they require one site already excited).  Restricted to the single-excitation manifold:\n\nHS_pm^daggerggrangle = (omega pm J)S_pm^daggerggrangle\n\nThe bright state S_+^daggerggrangle sits at omega + J and carries the full collective dipole moment, so when the atoms are coupled to a common radiation continuum, this state decays at rate 2Gamma (superradiance).  The dark state S_-^daggerggrangle sits at omega - J and has zero collective dipole moment, so it is decoupled from the symmetric radiation mode and is effectively immortal (subradiance).","title":"Diagonalising the single-excitation manifold"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Number-conservation","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Total atomic excitation N_e = sigma_i^ee + sigma_j^ee commutes with H:\n\ncommutator(assume_distinct_index(σee(i) + σee(j), [(i, j)]), H)\n\n0\n\nSo the four-dimensional Hilbert space (2times 2 atoms = ggrangle egrangle gerangle eerangle) decomposes into the zero-excitation singlet, the dipole-split single-excitation doublet +rangle -rangle we just diagonalised, and the two-excitation singlet eerangle at energy 2omega.","title":"Number conservation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Numerical-verification","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"We diagonalise the 4-dimensional Hilbert space and check the spectrum directly:\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, LinearAlgebra\n\nω_val, J_val = 1.0, 0.25\nb_atom = NLevelBasis(2)\nb = b_atom ⊗ b_atom\n\n# Build numeric operators by hand (two atoms on the same NLevelSpace are\n# the simplest non-indexed scenario for to_numeric).\nσ_e_a = (transition(b_atom, 2, 2)) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_e_b = one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 2)\nσ_eg_a = transition(b_atom, 2, 1) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_ge_a = transition(b_atom, 1, 2) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_eg_b = one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 1)\nσ_ge_b = one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 1, 2)\n\nH_num = ω_val * (σ_e_a + σ_e_b) + J_val * (σ_eg_a * σ_ge_b + σ_eg_b * σ_ge_a)\nE = sort(real.(eigvals(Matrix(H_num.data))))\n\nprintln(\"spectrum             = \", round.(E; digits = 4))\nprintln(\n    \"expected {0, ω-J, ω+J, 2ω} = \",\n    round.([0.0, ω_val - J_val, ω_val + J_val, 2 * ω_val]; digits = 4)\n)\n\nspectrum             = [0.0, 0.75, 1.25, 2.0]\nexpected {0, ω-J, ω+J, 2ω} = [0.0, 0.75, 1.25, 2.0]\n\n\nThe four eigenvalues come out as 0 omega - J omega + J 2omega (the singlet, dark, bright, and doubly-excited levels), in exact agreement with the symbolic derivation.  All four sat in a four-dimensional Hilbert space derived from two indexed atoms with no physical Hilbert-space duplication: the same NLevelSpace carries both sites, with the index machinery handling site identity.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Coupling-to-a-cavity:-vacuum-Rabi-splitting","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"The bright-dark dichotomy gets its name from a physical observable: coupling to a radiation mode.  Adding a common cavity field at frequency omega_0 to the existing two-atom system,\n\nH to H + omega_0 a^dagger a\n  + ga^dagger(sigma_i^ge + sigma_j^ge)\n  + ga(sigma_i^eg + sigma_j^eg)\n\nthe atom-field coupling appears only through the collective lowering S_- = sigma_i^ge + sigma_j^ge and its hermitian conjugate.  The dark raising operator S_-^dagger is orthogonal to S_+ and the package confirms it directly: the cavity field commutes with the dark operator:\n\nhcav = FockSpace(:cavity)\nhatom = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\nhh = hcav ⊗ hatom\n@variables ω₀ g\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(hh, 1)\nic = Index(hh, :i, 2, hatom)\njc = Index(hh, :j, 2, hatom)\nσge_c(idx) = IndexedOperator(Transition(hatom, :σ, :g, :e), idx)\nσeg_c(idx) = IndexedOperator(Transition(hatom, :σ, :e, :g), idx)\n\nH_int = g * a' * (σge_c(ic) + σge_c(jc)) + g * a * (σeg_c(ic) + σeg_c(jc))\nH_int = assume_distinct_index(H_int, [(ic, jc)])\n\nS_dark_c = assume_distinct_index(σeg_c(ic) - σeg_c(jc), [(ic, jc)])\n\ncommutator(H_int, S_dark_c)\n\ng * a' * σ_i₁₁ - g * a' * σ_i₂₂ - g * a' * σ_j₁₁ + g * a' * σ_j₂₂\n\nThe output is supported only on two-excitation operators of the form sigma^eesigma^eg that annihilate the ground state.  So acting on 0_c ggrangle, H_mathrmint S_-^dagger returns zero, so the dark single-excitation state is invisible to the cavity.\n\nThe bright state, by contrast, hybridises with the cavity photon to form polaritons.  In the single-excitation basis 1_c ggrangle 0_c +rangle 0_c -rangle, the package's canonical form factorises into a 2times 2 polariton block and a decoupled dark eigenvalue.  At resonance omega_0 = omega + J the polariton block is beginpmatrixomega_0  gsqrt2 gsqrt2  omega + Jendpmatrix, giving the vacuum Rabi splitting 2gsqrt2:\n\nω₀_val, g_val = ω_val + J_val, 0.2\nb_cav = FockBasis(2)\nb_full = b_cav ⊗ b_atom ⊗ b_atom\na_num = destroy(b_cav) ⊗ one(b_atom) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_ee_a2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 2) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_ee_b2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 2)\nσ_eg_a2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 1) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_ge_a2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 1, 2) ⊗ one(b_atom)\nσ_eg_b2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 2, 1)\nσ_ge_b2 = one(b_cav) ⊗ one(b_atom) ⊗ transition(b_atom, 1, 2)\n\nH_tc = ω₀_val * a_num' * a_num + ω_val * (σ_ee_a2 + σ_ee_b2) +\n    J_val * (σ_eg_a2 * σ_ge_b2 + σ_eg_b2 * σ_ge_a2) +\n    g_val * (\n    a_num' * σ_ge_a2 + a_num' * σ_ge_b2 +\n        a_num * σ_eg_a2 + a_num * σ_eg_b2\n)\n\nE_tc = sort(real.(eigvals(Hermitian(Matrix(H_tc.data)))))\npol_lower = ω₀_val - g_val * sqrt(2)\npol_upper = ω₀_val + g_val * sqrt(2)\ndark_lvl = ω_val - J_val\nprintln(\n    \"single-excitation spectrum (numeric): \",\n    round.(E_tc[2:4]; digits = 4)\n)\nprintln(\n    \"polariton + dark prediction:           \",\n    round.(sort([pol_lower, dark_lvl, pol_upper]); digits = 4)\n)\n\nsingle-excitation spectrum (numeric): [0.75, 0.9672, 1.5328]\npolariton + dark prediction:           [0.75, 0.9672, 1.5328]\n\n\nThe three single-excitation eigenvalues fall right where the analytic story predicts: a lower polariton at omega_0 - gsqrt2, the untouched dark state at omega - J, and an upper polariton at omega_0 + gsqrt2.  Dropping a single atom from this experiment would leave only the ordinary Jaynes-Cummings g splitting; the sqrt2 is the collective enhancement.","title":"Coupling to a cavity: vacuum Rabi splitting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/two_atom/#Generalising-to-N-atoms-with-Σ","page":"Two-Atom Dipole–Dipole Interaction","text":"Stepping up to N identical atoms uses the same operator constructions, but now with a symbolic range N and explicit Σ sums.  The dipole-dipole term becomes Jsum_i neq j sigma_i^egsigma_j^ge and the cavity coupling becomes gsum_i (a^dagger sigma_i^ge + asigma_i^eg):\n\n@variables Nsym\niN = Index(hh, :i, Nsym, hatom)\njN = Index(hh, :j, Nsym, hatom)\n\nH_dd_N = J * Σ(Σ(σeg_c(iN) * σge_c(jN), jN, [iN]), iN)\nH_int_N = g * Σ(a' * σge_c(iN) + a * σeg_c(iN), iN)\n\nH_dd_N\n\nΣ(j=1:Nsym)Σ(i=1:Nsym)(i≠j) J * σ_i₂₁ * σ_j₁₂\n\nH_int_N\n\nΣ(i=1:Nsym) (g * a * σ_i₂₁ + g * a' * σ_i₁₂)\n\nThe single-excitation block decomposes exactly as for N = 2: a 2times 2 polariton sector for 1_c ggcdots grangle 0_c Brangle with bright energy omega + (N-1)J and enhanced coupling gsqrtN, plus an (N-1)-fold degenerate dark manifold at omega - J.  At resonance omega_0 = omega + (N-1)J the polariton splitting is the collective vacuum Rabi splitting 2gsqrtN, a hallmark of cavity QED with cold-atom ensembles.\n\nfunction tavis_single_excitation(N::Int; ω = 1.0, J = 0.05, g = 0.2)\n    b_c = FockBasis(2)\n    b_a = NLevelBasis(2)\n    bs = vcat([b_c], fill(b_a, N))\n    b_tot = reduce(⊗, bs)\n    op_at(idx, op_local) = reduce(\n        ⊗,\n        vcat(\n            [idx == 0 ? op_local : one(b_c)],\n            [k == idx ? op_local : one(b_a) for k in 1:N],\n        ),\n    )\n    a_n = op_at(0, destroy(b_c))\n    σee_n = [op_at(k, transition(b_a, 2, 2)) for k in 1:N]\n    σeg_n = [op_at(k, transition(b_a, 2, 1)) for k in 1:N]\n    σge_n = [op_at(k, transition(b_a, 1, 2)) for k in 1:N]\n    ω₀ = ω + (N - 1) * J\n    H_N = ω₀ * a_n' * a_n + ω * sum(σee_n) +\n        J * sum(σeg_n[p] * σge_n[q] for p in 1:N, q in 1:N if p != q) +\n        g * sum(a_n' * σge_n[k] + a_n * σeg_n[k] for k in 1:N)\n    N_tot = a_n' * a_n + sum(σee_n)\n    Hd = Hermitian(Matrix(H_N.data))\n    F = eigen(Hd)\n    Ns = real.(diag(F.vectors' * Matrix(N_tot.data) * F.vectors))\n    keep = findall(n -> abs(n - 1) < 1.0e-6, Ns)\n    return ω₀, sort(real.(F.values[keep]))\nend\n\nfor N in 2:4\n    ω₀_val_N, E_single = tavis_single_excitation(N)\n    pol_lo_th = ω₀_val_N - 0.2 * sqrt(N)\n    pol_hi_th = ω₀_val_N + 0.2 * sqrt(N)\n    dark_th = 1.0 - 0.05\n    th_sorted = sort(vcat([pol_lo_th, pol_hi_th], fill(dark_th, N - 1)))\n    println(\n        \"N = $N:  numeric single-excitation = \",\n        round.(E_single; digits = 4)\n    )\n    println(\n        \"        theory                     = \",\n        round.(th_sorted; digits = 4),\n        \"   (ε_pol± = ω_c ± g√N, \",\n        \"dark ×$(N - 1) at ω - J)\"\n    )\nend\n\nN = 2:  numeric single-excitation = [0.7672, 0.95, 1.3328]\n        theory                     = [0.7672, 0.95, 1.3328]   (ε_pol± = ω_c ± g√N, dark ×1 at ω - J)\nN = 3:  numeric single-excitation = [0.7536, 0.95, 0.95, 1.4464]\n        theory                     = [0.7536, 0.95, 0.95, 1.4464]   (ε_pol± = ω_c ± g√N, dark ×2 at ω - J)\nN = 4:  numeric single-excitation = [0.75, 0.95, 0.95, 0.95, 1.55]\n        theory                     = [0.75, 0.95, 0.95, 0.95, 1.55]   (ε_pol± = ω_c ± g√N, dark ×3 at ω - J)\n\n\nThe lower polariton tracks omega_0 - gsqrtN exactly for every N, demonstrating the collective sqrtN enhancement in a closed form.  Doubling the atom count gives roughly a sqrt2 increase in the bright-state cavity coupling, invisible to the dark manifold, which remains at omega - J regardless of how many atoms join.  All of this came from the same indexed NLevelSpace, a single \\Sigma for the atom-cavity coupling, and a doubly-nested \\Sigma (with i \\neq j) for the dipole-dipole term, with no per-atom Hilbert-space book-keeping.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Generalising to N atoms with Σ"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#Differentiable-Quantum-Optimal-Control","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"Gradient-based quantum optimal control means shaping a control pulse, evolving the state, measuring how far the final state is from a target, and descending the gradient of that error with respect to the pulse parameters. Two packages divide the work:\n\nSecondQuantizedAlgebra is the symbolic layer. You write the Hamiltonian and operators symbolically, with the control entering through a time_parameter value function (p, t) -> value of the solver parameter vector p and time t. to_numeric compiles that symbolic expression into the numeric operator (a QobjEvo) the solver consumes. Because p is threaded into its coefficients, that operator is differentiable with respect to p. No manual operator assembly is needed.\nQuantumToolbox is the numeric engine. It solves the dynamics and, through the SciMLSensitivity adjoint machinery with a reverse-mode backend such as Mooncake or Enzyme, differentiates the solve with respect to p. This is specific to the QuantumToolbox backend; the QuantumOpticsBase backend has no solver parameter vector to differentiate against.\n\nWe solve the same task twice: first as a closed system with sesolve, then, after adding photon loss, as an open system with mesolve. The task is single-photon state preparation in a Kerr resonator. The Kerr nonlinearity shifts the 1 to 2 transition out of resonance (photon blockade), so a resonant drive can pump 0 to 1 but not beyond. We optimize the drive pulse to reach the Fock state 1rangle from vacuum.","title":"Differentiable Quantum Optimal Control"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#Setup","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"The control Hamiltonian in the frame rotating at the drive frequency is\n\nH(t) = Delta a^dagger a + fracK2 a^dagger a^dagger a a\n     + varepsilon(t)(a^dagger + a)\n\nwith detuning Delta, Kerr strength K, and a real control varepsilon(t).\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nusing QuantumToolbox\nusing Symbolics\n\nusing SciMLSensitivity\nimport Mooncake                       # loads the Mooncake reverse-mode backend\nusing DifferentiationInterface: AutoMooncake\nusing Optimization, OptimizationOptimJL\n\nh = FockSpace(:c)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h)\n@variables Δ K ε κ\n\n4-element Vector{Symbolics.Num}:\n Δ\n K\n ε\n κ\n\nPhysical constants and control discretization. The pulse is a Fourier sine series, which is smooth and vanishes at both ends of the interval, and its amplitudes are the parameters we optimize.\n\nN = 10                       # Fock cutoff (Hilbert dimension N + 1)\nΔ0, K0 = 0.0, -2.0           # on resonance for 0 -> 1; Kerr blockade detunes 1 -> 2\nκ0 = 0.05                    # photon-loss rate (used only for the open-system part)\nT = 6.0                      # control duration\ntlist = collect(range(0, T, 61))\nM = 6                        # number of control parameters\n\npulse(p, t) = sum(p[k] * sinpi(k * t / T) for k in eachindex(p))","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#The-differentiable-control-Hamiltonian","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"The static part is fixed with parameter (substituted eagerly). The drive is registered through time_parameter as the two-argument (p, t) function, whose arity marks the coefficient as solver-parameter aware and makes the returned QobjEvo differentiable with respect to p. The Hamiltonian is built once and reused by both solvers; each solver call supplies the current p.\n\nHc = to_numeric(\n    Δ * a' * a + (K / 2) * a' * a' * a * a + ε * (a' + a), h, N;\n    backend = QuantumToolboxBackend(),\n    parameter = Dict(Δ => Δ0, K => K0),\n    time_parameter = Dict(ε => (p, t) -> pulse(p, t)),\n)\n\nψ0 = fock(N + 1, 0)\nproj1 = fock(N + 1, 1) * fock(N + 1, 1)'\nsensealg = BacksolveAdjoint(autojacvec = SciMLSensitivity.MooncakeVJP())\n\nOptimization.jl is the idiomatic SciML interface: wrap the cost in an OptimizationFunction carrying the AD backend, build an OptimizationProblem, and solve. For a smooth, deterministic, low-dimensional control problem with exact gradients, the quasi-Newton LBFGS converges in far fewer solver evaluations than a first-order method such as Adam. This small helper optimizes a cost from a flat initial pulse and records the loss trace.\n\nfunction optimize_pulse(cost)\n    losses = Float64[]\n    optf = OptimizationFunction((u, p) -> cost(u), AutoMooncake())\n    prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, fill(0.3, M))\n    res = solve(prob, OptimizationOptimJL.LBFGS(); maxiters = 100, callback = (s, l) -> (push!(losses, l); false))\n    return res.u, losses\nend","title":"The differentiable control Hamiltonian"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#Closed-system-(sesolve)","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"With no dissipation the state stays pure. The cost is the infidelity 1 - langle 1  psi(T) rangle^2, one minus the population in 1rangle at the final time. sesolve receives params and the adjoint chosen for correct gradients here, BacksolveAdjoint with the Mooncake vector-Jacobian product; Enzyme works too, while Zygote returns silently wrong gradients for time evolution.\n\ninfidelity_closed(params) =\n    1 - real(expect(proj1, sesolve(Hc, ψ0, tlist; params, sensealg, progress_bar = Val(false)).states[end]))\n\np_closed, losses_closed = optimize_pulse(infidelity_closed)\n1 - infidelity_closed(p_closed)   # near unity: closed dynamics conserve probability\n\n0.9999361731713867\n\nThe optimized pulse drives the mean photon number from 0 to 1, and the final population lands almost entirely in 1rangle; the Kerr blockade suppresses 2rangle.\n\nusing Plots\ngr()\n\nn_op = create(N + 1) * destroy(N + 1)\nsol_closed = sesolve(Hc, ψ0, tlist; params = p_closed, e_ops = [n_op], progress_bar = Val(false))\nnt_closed = real.(sol_closed.expect[1, :])\nεt_closed = pulse.(Ref(p_closed), tlist)\nfinalpops_closed = [real(expect(fock(N + 1, n) * fock(N + 1, n)', sol_closed.states[end])) for n in 0:4]\n\nfig_closed = plot(; layout = (1, 3), size = (1050, 320), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nplot!(fig_closed[1], 1:length(losses_closed), losses_closed; xlabel = \"iteration\", ylabel = \"infidelity 1 − F\", yscale = :log10, title = \"LBFGS convergence\", color = :crimson, label = false)\n\nplot!(fig_closed[2], tlist, nt_closed; xlabel = \"t\", ylabel = \"⟨n⟩ / ε(t)\", title = \"optimized pulse & dynamics\", label = \"⟨n⟩(t)\", color = :navy, legend = :topleft)\nplot!(fig_closed[2], tlist, εt_closed; label = \"ε(t)\", color = :darkorange, linestyle = :dash)\n\nplot!(fig_closed[3], 0:4, finalpops_closed; xlabel = \"Fock state n\", ylabel = \"population\", title = \"final state\", xticks = 0:4, color = :seagreen, label = false, seriestype = :bar)\n\nfig_closed\n\n(Image: )","title":"Closed system (sesolve)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#Open-system-(mesolve)","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"Now add photon loss through a single collapse operator c = sqrtkappa a, giving the Lindblad master equation dotrho = -iH(t) rho + kappamathcalDarho. The collapse operator is assembled the same way as the Hamiltonian, from sqrt(κ) * a.\n\nc_op = to_numeric(sqrt(κ) * a, h, N; backend = QuantumToolboxBackend(), parameter = Dict(κ => κ0))\n\nThe loss rate is a control too: writing time_parameter = Dict(κ => (p, t) -> ...) would make kappa a differentiable coefficient as well, so a tunable dissipation could be optimized alongside the drive.\n\nThe cost is now 1 - langle 1  rho(T)  1 rangle on the final density matrix. mesolve builds its Liouvillian from the time-dependent operators as a lazy superoperator sum of a single concrete type, so the adjoint solve sees no dynamic dispatch and the same sensealg applies.\n\ninfidelity_open(params) =\n    1 - real(expect(proj1, mesolve(Hc, ψ0, tlist, [c_op]; params, sensealg, progress_bar = Val(false)).states[end]))\n\np_open, losses_open = optimize_pulse(infidelity_open)\n1 - infidelity_open(p_open)   # below unity: decoherence-limited\n\n0.9065668158944359","title":"Open system (mesolve)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/differentiable_control/#Open-system-results","page":"Differentiable Quantum Control","text":"Photon loss caps the achievable fidelity below the closed-system value: the single excitation decays throughout the pulse, so the final 1rangle population sits below one and the state is left mixed.\n\nsol_open = mesolve(Hc, ψ0, tlist, [c_op]; params = p_open, e_ops = [n_op], progress_bar = Val(false))\nnt_open = real.(sol_open.expect[1, :])\nεt_open = pulse.(Ref(p_open), tlist)\nfinalpops_open = [real(expect(fock(N + 1, n) * fock(N + 1, n)', sol_open.states[end])) for n in 0:4]\n\nfig_open = plot(; layout = (1, 3), size = (1050, 320), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nplot!(fig_open[1], 1:length(losses_open), losses_open; xlabel = \"iteration\", ylabel = \"infidelity 1 − F\", yscale = :log10, title = \"LBFGS convergence\", color = :crimson, label = false)\n\nplot!(fig_open[2], tlist, nt_open; xlabel = \"t\", ylabel = \"⟨n⟩ / ε(t)\", title = \"optimized pulse & dynamics\", label = \"⟨n⟩(t)\", color = :navy, legend = :topleft)\nplot!(fig_open[2], tlist, εt_open; label = \"ε(t)\", color = :darkorange, linestyle = :dash)\n\nplot!(fig_open[3], 0:4, finalpops_open; xlabel = \"Fock state n\", ylabel = \"population\", title = \"final state\", xticks = 0:4, color = :seagreen, label = false, seriestype = :bar)\n\nfig_open\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Open-system results"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/extending/#Extending-the-package","page":"Extending the package","text":"The package has two extension points. A new operator kind is added inside the package, since every operator is one concrete struct. A numeric backend is added from outside, by implementing a small interface.\n\nPages = [\"extending.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Extending the package"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/extending/#Adding-an-operator-kind","page":"Extending the package","text":"Every operator is an Op, and its role is the kind field of type OpKind. The algebra never inspects an operator directly. It talks to operators through six functions in src/operators/operators.jl, each a single method on (::Op, ::Op) that branches on kind:\n\nFunction Returns Purpose\nsite_compare(a, b, ne) Less, Equal, Undetermined or Greater site relation of two operators, which drives the sort\ncan_commute(a, b) Bool whether a same-site pair may swap without a residual\ncommute_pair(a, b) (c1, r1, c2, r2, n) the residual ab - ba = c_1 r_1 + c_2 r_2, where each r is one operator for n = 1 and the identity for n = 0\nmay_reduce(a, b) Bool whether reduce_pair can do anything, a cheap pre-check\nreduce_pair(a, b) (kind, op, c) a local identity: the pair becomes one operator, a number, or stays\nground_state_expand(op) (is_gs, g, n, site) the completeness data of a ground-state projector\n\nTwo residual slots exist because a collective commutator S^ij S^kl = delta_jk S^il - delta_li S^kj has two terms; the other kinds fill only the first.\n\nA new kind touches these places:\n\nThe kind. An OP_* member of OpKind in src/operators/op.jl, an is_* predicate, and an export.\nThe constructor. A function in the file of its Hilbert space that validates the arguments and returns an Op. Levels or axes go in the shared fields l1, l2, g, nlev; a kind that needs none leaves them zero. Write the constructor for the single space only; its ProductSpace methods are one-liners through on_factor, which checks the factor and moves the operator onto it.\nThe algebra. A branch in each function of the table that the kind needs. Kinds on one space that interact, like annihilation and creation, form a family in site_family.\nAdjoint. A branch of adjoint in src/operators/operators.jl. order_key reads the shared fields and needs no change, as long as distinct operators of the kind differ in those fields.\nNumerics. A branch of numeric_operator in every backend under ext/, or a deliberate ArgumentError where a backend has no representation.\nPrinting. The Unicode printer in src/printing/printing.jl and the LaTeX recipe in src/printing/latexify_recipes.jl.\nTests. Construction in test/fundamentals/, the algebra rules in test/algebra/, the conversion in test/numeric/, and the output in test/presentation/. The order_key contract test in test/fundamentals/ordering_test.jl loops over a list of operators, which needs one of the new kind.\n\nAn extra payload field on Op, for instance a Union{Nothing, …}, is not an option: Op must stay a concrete bits type, which the quality tests enforce.","title":"Adding an operator kind"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/extending/#Adding-a-numeric-backend","page":"Extending the package","text":"The numeric core in src/numeric/ converts an expression into a list of terms, each a ComplexF64 coefficient with a list of operator factors, and hands them to a backend. A backend is a singleton subtype of NumericBackend with methods for the following functions:\n\nFunction Responsibility\nnumeric_basis(be, h, dims) build the full basis from a Hilbert space and truncations\nnumeric_num_subsystems(be, basis) number of tensor factors\nnumeric_subbasis(be, basis, slot) one tensor factor\nnumeric_operator(be, op, subbasis) the matrix of one operator\nnumeric_embed(be, basis, slot, leaf) a factor placed in the full space\nnumeric_identity(be, basis) the identity on the full space\nnumeric_assemble(be, basis, terms) the sum of the terms, possibly lazy\nnumeric_materialize(be, assembled, op_type) the representation returned to the user\n\nnumeric_materialize gives op_type a fixed meaning: EagerOperator() returns the backend's ordinary operator, identity returns the assembled object unchanged, and any other function is applied to it. Materialization happens once, after assembly, so a single operator, a product and a sum come out in the same representation.\n\nA minimal backend:\n\nimport SecondQuantizedAlgebra as SQA\n\nstruct MyBackend <: SQA.NumericBackend end\n\nSQA.numeric_basis(::MyBackend, h::SQA.HilbertSpace, dims) = error(\"build the basis\")\nSQA.numeric_num_subsystems(::MyBackend, basis) = error(\"count subsystems\")\nSQA.numeric_subbasis(::MyBackend, basis, slot::Int) = error(\"select a subsystem\")\nSQA.numeric_operator(::MyBackend, op::SQA.Op, subbasis) = error(\"construct a leaf\")\nSQA.numeric_embed(::MyBackend, basis, slot::Int, leaf) = error(\"embed a leaf\")\nSQA.numeric_identity(::MyBackend, basis) = error(\"construct the identity\")\nSQA.numeric_assemble(::MyBackend, basis, terms) = error(\"assemble terms\")\nSQA.numeric_materialize(::MyBackend, assembled, ::SQA.EagerOperator) =\n    error(\"make an eager operator\")\nSQA.numeric_materialize(::MyBackend, assembled, ::typeof(identity)) = assembled\n\nIt is used as to_numeric(H, h, dims; backend = MyBackend()); only the two bundled backends are found automatically. Further features need further methods:\n\nFeature Additional methods\ntime-dependent parameters numeric_assemble_td\nconversion with a state in place of a basis numeric_backend(state), numeric_basis(state)\nnumeric_average and expect the state methods and numeric_expect\nsuperoperators and to_numeric_master numeric_superoperator, numeric_matrix, and numeric_scaled_td for time-dependent collapse operators\n\nTwo rules keep a backend fast and inferable. The type of the assembled object must not depend on the number of terms, so a vector-backed lazy sum is used rather than a tuple. And a time-dependent coefficient that reads the solver parameters is a two-argument function (p, t) -> value, recognized by its arity rather than by a wrapper type. The two bundled extensions under ext/ are complete examples.","title":"Adding a numeric backend"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NumericBackend","page":"Extending the package","text":"NumericBackend\n\nAbstract supertype for numeric-conversion backends.\n\nA backend is normally a concrete singleton whose methods implement the numeric hooks. See Adding a numeric backend for the interface.\n\nBundled implementations are selected with QuantumOpticsBackend or QuantumToolboxBackend after loading the corresponding package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NumericBackend"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_basis","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_basis(be, h::HilbertSpace, dims)\n\nBuild the full numeric basis for Hilbert space h with user dimensions dims (Fock cutoff / spin number; ignored for spaces that carry their own level count). Returns a Basis (QuantumOptics) or integer dims (QuantumToolbox).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_basis"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_num_subsystems","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_num_subsystems(be, basis) -> Int\n\nNumber of tensor-product subsystems represented by basis. Backends use this hook to validate indexed-site layouts before emitting any numeric operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_num_subsystems"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_subbasis","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_subbasis(be, basis, slot::Int)\n\nThe subsystem basis for slot of a composite basis (the basis itself for a simple one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_subbasis"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_operator","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_operator(be, op::Op, subbasis)\n\nMap a single-site operator op to its numeric matrix on subbasis (the subsystem basis for op's slot). Backends implement it as a closed k === OP_* value-branch on op.kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_operator"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_embed","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_embed(be, basis, slot::Int, leaf)\n\nPlace the single-site numeric operator leaf at slot of basis, returning an operator on the full space (the leaf unchanged for a simple basis, a lazy/concrete tensor for a composite one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_embed"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_identity","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_identity(be, basis)\n\nThe identity operator on basis (lazy on a composite basis).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_identity"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_assemble","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_assemble(be, basis, terms)\n\nAssemble a static lazy operator from a backend-neutral terms list. Each entry is (c::ComplexF64, factors::Vector{<leaf>}) with factors already embedded at their slots. The result is one concrete vector-backed lazy type for any term count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_assemble"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_assemble_td","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_assemble_td(be, basis, td_terms)\n\nTime-dependent counterpart of numeric_assemble.\n\nEach coefficient is Union{ComplexF64, Function}: a constant, a t -> ComplexF64, or a solver-parameter-aware (p, t) -> ComplexF64. The result is the backend's native time-dependent operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_assemble_td"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_materialize","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_materialize(be, op, op_type)\n\nMaterialize a static lazily assembled operator op according to op_type, applied exactly once at the public boundary. Implementations must treat EagerOperator as a request for their ordinary eager operator and identity as a request for op unchanged. Other callables are backend-defined. The eager representation need not be sparse, although both bundled backends choose sparse storage.\n\nThis hook is not used by numeric_average, which consumes the lazy assembly, or by time-dependent conversion, which returns the backend's native time-dependent operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_materialize"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.EagerOperator","page":"Extending the package","text":"EagerOperator()\n\nThe default op_type: request the backend's ordinary eager operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.EagerOperator"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_expect","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_expect(be, numop, state)\n\nExpectation value ⟨state| numop |state⟩ (a ComplexF64), or a Vector{ComplexF64} when state is a vector of states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_expect"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_backend","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_backend(state) -> NumericBackend\n\nReturn the numeric backend owning state. Third-party backends must implement this hook for their state types to support numeric_average and state-based to_numeric.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_superoperator","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_superoperator(be, ctx::NumericContext, terms::Vector{Tuple{ComplexF64, Vector{Op}, Vector{Op}}})\n\nBuild the backend's eager superoperator Σ c * sprepost(L, R) from sandwich terms. Each side is converted with the ordinary operator path of ctx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_superoperator"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_matrix","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_matrix(be, op) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nThe matrix of an eager backend operator, used for the Hermiticity check of to_numeric_master.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_scaled_td","page":"Extending the package","text":"numeric_scaled_td(be, op, f)\n\nWrap the static backend operator op with the time-dependent scalar coefficient f, a t -> value or (p, t) -> value closure. Only backends with time-dependent collapse operators implement it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_scaled_td"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/extending/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NumericContext","page":"Extending the package","text":"NumericContext(backend, basis, op_subs, scalar_subs, sites)\n\nSettings of one numeric conversion, passed through the numeric core.\n\nIt holds the backend singleton, the opaque per-backend basis, the operator-substitution dict op_subs, the indexed scalar_subs, and the indexed sites map (empty for the non-indexed path). All fields are concrete via the type parameters, so the context never widens inference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NumericContext"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Hilbert-spaces-and-operators","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"A model starts from the Hilbert space of each subsystem. The type of the space fixes which operators live on it and which algebraic relations they obey:\n\nSubsystem Space Operators Defining relation\nBosonic mode FockSpace Destroy, Create a a^dagger = 1\nFinite-level system NLevelSpace Transition sigma^ijsigma^kl = delta_jksigma^il\nQubit in Pauli form PauliSpace Pauli sigma_jsigma_k = delta_jk + iepsilon_jklsigma_l\nAngular momentum SpinSpace Spin S_j S_k = iepsilon_jklS_l\nCanonical quadratures PhaseSpace Position, Momentum x p = i\n\nThe examples below illustrate these spaces with a cavity mode, a two-level atom, a spin, and the position and momentum of a mechanical mirror. We combine them in one product space to show how operators address individual subsystems. Choose the spaces needed for your own model; no coupling between subsystems is implied by the product space.\n\nPages = [\"spaces_and_operators.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Hilbert spaces and operators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Hilbert-spaces","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Each subsystem gets its own space, labelled by a name. The atom lists its levels by name, which then serve to address its transitions.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nha = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\nhs = SpinSpace(:spin)\nhm = PhaseSpace(:mirror)\nhc, ha, hs, hm\n(ℋ(cavity), ℋ(atom), ℋ(spin), ℋ(mirror))\n\nAll of them share the supertype HilbertSpace.","title":"Hilbert spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Composite-systems","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Subsystems are joined with the tensor product. Its factors are numbered from left to right, and this number is how an operator refers to the subsystem it acts on: here the cavity is factor 1 and the mirror factor 4.\n\nh = hc ⊗ ha ⊗ hs ⊗ hm\nℋ(cavity) ⊗ ℋ(atom) ⊗ ℋ(spin) ⊗ ℋ(mirror)","title":"Composite systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Operators","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"An operator is built from its space, a name, and whatever else fixes it: two levels for a transition, an axis for Pauli and spin operators. The name belongs to the identity of the operator, so two modes with different names are independent. Writing every name twice, once for the variable and once for the operator, is avoided with @qnumbers.\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) x::Position(h, 4) p::Momentum(h, 4)\na, x, p\n(a, x, p)","title":"Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Bosonic-modes","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"A mode is described by its annihilation operator. The creation operator follows as its adjoint, so it rarely needs to be constructed on its own.\n\na' == Create(h, :a, 1)\ntrue","title":"Bosonic modes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Finite-level-systems","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Atoms, qudits and other discrete systems are written in terms of transitions between their levels, addressed by the level names. Projectors are the special case of equal levels.\n\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\nσ(:g, :e), σ(:e, :e)\n(σ₁₂, σ₂₂)\n\nA transition stores its levels as numbers, in the order the space names them, together with the level count and the ground level.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: n_levels, ground_state, transition_levels\ntransition_levels(σ(:g, :e)), n_levels(σ(:g, :e)), ground_state(σ(:g, :e))\n((1, 2), 2, 1)","title":"Finite-level systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Pauli-and-spin-operators","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"A two-level system can equally be written with Pauli matrices, which reduce every product to a single matrix or a number.\n\nτ(k) = Pauli(PauliSpace(:qubit), :τ, k)\nτ(1) * τ(1), τ(1) * τ(2)\n(1, im * τz)\n\nSpin operators extend this to angular momenta of unspecified size, such as the collective spin of the model.\n\nS(k) = Spin(h, :S, k, 3)\nS(1), S(2), S(3)\n(Sx, Sy, Sz)\n\nThe axis of a Pauli or spin operator is read back as 1, 2 or 3.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: operator_axis\noperator_axis(τ(2)), operator_axis(S(3))\n(2, 3)","title":"Pauli and spin operators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Quadratures","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"A mode can also be described by its position and momentum quadratures instead of ladder operators, as the mirror is here.\n\np * x\n-im + x * p","title":"Quadratures"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Inspecting-operators","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Every operator has the same type, so its kind is read with a function rather than a type check. Methods for any operator expression are written for QField, and for single operators for QSym.\n\noptype(a'), is_create(a'), is_transition(σ(:g, :e))\n(SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OP_CREATE, true, true)\n\nThe name, the site index and the subsystems of an operator or an expression are read off directly. The coupling between the cavity and the atom acts on factors 1 and 2.\n\noperator_name(σ(:g, :e)), acts_on(a' * σ(:g, :e))\n(:σ, [1, 2])","title":"Inspecting operators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Adjoints","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"The adjoint ' of an expression conjugates its coefficients along with the operators. dagger is the same under the name used by QuantumOptics.jl.\n\n@variables g::Number\ndagger(g * a' * σ(:g, :e))\nconj(g) * a * σ₂₁\n\nqadjoint applies to coefficients as well. Unlike conj, it takes the conjugate through to the individual parameters.\n\n@variables θ\nqadjoint(g^2 * cos(θ)), conj(g^2 * cos(θ))\n((conj(g)^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)*cos(θ), conj(cos(θ)*(g^\u001b[34m2\u001b[39m)))","title":"Adjoints"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#Printing-and-LaTeX","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Expressions print with Unicode subscripts in the REPL. With Latexify.jl loaded, latexify turns any expression into LaTeX.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra, Latexify\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) σ::Transition(h, 2, 1, 2)\n@variables g\n\nlatexify(g * (a' * σ + σ' * a))\nL\"$g a{\\sigma}^{{12}} + g a^{\\dagger}{\\sigma}^{{21}}$\"","title":"Printing and LaTeX"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.HilbertSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"HilbertSpace\n\nSupertype of all Hilbert spaces.\n\nThe concrete spaces are FockSpace, NLevelSpace, CollectiveNLevelSpace, PauliSpace, SpinSpace, PhaseSpace and their tensor product ProductSpace. The type is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2) isa SecondQuantizedAlgebra.HilbertSpace\ntrue\n\nSee also ProductSpace, ⊗.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.HilbertSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.FockSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"FockSpace(name::Symbol) <: HilbertSpace\n\nHilbert space of a bosonic mode, home of the ladder operators Destroy and Create. The name is a label used for printing.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity)\nℋ(cavity)\n\njulia> a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> a * a'\n1 + a' * a\n\nSee also Destroy, PhaseSpace, ⊗.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.FockSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NLevelSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"NLevelSpace(name::Symbol, n::Int)\nNLevelSpace(name::Symbol, n::Int, ground_state::Int)\nNLevelSpace(name::Symbol, levels::Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}})\n\nHilbert space of a system with n discrete levels, home of the Transition operators.\n\nLevels are numbered 1:n, or named by a tuple of symbols in the order of the basis. The ground_state (default 1; the first name for named levels) marks the projector granglelangle g that expand_completeness can eliminate through granglelangle g = 1 - sum_k neq gkranglelangle k.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\nℋ(atom)\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, :g, :e);\n\njulia> σ' * σ\nσ₂₂\n\njulia> h3 = NLevelSpace(:atom, 3, 2);\n\njulia> expand_completeness(Transition(h3, :σ, 2, 2))\n1 - σ₁₁ - σ₃₃\n\nSee also Transition, expand_completeness, PauliSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.NLevelSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PauliSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"PauliSpace(name::Symbol) <: HilbertSpace\n\nHilbert space of a two-level system written with Pauli matrices, home of the Pauli operators.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = PauliSpace(:qubit)\nℋ(qubit)\n\njulia> σz = Pauli(h, :σ, 3);\n\njulia> σz * σz\n1\n\nSee also Pauli, NLevelSpace, SpinSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PauliSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SpinSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"SpinSpace(name::Symbol) <: HilbertSpace\n\nHilbert space of an angular momentum, home of the Spin operators.\n\nThe spin length S does not enter the algebra. It is fixed only by the basis chosen for numerics, so the same expressions hold for every S.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = SpinSpace(:S)\nℋ(S)\n\njulia> Sz = Spin(h, :S, 3)\nSz\n\nSee also Spin, PauliSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SpinSpace"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PhaseSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"PhaseSpace(name::Symbol) <: HilbertSpace\n\nHilbert space of one mode in quadrature form, home of Position and Momentum.\n\nA PhaseSpace holds a single canonical pair. The quadrature names are only labels: a second Position under another name is the same observable. Several modes therefore need several spaces combined with ⊗.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = PhaseSpace(:osc)\nℋ(osc)\n\njulia> x = Position(h, :x); y = Position(h, :y); p = Momentum(h, :p);\n\njulia> commutator(x, p), commutator(y, p)\n(im, im)\n\nSee also Position, Momentum, FockSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PhaseSpace"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#TensorCore.:⊗-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.HilbertSpace, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.HilbertSpace}","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"⊗(space::HilbertSpace, spaces::HilbertSpace...)\n\nTensor product of Hilbert spaces, returning a ProductSpace.\n\nNested products are flattened, so (A ⊗ B) ⊗ C == A ⊗ (B ⊗ C) and the factors are always numbered left to right. Type it as \\otimes<tab>, or use the ASCII alias tensor.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> A = FockSpace(:a); B = FockSpace(:b); C = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> A ⊗ B ⊗ C\nℋ(a) ⊗ ℋ(b) ⊗ ℋ(atom)\n\njulia> (A ⊗ B) ⊗ C == A ⊗ (B ⊗ C)\ntrue\n\nSee also ProductSpace, tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TensorCore.:⊗"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#TensorCore.tensor","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"tensor(space::HilbertSpace, spaces::HilbertSpace...)\n\nASCII alias for ⊗, the tensor product of Hilbert spaces.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> tensor(FockSpace(:a), FockSpace(:b))\nℋ(a) ⊗ ℋ(b)\n\nSee also ⊗, ProductSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TensorCore.tensor"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ProductSpace","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"ProductSpace{T} <: HilbertSpace\n\nTensor product of Hilbert spaces, built with ⊗ or tensor.\n\nThe factors are numbered 1, 2, … from left to right. An operator on a product space names the factor it acts on by this number, e.g. Destroy(h, :a, 1). The number may be left out when only one factor has the right type; otherwise the constructor throws an ArgumentError.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2)\nℋ(cavity) ⊗ ℋ(atom)\n\njulia> a = Destroy(h, :a, 1); σ = Transition(h, :σ, 1, 2);\n\njulia> a * σ\na * σ₁₂\n\nSee also ⊗, acts_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ProductSpace"},{"category":"macro","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.@qnumbers","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"@qnumbers ops...\n\nDefine operators and bind each to a variable of the same name.\n\nEach argument reads name::Constructor(space, args...) and stands for name = Constructor(space, :name, args...), so the operator name is written once. Several operators can be declared in one call.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) σ::Transition(h, 1, 2, 2)\n(a, σ₁₂)\n\njulia> a' * σ\na' * σ₁₂\n\nSee also Destroy, Transition, Pauli, Spin, Position, Momentum, CollectiveTransition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.@qnumbers"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Destroy","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Destroy(h::FockSpace, name::Symbol)\nDestroy(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, [subsystem::Int])\n\nAnnihilation operator of the bosonic mode name.\n\nThe operator a obeys a a^dagger = 1, and products come out normal ordered. The adjoint a' is the Create operator. Modes with different names are independent. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the FockSpace factor and may be omitted when there is only one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> a * a'\n1 + a' * a\n\nSee also Create, FockSpace, @qnumbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Destroy"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Create","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Create(h::FockSpace, name::Symbol)\nCreate(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, [subsystem::Int])\n\nCreation operator of the bosonic mode name.\n\nThe operator a^dagger is the adjoint of Destroy, and usually written as a' instead. Arguments are as for Destroy.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity);\n\njulia> a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> Create(h, :a) == a'\ntrue\n\nSee also Destroy, FockSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Create"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Transition","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Transition(h::NLevelSpace, name::Symbol, i, j)\nTransition(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, i, j, [subsystem::Int])\n\nTransition operator from level j to level i.\n\nThe operator is sigma^ij = iranglelangle j. The levels are integers or the symbolic names of the NLevelSpace; either way it prints with level numbers. Transitions on the same system compose, sigma^ijsigma^kl = delta_jksigma^il, and the adjoint swaps the levels. The ground-state projector sigma^gg is kept as it is; call expand_completeness to rewrite it in terms of the other projectors. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the factor and may be omitted when only one NLevelSpace is present.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, 1, 2);\n\njulia> σ * σ', σ' * σ, σ * σ\n(σ₁₁, σ₂₂, 0)\n\nSee also NLevelSpace, expand_completeness, @qnumbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Transition"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transition_levels","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"transition_levels(o::Op) -> Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nThe levels (i, j) of a Transition iranglelangle j. Throws an ArgumentError for any other operator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: transition_levels\n\njulia> transition_levels(Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 3), :σ, 3, 1))\n(3, 1)\n\nSee also n_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transition_levels"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.n_levels","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"n_levels(o::Op) -> Int\nground_state(o::Op) -> Int\n\nThe number of levels and the ground level of the NLevelSpace a Transition acts on. Throws an ArgumentError for any other operator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: n_levels, ground_state\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 3), :σ, 3, 1);\n\njulia> n_levels(σ), ground_state(σ)\n(3, 1)\n\nSee also transition_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.n_levels"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ground_state","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"n_levels(o::Op) -> Int\nground_state(o::Op) -> Int\n\nThe number of levels and the ground level of the NLevelSpace a Transition acts on. Throws an ArgumentError for any other operator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: n_levels, ground_state\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 3), :σ, 3, 1);\n\njulia> n_levels(σ), ground_state(σ)\n(3, 1)\n\nSee also transition_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ground_state"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Pauli","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Pauli(h::PauliSpace, name::Symbol, axis::Int)\nPauli(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, axis::Int, [subsystem::Int])\n\nPauli matrix along axis, where 1, 2, 3 stand for x, y, z.\n\nPauli operators are Hermitian and multiply as sigma_j sigma_k = delta_jk + i epsilon_jkl sigma_l, so every product on one qubit reduces to a single Pauli matrix or a number. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the PauliSpace factor and may be omitted when there is only one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = PauliSpace(:s);\n\njulia> σx = Pauli(h, :σ, 1); σy = Pauli(h, :σ, 2);\n\njulia> σx * σy\nim * σz\n\njulia> σx * σx\n1\n\nSee also PauliSpace, Spin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Pauli"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Spin","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Spin(h::SpinSpace, name::Symbol, axis::Int)\nSpin(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, axis::Int, [subsystem::Int])\n\nAngular momentum component along axis, where 1, 2, 3 stand for x, y, z.\n\nSpin operators are Hermitian and obey S_j S_k = i epsilon_jkl S_l. Products are ordered by axis, x before y before z. Squares such as S_x^2 stay as they are, since the spin length is not fixed. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the SpinSpace factor and may be omitted when there is only one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = SpinSpace(:S);\n\njulia> Sx = Spin(h, :S, 1); Sy = Spin(h, :S, 2);\n\njulia> Sy * Sx\n-im * Sz + Sx * Sy\n\nSee also SpinSpace, Pauli.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Spin"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_axis","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"operator_axis(o::Op) -> Int\n\nThe axis 1, 2 or 3 of a Pauli or Spin operator. Throws an ArgumentError for any other operator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: operator_axis\n\njulia> operator_axis(Pauli(PauliSpace(:q), :σ, 2))\n2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_axis"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Position","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Position(h::PhaseSpace, name::Symbol)\nPosition(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, [subsystem::Int])\n\nPosition quadrature of a mode.\n\nThe operator x = (a + a^dagger)sqrt2 is Hermitian and canonically conjugate to Momentum, x p = i. Products are ordered with position to the left of momentum. On a ProductSpace, subsystem selects the PhaseSpace factor and may be omitted when there is only one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = PhaseSpace(:osc);\n\njulia> @qnumbers x::Position(h) p::Momentum(h);\n\njulia> commutator(x, p)\nim\n\nSee also Momentum, PhaseSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Position"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Momentum","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Momentum(h::PhaseSpace, name::Symbol)\nMomentum(h::ProductSpace, name::Symbol, [subsystem::Int])\n\nMomentum quadrature of a mode.\n\nThe operator p = i(a^dagger - a)sqrt2 is Hermitian and canonically conjugate to Position. Arguments are as for Position.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = PhaseSpace(:osc);\n\njulia> @qnumbers x::Position(h) p::Momentum(h);\n\njulia> p' == p\ntrue\n\njulia> p * x\n-im + x * p\n\nSee also Position, PhaseSpace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Momentum"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"QField\n\nSupertype of every operator expression.\n\nOperators (QSym), their polynomials (QAdd) and expressions with formal functions (QExpr) are all QFields. They support +, -, *, / by numbers, integer powers, adjoint, and comparison with == and isequal. A method written for QField accepts any of them.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> (a, a' * a, cos(a + a')) isa NTuple{3, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}\ntrue\n\nSee also QSym, QAdd, QExpr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QSym","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"QSym <: QField\n\nSupertype of single operators.\n\nEvery operator, whatever its kind, is an Op, the only subtype; its kind is read with optype.\n\nSee also QField, Op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QSym"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Op","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"Op <: QSym\n\nType of every single operator, whatever its kind.\n\nOperators are made with Destroy, Create, Transition, CollectiveTransition, Pauli, Spin, Position and Momentum, never with Op directly. Since all of them share this type, use optype or the predicates such as is_destroy to tell them apart instead of isa.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> a isa Op, is_destroy(a), is_create(a')\n(true, true, true)\n\nSee also optype, operator_name, acts_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Op"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.optype","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"optype(o::Op) -> OpKind\n\nKind of the operator o.\n\nThe result is an OpKind constant, public but not exported, so write it qualified, e.g. SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OP_DESTROY. For a single check the predicates such as is_destroy read better.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 2), :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> optype(σ) == SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OP_TRANSITION\ntrue\n\nSee also Op, is_destroy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.optype"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OpKind","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"OpKind\n\nKind of an operator, as returned by optype.\n\nThe values are OP_DESTROY, OP_CREATE, OP_TRANSITION, OP_COLLECTIVE_TRANSITION, OP_PAULI, OP_SPIN, OP_POSITION and OP_MOMENTUM. They are public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> optype(Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a)') === SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OP_CREATE\ntrue\n\nSee also optype, is_destroy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.OpKind"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_destroy","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_destroy(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is an annihilation operator, see Destroy.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> is_destroy(a), is_destroy(a')\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_create, is_transition, is_collective_transition, is_pauli, is_spin, is_position, is_momentum, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_destroy"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_create","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_create(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a creation operator, see Create.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> is_create(a'), is_create(a)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_create"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_transition","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_transition(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a transition operator, see Transition.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 2), :σ, 1, 2);\n\njulia> is_transition(σ)\ntrue\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_transition"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_collective_transition","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_collective_transition(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a collective transition operator, see CollectiveTransition.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> S = CollectiveTransition(CollectiveNLevelSpace(:atoms, 2), :S, 1, 2);\n\njulia> is_collective_transition(S), is_transition(S)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_collective_transition"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_pauli","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_pauli(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a Pauli operator, see Pauli.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> σx = Pauli(PauliSpace(:qubit), :σ, 1);\n\njulia> is_pauli(σx)\ntrue\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_pauli"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_spin","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_spin(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a spin operator, see Spin.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> Sx = Spin(SpinSpace(:S), :S, 1);\n\njulia> is_spin(Sx)\ntrue\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_spin"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_position","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_position(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a position quadrature, see Position.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> x = Position(PhaseSpace(:osc), :x);\n\njulia> is_position(x), is_momentum(x)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_position"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_momentum","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"is_momentum(o::Op) -> Bool\n\nWhether o is a momentum quadrature, see Momentum.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> p = Momentum(PhaseSpace(:osc), :p);\n\njulia> is_momentum(p)\ntrue\n\nSee also is_destroy, optype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_momentum"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_name","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"operator_name(op::Op) -> Symbol\n\nName of the operator op, as given at construction.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> operator_name(a), operator_name(a')\n(:a, :a)\n\nSee also operator_index, acts_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_name"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_index","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"operator_index(o::Op) -> Index\n\nSite Index of the operator o. An operator without an index returns a placeholder for which has_index is false.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @variables N;\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, N, h);\n\njulia> σ = Transition(h, :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> operator_index(IndexedOperator(σ, i)) == i, has_index(operator_index(σ))\n(true, false)\n\nSee also IndexedOperator, operator_name, acts_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.operator_index"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.acts_on","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"acts_on(expr) -> Vector{Int}\nacts_on(i::Index) -> Vector{Int}\n\nSubsystems that expr acts on, as sorted positions in its ProductSpace.\n\nWorks on operators, operator expressions and their averages. A number acts on nothing and returns an empty vector. For an Index it returns the subsystem the index runs over.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:a) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:b, 2);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) σ::Transition(h, 1, 2, 2);\n\njulia> acts_on(a' * a), acts_on(a' * σ)\n([1], [1, 2])\n\nSee also operator_name, operator_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.acts_on"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.set_acts_on","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"set_acts_on(op, subsystem::Int)\n\nCopy of the operator op acting on another factor of a product space.\n\nName, kind, levels and index are kept. This reuses an operator built on a single space inside a product space, or moves it to another factor. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: set_acts_on\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 2), :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> Destroy(h, :a, 1) * set_acts_on(σ, 2)\na * σ₂₁\n\nSee also acts_on, rename.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.set_acts_on"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#QuantumInterface.dagger-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"dagger(x::QField)\n\nHermitian conjugate of an operator expression, the same as x'.\n\nCoefficients are conjugated along with the operators. The name matches dagger from QuantumOptics.jl, so code can use it for symbolic and numeric operators alike.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) σ::Transition(h, 1, 2, 2);\n\njulia> dagger(2im * a' * σ)\n-2im * a * σ₂₁\n\nSee also qadjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumInterface.dagger"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.qadjoint","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"qadjoint(x)\nqconj(x)\n\nHermitian conjugate of an operator expression or a coefficient.\n\nFor an operator expression this is x'. For a coefficient the conjugate is taken through the expression tree, so that it reaches the individual parameters instead of wrapping the whole expression in conj. An average becomes the average of the adjoint, as with inner_adjoint.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables g::Number;\n\njulia> qadjoint(2im * a), qadjoint(g)\n(-2im * a', conj(g))\n\nSee also dagger, inner_adjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.qadjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/spaces_and_operators/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transition_superscript","page":"Hilbert spaces and operators","text":"transition_superscript(x::Bool)\n\nChoose whether transition levels are written as superscripts or subscripts in LaTeX.\n\nWith true, the default, sigma^ij is written; with false, sigma_ij. The setting is global and applies to every later call of latexify. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: transition_superscript\n\njulia> using Latexify\n\njulia> σ = Transition(NLevelSpace(:atom, 2), :σ, 2, 1);\n\njulia> transition_superscript(false); latexify(σ)\nL\"${\\sigma}_{{21}}$\"\n\njulia> transition_superscript(true); latexify(σ)\nL\"${\\sigma}^{{21}}$\"\n\nSee also Transition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.transition_superscript"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Cavity-Optomechanics-and-the-Polaron-Transformation","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"A single optical cavity coupled to a mechanical oscillator by radiation pressure has the Hamiltonian\n\nH = omega_0 a^dagger a + omega_m b^dagger b\n  + g a^dagger a(b + b^dagger)\n\nwhere a a^dagger are cavity (optical) ladder operators, b b^dagger the mechanical mode, and g the single-photon optomechanical coupling. Although H is non-linear, it has a hidden structure: the cavity photon number a^dagger a commutes with everything in sight, so each photon-number sector sees the mechanical mode as a displaced harmonic oscillator.  The polaron (Lang-Firsov) transformation makes this exact and yields an effective Kerr nonlinearity for the cavity.\n\nThe unitary that does the job is\n\nU = explefttfracgomega_ma^dagger a(b - b^dagger)right\n\nBecause a^dagger a commutes with the displacement generator, U^dagger b U = b - (gomega_m)a^dagger a is exact, since the Hadamard series truncates at first order.  We exploit that by simply substituting the displaced operators into H and letting the package's eager normal-ordering do the bookkeeping.","title":"Cavity Optomechanics and the Polaron Transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Setup","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"using SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nhm = FockSpace(:mech)\nh = hc ⊗ hm\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\n@qnumbers b::Destroy(h, 2)\n\n@variables ω₀ ωₘ g\n\nH = ω₀ * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b + g * a' * a * (b + b')\n\nω₀ * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b + g * a' * a * b + g * a' * a * b'","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Heisenberg-dynamics:-the-radiation-pressure-force","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"The mechanical mode feels a force -ga^dagger a from the optical field, while the optical mode is purely phase-modulated by mechanical position:\n\n-1im * commutator(b, H)\n\n(-ωₘ)im * b + (-g)im * a' * a\n\n-1im * commutator(a, H)\n\n(-ω₀)im * a + (-g)im * a * b + (-g)im * a * b'\n\nReading off,\n\ndot b = -iomega_m b - i g a^dagger a qquad\ndot a = -iomega_0 a - i g a(b + b^dagger)\n\nThe first equation makes the displacement structure explicit: at fixed a^dagger a = n, the mechanical mode oscillates around a shifted origin langle brangle_texteq = -(gomega_m)n.","title":"Heisenberg dynamics: the radiation-pressure force"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Polaron-transformation-by-operator-substitution","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"A unitary transformation acts on operators by conjugation: tilde O = U^dagger O U.  Here that means\n\ntilde a = a qquad\ntilde b = b - tfracgomega_ma^dagger a\nqquad\ntilde b^dagger = b^dagger - tfracgomega_ma^dagger a\n\nwhere the first identity holds because a^dagger a is the generator of the transformation and commutes with a up to scalars that cancel (cf. the Hadamard series).  Substitute these into H and the package canonicalises the result:\n\nα = g / ωₘ\nb_tilde = b - α * a' * a\nbd_tilde = b' - α * a' * a\n\nH_pol = ω₀ * a' * a + ωₘ * bd_tilde * b_tilde + g * a' * a * (b_tilde + bd_tilde)\n\n((-(g^2)) / ωₘ + ω₀) * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b - ((g^2) / ωₘ) * a' * a' * a * a","title":"Polaron transformation by operator substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Effective-Kerr-Hamiltonian","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"Combining the canonical form of H_mathrmpol with the identity (a^dagger a)^2 = a^dagger^2 a^2 + a^dagger a gives\n\nboxed\n  H_mathrmpol\n  = omega_0 a^dagger a + omega_m b^dagger b\n  - fracg^2omega_m(a^dagger a)^2\n\n\nThe optomechanical coupling has been eliminated entirely in favour of a Kerr nonlinearity of strength K = g^2omega_m on the cavity mode. Photons attract each other with energy -Kn(n-1), a self-Kerr anharmonicity that is now routinely measured in superconducting and membrane-in-the-middle optomechanical experiments.  The mechanical mode, in the polaron frame, has decoupled completely from the optics.","title":"Effective Kerr Hamiltonian"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Numerical-verification","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"We diagonalise both the original H and the Kerr-only effective Hamiltonian H_mathrmKerr = omega_0a^dagger a + omega_mb^dagger b - (g^2omega_m)(a^dagger a)^2 and check that they share the same spectrum.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, LinearAlgebra, Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\nω₀_val, ωm_val = 1.0, 0.4\nn_max_c, n_max_m = 4, 60\nb_cav = FockBasis(n_max_c)\nb_mech = FockBasis(n_max_m)\nb_total = b_cav ⊗ b_mech\n\nH_Kerr = ω₀ * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b - (g^2 / ωₘ) * a' * a * a' * a\n\ngs = range(0.0, 0.6 * ωm_val, length = 16)\nE_full = Vector{Float64}[]\nE_eff = Vector{Float64}[]\nfor g_val in gs\n    subs = Dict(ω₀ => ω₀_val, ωₘ => ωm_val, g => g_val)\n    Hf = dense(to_numeric(substitute(H, subs), b_total))\n    He = dense(to_numeric(substitute(H_Kerr, subs), b_total))\n    push!(E_full, sort(real.(eigvals(Hermitian(Hf.data))))[1:8])\n    push!(E_eff, sort(real.(eigvals(Hermitian(He.data))))[1:8])\nend\n\nfig = plot(; xlabel = L\"g / \\omega_m\", ylabel = L\"(E_n - E_0) / \\omega_0\", title = \"Optomechanics: lowest 8 levels\", margin = 5Plots.mm)\ncolors = palette(:default)\nfor n in 2:8\n    full = [E_full[i][n] - E_full[i][1] for i in eachindex(gs)]\n    eff = [E_eff[i][n] - E_eff[i][1] for i in eachindex(gs)]\n    scatter!(fig, collect(gs) ./ ωm_val, full; color = colors[n - 1], marker = :circle, label = false)\n    plot!(fig, collect(gs) ./ ωm_val, eff; color = colors[n - 1], linestyle = :dash, label = false)\nend\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nDashed lines: Kerr-effective spectrum E_nm^mathrmeff = omega_0 n - (g^2omega_m)n^2 + omega_m m, visible as bundles of omega_m-spaced mechanical phonons attached to each Kerr-shifted cavity level. Markers: exact diagonalisation of the full radiation-pressure Hamiltonian.  They agree to truncation error across the whole coupling range, since the polaron substitution is exact and the package's eager canonicalisation reproduced the textbook derivation in three operator substitutions.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/optomechanics/#Membrane-in-the-middle:-quadratic-coupling","page":"Cavity Optomechanics","text":"A different geometry (a dielectric membrane suspended between two fixed mirrors) replaces the linear a^dagger a(b + b^dagger) coupling by a quadratic one,\n\nH_mathrmmim = omega_0 a^dagger a + omega_m b^dagger b\n  + g_2 a^dagger a(b + b^dagger)^2\n\nBecause the membrane sits at an optical node, the cavity frequency depends on the square of its displacement, enabling QND measurement of the phonon number.  Expanding (b + b^dagger)^2 is the package's job:\n\n@variables g₂\nH_mim = ω₀ * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b + g₂ * a' * a * (b + b')^2\n\n(g₂ + ω₀) * a' * a + ωₘ * b' * b + g₂ * a' * a * b * b + 2g₂ * a' * a * b' * b + g₂ * a' * a * b' * b'\n\nThree distinct effects fall out: a constant cavity-frequency renormalisation +g_2, a photon-number-dependent mechanical frequency 2 g_2 a^dagger a accompanying b^dagger b, and a photon-number-dependent two-phonon drive g_2 a^dagger a(b^2 + b^dagger 2).  Restricting to a cavity Fock sector nrangle_c (which is preserved because a^dagger a commutes with everything in H_mathrmmim), the mechanical Hamiltonian is\n\nH_m(n) = (omega_m + 2 g_2 n)b^dagger b\n  + g_2 n(b^2 + b^dagger 2) + (omega_0 + g_2)n\n\nThis is precisely the single-mode squeezing Hamiltonian solved in the Bogoliubov example, with effective oscillator frequency omega(n) = omega_m + 2 g_2 n and parametric drive kappa(n) = 2 g_2 n.  The Bogoliubov dispersion gives a photon-number-conditional mechanical frequency\n\nvarepsilon(n) = sqrtomega(n)^2 - kappa(n)^2\n  = omega_msqrt1 + 4g_2n  omega_m\n\nThe mechanical ground state in each cavity sector is a squeezed vacuum whose squeezing parameter grows monotonically with the cavity photon number.  Including the squeezing-induced zero-point shift, the full spectrum reads\n\nE(n m) = (omega_0 + g_2)n + varepsilon(n)m + tfrac12biglvarepsilon(n) - omega_m - 2 g_2 nbigr\n\nWe verify by exact diagonalisation:\n\nω₀_val2, ωm_val2, g2_val = 2.0, 1.0, 0.1\nb_total2 = FockBasis(4) ⊗ FockBasis(40)\nH_mim_op = dense(\n    to_numeric(\n        substitute(H_mim, Dict(ω₀ => ω₀_val2, ωₘ => ωm_val2, g₂ => g2_val)),\n        b_total2,\n    ),\n)\nF_mim = eigen(Hermitian(H_mim_op.data))\n\nε_mim(n) = ωm_val2 * sqrt(1 + 4 * g2_val * n / ωm_val2)\nE_th(n, m) = (ω₀_val2 + g2_val) * n + ε_mim(n) * m +\n    (ε_mim(n) - ωm_val2 - 2 * g2_val * n) / 2\n\npreds = sort(\n    [(n, m, E_th(n, m)) for n in 0:3 for m in 0:10];\n    by = t -> t[3],\n)[1:12]\nfor k in 1:12\n    n, m, eth = preds[k]\n    enum = F_mim.values[k]\n    println(\n        \"  (n=$n, m=$m)  E_th = $(round(eth; digits = 4))\",\n        \"   E_num = $(round(enum; digits = 4))\",\n        \"   |Δ| = $(round(abs(eth - enum); digits = 8))\"\n    )\nend\n\n  (n=0, m=0)  E_th = 0.0   E_num = 0.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=0, m=1)  E_th = 1.0   E_num = 1.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=0, m=2)  E_th = 2.0   E_num = 2.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=1, m=0)  E_th = 2.0916   E_num = 2.0916   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=0, m=3)  E_th = 3.0   E_num = 3.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=1, m=1)  E_th = 3.2748   E_num = 3.2748   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=0, m=4)  E_th = 4.0   E_num = 4.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=2, m=0)  E_th = 4.1708   E_num = 4.1708   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=1, m=2)  E_th = 4.458   E_num = 4.458   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=0, m=5)  E_th = 5.0   E_num = 5.0   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=2, m=1)  E_th = 5.5125   E_num = 5.5125   |Δ| = 0.0\n  (n=1, m=3)  E_th = 5.6413   E_num = 5.6413   |Δ| = 0.0\n\n\nEvery level matches the closed-form prediction to truncation precision. The first few photon-conditional mechanical frequencies are varepsilon(0) = omega_m, varepsilon(1) approx 10954omega_m, varepsilon(2) approx 11832omega_m.  The mechanical mode is stiffer in every higher-photon sector, with the stiffening predictable in closed form.  This number-state-conditional spring constant is what makes the membrane-in-the-middle a QND phonon-counter: by reading the cavity transmission's frequency-tracked sidebands one literally counts mechanical quanta.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Membrane-in-the-middle: quadratic coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Docstrings of internal types and functions, for reading next to the code. They describe the current implementation and may change without notice. The rules they implement are on the Architecture page.\n\nPages = [\"internals.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/internals/#Terms-and-canonicalization","page":"Internals","text":"A QAdd stores its terms in a QTermDict. Every operation that changes an operator product ends in canonicalize!, which sorts with partial_sort! and then streams the product through the passes of stream!.","title":"Terms and canonicalization"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/internals/#Exact-coefficients","page":"Internals","text":"A Coeff holds an exact number, a polynomial in the parameters, or a raw SymbolicUtils expression. The exact numbers and polynomials are built from the following types.","title":"Exact coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/internals/#Orderings","page":"Internals","text":"The total, identity-faithful ordering used by downstream packages to choose canonical representatives.","title":"Orderings"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/internals/#Averages-and-sums","page":"Internals","text":"An average is a SymbolicUtils term with the operation AvgFunc, and the average of an indexed sum is a term whose scope is a SumScope argument. The accessors read that scope.","title":"Averages and sums"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QTermDict","page":"Internals","text":"QTermDict\n\nAlias for Dict{QTerm, CNum}, the storage type backing every QAdd. Each entry maps an exact term identity (ops, ne) to its prefactor. Iteration yields Pair{QTerm, CNum}; callers reach term.ops and term.ne directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QTermDict"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SiteCmp","page":"Internals","text":"SiteCmp\n\nResult of site_compare, the site relation of two operators in the canonical sort.\n\nLess and Greater order operators on distinct sites. Equal marks a provably shared site, where the reduce and commute passes may act. Undetermined marks two free indices that may or may not coincide, where no rule may act. Both of the latter leave the pair in place; they are separate values so that a comparison cannot return one where the other is meant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SiteCmp"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.partial_sort!","page":"Internals","text":"partial_sort!(ops::Vector{Op}, ne::Vector{NonEqualPair})\n\nIn-place stable partial-sort using site_compare. Distinct-site adjacents are placed in canonical order; Equal and Undetermined pairs are left in physical order so the sibling passes can interpret them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.partial_sort!"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.canonicalize!","page":"Internals","text":"canonicalize!(out::QTermDict, ops::Vector{Op}, c::CNum, ne::Vector{NonEqualPair})\n\nRe-establish canonical form: sort, then run the pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.canonicalize!"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.stream!","page":"Internals","text":"stream!(out::QTermDict, ops::Vector{Op}, c::CNum, ne::Vector{NonEqualPair})\n\nThe default canonicalization pipeline: reduce, commute, reduce, then store in out.\n\nThe first reduce composes same-site Transition and Pauli pairs, which compose rather than commute. Commute then swaps the remaining same-site pairs (Fock, Spin, PhaseSpace and CollectiveTransition) and emits their commutator residuals. The trailing reduce catches a composition that a residual creates next to a same-site neighbour, such as the contracted operator of a Spin commutator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.stream!"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.GaussianRational","page":"Internals","text":"GaussianRational{T}\n\nAn exact Gaussian rational (re + im*i) / den, an element of ℚ(i). The denominator is positive and gcd(re, im, den) == 1, so every value has exactly one representation and isequal compares fields. A Gaussian integer has den == 1, and its arithmetic needs no gcd.\n\nIt is a storage type, not a Number: methods of a new Number subtype on ==, hash and the arithmetic operators invalidate SymbolicUtils code that dispatches on abstract Number arguments, which slowed its simplify by about a fifth. An exact value enters a symbolic expression as a Rational or Complex{Rational} instead.\n\nT = Int is the small tier. Its arithmetic is checked and throws OverflowError, like Rational{Int}; the kernels mul_checked/add_checked report the overflow as a flag instead. No field of a small-tier value is typemin(Int), so negation and conjugation never overflow. T = BigInt is the big tier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.GaussianRational"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ExactComplex","page":"Internals","text":"ExactComplex\nBigExactComplex\n\nThe two tiers of exact numbers, GaussianRational{Int} and GaussianRational{BigInt}.\n\nExactness is a property of the type: an exact value is a GaussianRational, and a ComplexF64 is always inexact. Every function that can produce an exact scalar takes the exact type E of its tier. Arithmetic in ExactComplex is checked and throws OverflowError; tiered (coeff.jl) catches that and redoes the whole computation in BigExactComplex, so the steps must not mutate their inputs. A result returns to ExactComplex whenever every scalar fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.ExactComplex"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Monomial","page":"Internals","text":"Monomial{E}\n\nOne term of a parameter polynomial: scalar * ∏ symᵢ^expᵢ. Factors are sorted by objectid and deduplicated; Rational{Int} exponents let radicals of a single atom merge (sqrt(p)*sqrt(p) = p). An exact scalar has the type E of its tier, ExactComplex or BigExactComplex, and every term of one Poly shares that tier. The slot field stores the scalar as an E, with a float packed into it (pack_scalar), so the layout is concrete; read it with monomial_scalar. A radical atom (a hashconsed Const{SymReal} of a prime, see radical_atom) always keeps its exponent in (0, 1): the constructor folds any integer part into scalar, so √2·√2 normalizes to the scalar 2, and √2·√3/√6/√24/2 all reduce to the same two prime atoms Const(2)^(1/2), Const(3)^(1/2) and compare isequal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Monomial"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Poly","page":"Internals","text":"Poly\n\nA sparse multivariate polynomial over named parameters (a sum of distinct Monomial terms in canonical order), kept off SymbolicUtils hashconsing and lowered to Complex{Num} only at the symbolic boundaries (see poly_to_num).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Poly"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.order_key","page":"Internals","text":"order_key(op::Op) -> Tuple\n\nTotal ordering key of an operator that ties two operators exactly when they are isequal.\n\nThe tuple is (space_index, kind, name rank, index, l1, l2, g, nlev): operators group by subspace, then by kind, then alphabetically by name, and the packed level and axis fields keep the remaining operators apart. Unlike hash, the key is reproducible across sessions and Julia versions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.order_key"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.term_order_key","page":"Internals","text":"term_order_key(t::QTerm) -> Tuple\n\nTotal ordering key for an operator product, built from order_key: length, then operator-by-operator, then the canonical-sorted non-equal constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.term_order_key"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.qadd_order_key","page":"Internals","text":"qadd_order_key(q::QAdd) -> Vector\n\nTotal, reproducible ordering key for a sum: its term/coefficient pairs in sorted order, so two QAdds compare with < on their keys and tie exactly when they are isequal. The coefficient contributes its printed form (a reproducible tiebreak, not a numeric order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.qadd_order_key"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superterm_order_key","page":"Internals","text":"superterm_order_key(t::SuperTerm)\n\nTotal ordering key for a sandwich: total length, then the left and right operators by order_key, then the canonical constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superterm_order_key"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superoperator_order_key","page":"Internals","text":"superoperator_order_key(S::SuperOperator) -> Vector\n\nTotal, reproducible ordering key: the sorted sandwich and coefficient keys. Two maps tie exactly when they are isequal up to their summation scope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superoperator_order_key"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.channel_order_key","page":"Internals","text":"channel_order_key(c::Channel)\n\nTotal ordering key for a channel: its jump and partner, then its scope and constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.channel_order_key"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.AvgFunc","page":"Internals","text":"AvgFunc\n\nSingleton callable used as the operation of average Term nodes; defining show_call on it gives the ⟨…⟩ display without type piracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.AvgFunc"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SumFunc","page":"Internals","text":"SumFunc\n\nSingleton callable used as the operation of moment-layer indexed-sum Term nodes (cf. AvgFunc). A node is sym_sum(body, scope): the summed body is an arbitrary averaged expression and the SumScope rides as a plain Term argument, so it participates in isequal/hash (metadata would be ignored, letting two differently-scoped sums over the same body wrongly cancel). Because the operation is neither + nor *, Add/Mul keep the node opaque, so the body and scope survive SymbolicUtils canonicalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SumFunc"},{"category":"type","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SumScope","page":"Internals","text":"SumScope\n\nOpaque carrier for an indexed-sum's summation scope (indices::Vector{Index} and ne::Vector{NonEqualPair}). Stored as a single scalar argument of a SumFunc node: SymbolicUtils array-ifies a bare Vector argument (losing the Index objects), but a scalar struct is kept opaque and recoverable, exactly like the QField argument of an average node. Custom ==/isequal/hash compare it by value, so two sums cancel only when their scopes are equal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SumScope"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_indexed_sum","page":"Internals","text":"is_indexed_sum(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is a sum of averages over an index, as made by average of a Σ.\n\nThe indices and constraints of the sum are read with get_sum_indices and get_sum_non_equal, and its summand with get_sum_body.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> x = average(Σ(IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2), i), i))\nΣ(i=1:10) ⟨σ_i₂₂⟩\n\njulia> is_indexed_sum(x)\ntrue\n\nSee also indexed_sum, average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_indexed_sum"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_sum_metadata","page":"Internals","text":"has_sum_metadata(x) -> Bool\n\nSame as is_indexed_sum, kept under its earlier name. The function is not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_sum_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices","page":"Internals","text":"get_sum_indices(q::QAdd) -> Vector{Index}\nget_sum_indices(q::QSym) -> Vector{Index}\n\nThe summation indices bound by q, in the order of its sum scope. A QSym and an unsummed QAdd bind none. The result is a copy, so mutating it does not change q.\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:site);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> i = Index(h, :i, 3, h);\n\njulia> SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices(Σ(IndexedOperator(a, i), i)) == [i]\ntrue\n\nSee also get_indices, constraint_pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\nget_sum_indices(x) -> Vector{Index}\n\nIndices summed over in the sum of averages x.\n\nThe function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: get_sum_indices\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> get_sum_indices(average(Σ(IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2), i), i)))\n1-element Vector{Index}:\n i\n\nSee also is_indexed_sum, get_sum_non_equal, get_sum_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_non_equal","page":"Internals","text":"get_sum_non_equal(x) -> Vector{Tuple{Index, Index}}\n\nPairs of indices required to differ in the sum of averages x.\n\nThe function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: get_sum_non_equal\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h); j = Index(h, :j, 10, h);\n\njulia> get_sum_non_equal(average(Σ(IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2), i), i, [j])))\n1-element Vector{Tuple{Index, Index}}:\n (i, j)\n\nSee also is_indexed_sum, get_sum_indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_non_equal"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_body","page":"Internals","text":"get_sum_body(x)\n\nSummand of the sum of averages x, with its index still free.\n\nThe function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: get_sum_body\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> get_sum_body(average(Σ(IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2), i), i)))\n⟨σ_i₂₂⟩\n\nSee also indexed_sum, get_sum_indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_sum_body"},{"category":"function","location":"dev/internals/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.indexed_sum","page":"Internals","text":"indexed_sum(body, indices::Vector{Index}; non_equal = Tuple{Index, Index}[])\n\nSum of the averaged expression body over indices, the inverse of get_sum_body.\n\nnon_equal lists pairs of indices that must differ. This rebuilds a sum after its summand has been rewritten, for instance when a cumulant expansion factorizes the averages in it. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: indexed_sum, get_sum_body\n\njulia> h = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2); i = Index(h, :i, 10, h);\n\njulia> x = average(Σ(IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, 2, 2), i), i));\n\njulia> isequal(indexed_sum(get_sum_body(x), [i]), x)\ntrue\n\nSee also get_sum_body, is_indexed_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.indexed_sum"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/contributing/#Contributing","page":"Contributing","text":"Contributions follow the ColPrac guidelines for pull requests, review and releases. Before changing code under src/, read the Architecture page: several of its invariants are not covered by tests.\n\nPages = [\"contributing.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/contributing/#Workflow","page":"Contributing","text":"Common tasks are targets of the Makefile:\n\nCommand Effect\nmake setup install the formatter Runic and the docs tools\nmake test run the whole test suite\nmake jet run the JET analysis on its own\nmake format format all Julia files with Runic\nmake docs build the documentation\nmake servedocs serve the documentation locally with live reload\nmake bench run the benchmarks\n\nA subset of the tests runs by passing path filters to the test runner, for instance Pkg.test(; test_args = [\"algebra/simplify\"]).","title":"Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/contributing/#Tests","page":"Contributing","text":"The tests are organized by behavior, not by source file: a test goes where a contributor would look when the behavior breaks. The directories and the placement rule are in test/README.md. Every bug fix comes with a test that fails without it.\n\nA pull request passes the quality gates in test/quality/:\n\nAqua: no unbound type parameters, stale dependencies or type piracy;\nJET: no type errors or runtime dispatch in the analyzed entry points;\nExplicitImports: every imported name is listed explicitly;\nCheckConcreteStructs: every struct field has a concrete type.\n\nThe continuous integration also runs the tests of the packages that build on this one, QuantumCumulants.jl, QuantumInputOutput.jl and FloquetExpansions.jl, against the change.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/contributing/#Code-style","page":"Contributing","text":"Use the most specific argument types possible, and an explicit ; before keyword arguments.\nKeep keyword arguments out of hot paths: accept them at the public function and forward positional arguments.\nCheck type stability with @inferred and allocations with @allocations instead of guessing.\nImport names explicitly, using X: f, g.\nName files and functions without a leading underscore; the module controls visibility.\nAdd a comment only for a reason that the code cannot show.\n\nDocstrings of public functions are user documentation: a one-sentence summary, every calling form, an example and a See also line. Implementation details belong in the developer documentation or in docstrings of internal functions.","title":"Code style"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/contributing/#Changelog","page":"Contributing","text":"Every change to user-facing code gets an entry in Changelog.md under the unreleased version, which the continuous integration checks: one or two sentences on what changed and where it matters, not on how. A breaking change goes under Changed (breaking).","title":"Changelog"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Parameters-and-coefficients","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"The coefficients of an operator expression are scalars: exact numbers or symbolic expressions from Symbolics.jl. They multiply operators without touching the operator algebra, and simplify applies symbolic identities to them.\n\nPages = [\"coefficients.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Parameters and coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Symbolic-parameters","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"Parameters are declared with @variables, which is re-exported from Symbolics. A plain declaration is real, so it is its own complex conjugate.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\na = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a)\n@variables ω η::Number ζ::Complex\n\n(ω * a)'\nω * a'\n\nA parameter declared ::Number is complex but stays a single symbol.\n\n(η * a)'\nconj(η) * a'\n\nA parameter declared ::Complex is split into independent real and imaginary parts.\n\n(ζ * a)'\n(real(ζ) + (-imag(ζ))im) * a'\n\nIn summary:\n\nDeclaration conj Representation\n@variables ω (or ω::Real) ω one real symbol\n@variables η::Number conj(η) one complex symbol\n@variables ζ::Complex real(ζ) - im*imag(ζ) independent real and imaginary parts\n\nA complex amplitude such as a drive is best declared ::Number, which keeps every product to a single symbol. ::Complex carries both parts through every product, and suits a parameter whose real and imaginary parts are wanted as independent unknowns.","title":"Symbolic parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Unit-phases","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"Time-dependent drives and rotating frames produce phase factors e^iphi. These are written with expim, which keeps them exact. The argument has to be real, since the identities overlinee^ix = e^-ix and e^ix = 1 hold only for real x.\n\nThe same phase is recognized however its argument is written.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: exponential_form, trigonometric_form\nimport Symbolics\n@variables ω t θ r\na = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a)\n\nexpim((ω + 2θ) * t) == expim(ω * t + 2θ * t)\ntrue\n\nInteger powers, negative ones included, and division also stay a single phase.\n\nexpim(θ)^3 / expim(ω * t), expim(θ)^-2\n(exp(im*(\u001b[34m3\u001b[39mθ - t*ω)), exp(-im*\u001b[34m2\u001b[39mθ))\n\nA fractional power would have to pick a branch, so sqrt(expim(θ)) and expim(θ)^(1//2) have no method rather than being rewritten as expim(θ/2).\n\nSubstitution rebuilds the phase from its new argument.\n\nsubstitute(expim(ω * t), Dict(ω => 2ω))\nexp(im*\u001b[34m2\u001b[39mt*ω)\n\nArguments are compared algebraically, not modulo 2pi, so expim(θ + 2π) is not recognized as expim(θ).\n\nDifferentiation follows the chain rule through the argument.\n\nSymbolics.derivative(expim(ω * t), t)\nexp(im*t*ω)*im*ω\n\nA coefficient that is exactly one unit phase has the familiar trigonometric projections.\n\nreal(expim(ω * t)), imag(expim(ω * t)), abs(expim(ω * t)), abs2(expim(ω * t))\n(cos(t*ω), sin(t*ω), 1, 1)\n\nA phase keeps the form it was written in, so positive and negative frequencies are not merged into a cosine.\n\n(expim(ω * t) + expim(-ω * t)) * a\n(exp(im*t*ω) + exp(-im*t*ω)) * a\n\nSwitching between the exponential and the trigonometric form is explicit, in both directions. Neither conversion is applied by display or by simplify, and expim(cos(θ)) stands for e^icostheta, not for a conversion of the cosine.\n\nBesides cosines and sines, exponential_form also recognizes exp(im * θ) and cis(θ).\n\nexponential_form(cis(θ)) * a\nexp(im*θ) * a\n\nA coefficient built from phases splits into its Fourier components.","title":"Unit phases"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Coefficient-identities","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"Symbolic identities are applied only by simplify. It covers trigonometric and hyperbolic identities, also with composite arguments, and leaves a coefficient unchanged when an identity cannot be applied without blowing up the expression.\n\nsimplify((cosh(r)^2 - sinh(r)^2) * (a + a'))\na + a'","title":"Coefficient identities"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Substitution","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"Parameters and operators are replaced in a single call, with all rules applied at once. Shifting a mode by a coherent amplitude while fixing a frequency gives the displaced Hamiltonian.\n\n@variables α::Number\nsubstitute(ω * a' * a, Dict(a => a + α, ω => 2))\n\u001b[34m2\u001b[39mconj(α)*α + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mconj(α) * a + \u001b[34m2\u001b[39mα * a' + 2 * a' * a","title":"Substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Taking-expressions-apart","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"To hand an expression to other code, for instance to build a numerical function from its coefficients, its parts can be read off directly. An expression first splits into its terms, and each term into a coefficient and operators.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sorted_arguments\nterms = sorted_arguments(ω * a' * a + η * a)\n2-element Vector{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}:\n η * a\n ω * a' * a\n\nget_prefactor(terms[2]), get_operators(terms[2])\n(ω, Op[a, a'])\n\nIterating an expression gives its terms as pairs of an operator product and a coefficient, kept in exact form. to_num converts a coefficient to Symbolics.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: to_num\n[(term.ops, to_num(c)) for (term, c) in 3ω * a' * a]\n1-element Vector{Tuple{Vector{Op}, Complex{Symbolics.Num}}}:\n ([a', a], \u001b[34m3\u001b[39mω)\n\ncoefficient_terms splits a coefficient into its terms, each an exact or floating scalar times powers of parameters, without going through Symbolics.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: coefficient_terms\ncoefficient_terms(3ω^2 - 1 // 2)\n2-element Vector{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CoefficientTerm}:\n SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CoefficientTerm(-1//2 + 0//1*im, Pair{Symbolics.Num, Rational{Int64}}[])\n SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CoefficientTerm(3//1 + 0//1*im, Pair{Symbolics.Num, Rational{Int64}}[ω => 2])\n\nA complex amplitude declared as @variables β::Number stays a single atom next to a phase, and its conjugate is the symbolic conj(β). Prefer it over ::Complex, which splits the parameter into real(β) + im*imag(β) in every coefficient:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:harmonic_example)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h)\n@variables ω::Real t::Real\n@variables β::Number\nβ * expim(ω * t) * a + conj(β) * expim(-ω * t) * a'\nexp(im*t*ω)*β * a + conj(β)*exp(-im*t*ω) * a'","title":"Taking expressions apart"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/coefficients/#Harmonic-projection","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"harmonics splits an expression into its Fourier components in a base frequency ω, the step behind a rotating-wave or secular approximation. harmonic returns one component, the coefficient of expim(n * ω * t), and secular the static one:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:harmonic_example)\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h)\n@variables ω::Real t::Real\nimport SecondQuantizedAlgebra: harmonic, harmonics, secular\n@variables ω0::Real ωd::Real F::Real τ::Real\nH = ω0 * a' * a - F * (a + a') * (expim(ωd * τ) + expim(-ωd * τ)) / 2\nHrot = transform(H, Rotation(a, ωd * τ, τ))\nharmonics(Hrot, ωd, τ)\nDict{Int64, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd} with 3 entries:\n  0  => \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a - \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a' + (ω0 - ωd) * a' * a\u001b[0m\n  2  => \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a'\u001b[0m\n  -2 => \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a\u001b[0m\n\nsecular(Hrot, ωd, τ)\n\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a - \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*F * a' + (ω0 - ωd) * a' * a\n\nThe decomposition is exact: summing harmonic(x, ω, t, n) * expim(n * ω * t) over the returned orders gives exponential_form(x). A phase that is not an exact integer multiple of ω * t plus a constant, or a time dependence outside a phase, throws an ArgumentError rather than being dropped, because assigning it would assume a relation between frequencies. The same functions act on SuperOperators and Lindbladians.\n\nmap_coefficients(f, expr) applies f to every scalar coefficient while preserving the operator structure.\n\ncoefficient(expr, product) reads the scalar multiplying a canonical operator product.","title":"Harmonic projection"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expim-Tuple{Real}","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"expim(x)\n\nExact unit phase with real argument x.\n\nexpim(x) stands for e^ix and is kept as an exact symbol rather than a generic exponential. Multiplying phases adds their arguments and opposite phases cancel, so the factors produced by drives and frame changes do not pile up.\n\nThe argument has to be real; for a complex exponent use exp. Fractional powers are refused, since they would have to pick a branch.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @variables ω t θ;\n\njulia> expim(ω * t) * expim(θ)\nexp(im*(θ + t*ω))\n\njulia> conj(expim(ω * t)) * expim(ω * t)\n1\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> expim(ω * t) * expim(-ω * t) * a\na\n\nSee also trigonometric_form, exponential_form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.expim"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.exponential_form","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"exponential_form(x)\n\nRewrite cosines and sines in x as unit phases.\n\nx can be a coefficient or a whole operator expression. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: exponential_form\n\njulia> @variables ω t;\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> exponential_form(cos(ω * t) * a)\n((1//2)*exp(im*t*ω) + (1//2)*exp(-im*t*ω)) * a\n\nSee also trigonometric_form, expim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.exponential_form"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.trigonometric_form","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"trigonometric_form(x)\n\nRewrite unit phases in x as cosines and sines.\n\nEach phase expim(θ) becomes cos θ + i sin θ, so opposite phases combine into real functions. x can be a coefficient or a whole operator expression. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: trigonometric_form\n\njulia> @variables ω t;\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> trigonometric_form((expim(ω * t) + expim(-ω * t)) * a)\n2cos(t*ω) * a\n\nSee also exponential_form, expim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.trigonometric_form"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.phase_terms","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"phase_terms(c)\n\nSplit a coefficient into its Fourier components.\n\nThe coefficient is written as a sum of amplitude * expim(phase), and each term of that sum is returned with the fields amplitude and phase. Cosines and sines have to be turned into phases first with exponential_form. A coefficient that is not a finite sum of phases throws an ArgumentError. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: phase_terms, exponential_form\n\njulia> @variables ω t g;\n\njulia> c = exponential_form(g * cos(ω * t));\n\njulia> terms = phase_terms(c); length(terms)\n2\n\njulia> terms[2].amplitude, terms[2].phase\n((1//2)*g, t*ω)\n\nSee also exponential_form, expim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.phase_terms"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PhaseTerm","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"PhaseTerm\n\nOne Fourier component of a coefficient, as returned by phase_terms.\n\nIt stands for amplitude * expim(phase), with the fields amplitude, a Coeff, and phase, a real symbolic expression.\n\nSee also phase_terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.PhaseTerm"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/coefficients/#SymbolicUtils.substitute-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QSym, AbstractDict}","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"substitute(expr::QField, rules::AbstractDict; replace_adjoint=true)\n\nReplace parameters and operators in expr according to rules.\n\nKeys may be parameters or operators, mixed freely. Operator rules act on the original expression all at once, so a rule whose replacement contains its own key, such as the shift a => a + α, is applied exactly once. A rule for a also covers a', unless a' has a rule of its own or replace_adjoint = false.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables Δ α::Number;\n\njulia> substitute(Δ * a' * a, Dict(Δ => 2))\n2 * a' * a\n\njulia> substitute(a' * a, Dict(a => a + α))\nconj(α)*α + conj(α) * a + α * a' + a' * a\n\nSee also change_index, Displace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SymbolicUtils.substitute"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.sorted_arguments","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"sorted_arguments(expr)\n\nTerms of expr, each as an expression of its own, in a fixed order.\n\nEach term can then be read with get_prefactor and get_operators. The function is not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sorted_arguments\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω g;\n\njulia> foreach(println, sorted_arguments(ω * a' * a + g * (a + a')))\ng * a\ng * a'\nω * a' * a\n\nSee also get_prefactor, get_operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.sorted_arguments"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_prefactor","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"get_prefactor(expr)\n\nCoefficient of a single-term expression expr, as a Complex{Num}.\n\nAn expression with more than one term throws an ArgumentError.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables g;\n\njulia> get_prefactor(2g * a' * a)\n2g\n\nSee also get_operators, get_variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_prefactor"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_operators","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"get_operators(expr)\n\nDistinct operators occurring in expr, in canonical order.\n\nAn operator and its adjoint count as two entries, and repeated factors appear once.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables g;\n\njulia> get_operators(g * a' * a + a)\n2-element Vector{Op}:\n a\n a'\n\nSee also get_variables, unique_up_to_adjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.get_operators"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/coefficients/#Symbolics.get_variables-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"get_variables(expr)\n\nSymbolic parameters occurring in the coefficients of expr.\n\nThe result can be passed to Symbolics functions such as build_function. An optional second argument restricts it to a given list of variables.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω g;\n\njulia> H = ω * a' * a + g * (a + a');\n\njulia> length(get_variables(H))\n2\n\nSee also get_prefactor, get_operators, substitute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Symbolics.get_variables"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QTerm","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"QTerm\n\nOperator product of one term of an expression.\n\nIterating an expression gives pairs term => coefficient of a QTerm and a Coeff. The field ops holds the operators in canonical order, and ne the pairs of indices the term requires to differ. It is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> [term.ops for (term, c) in 2 * a' * a]\n1-element Vector{Vector{Op}}:\n [a', a]\n\nSee also Coeff, get_operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Coeff","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"Coeff\n\nExact coefficient of an operator term.\n\nIterating an expression and exponential_form return coefficients in this form. It holds exact numbers, such as 1//2 + im, polynomials in the parameters, and any other symbolic expression, and it supports the usual arithmetic with numbers, parameters and other coefficients. to_num converts it to Symbolics. It is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: exponential_form, Coeff\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> exponential_form(cos(θ)) isa Coeff\ntrue\n\nSee also to_num, get_prefactor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Coeff"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_num","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"to_num(c::Coeff)\n\nCoefficient as a Complex{Num} of Symbolics.\n\nUse it to pass a coefficient read from an expression to code built on Symbolics, such as build_function. It is public but not exported.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: exponential_form, to_num\n\njulia> @variables θ;\n\njulia> to_num(exponential_form(cos(θ))) isa Complex\ntrue\n\nSee also Coeff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_num"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_coefficient_terms","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"has_coefficient_terms(c) -> Bool\n\nWhether the coefficient c is stored as a sum of CoefficientTerms, so that coefficient_terms can read it. Numbers and Laurent polynomials in parameters, radicals and function calls such as sin(q) are. A quotient by a sum or a root of a sum, such as p / (1 + q) or sqrt(p + q), is kept as one symbolic expression and is not.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: has_coefficient_terms\n\njulia> using Symbolics: @variables\n\njulia> @variables p q;\n\njulia> has_coefficient_terms(2p^2 + sin(q)), has_coefficient_terms(p / (1 + q))\n(true, false)\n\nSee also coefficient_terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.has_coefficient_terms"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.coefficient_terms","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"coefficient_terms(c) -> Vector{CoefficientTerm}\n\nThe terms of the coefficient c, without a zero term. A number gives one term with no powers, and zero gives no term. Throws an ArgumentError when c has no has_coefficient_terms form.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: coefficient_terms\n\njulia> using Symbolics: @variables\n\njulia> @variables p;\n\njulia> [(t.scalar, t.powers) for t in coefficient_terms(2p^2 + 1 // 3)]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Complex{Rational{BigInt}}, Vector{Pair{Symbolics.Num, Rational{Int64}}}}}:\n (1//3 + 0//1*im, [])\n (2//1 + 0//1*im, [p => 2])\n\nSee also CoefficientTerm, has_coefficient_terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.coefficient_terms"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CoefficientTerm","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"CoefficientTerm\n\nOne term scalar * ∏ xᵢ^eᵢ of a coefficient, as returned by coefficient_terms. The scalar is a Complex{Rational{BigInt}} when exact and a ComplexF64 when inexact. powers holds the atom => exponent pairs with nonzero rational exponents, possibly negative. An atom is a parameter, a prime under a radical (√2 is 2 => 1//2), or a symbolic subexpression the coefficient keeps whole, such as sin(g). The order of powers is not specified.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: coefficient_terms, CoefficientTerm\n\njulia> using Symbolics: @variables\n\njulia> @variables p;\n\njulia> only(coefficient_terms(3p^2))\nCoefficientTerm(3//1 + 0//1*im, Pair{Symbolics.Num, Rational{Int64}}[p => 2])\n\nSee also coefficient_terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.CoefficientTerm"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.harmonics","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"harmonics(x, ω, t) -> Dict{Int}\n\nDecompose x into its Fourier components in the base frequency ω, so that\n\nx == sum(xₙ * expim(n * ω * t) for (n, xₙ) in harmonics(x, ω, t))\n\nafter exponential_form. x is a coefficient, a quantum expression, a SuperOperator or a Lindbladian; the components have the type of x (a Coeff for a scalar, a QAdd for an operator). Zero components are omitted.\n\nTrigonometric factors are converted to expim first, as in phase_terms. Every phase must be n * ω * t + φ with an exact integer n and a t-independent offset φ, which stays in the component as expim(φ). Anything else throws an ArgumentError instead of being dropped or assigned to a guessed harmonic: a frequency that is not an exact integer multiple of ω (such as ω' or ω/2), a phase nonlinear in t, or a t-dependence outside a phase. Choose ω as the common base frequency of all phases.\n\nFor a Lindbladian the projection acts on the GKSL form, H, the rates and the jumps, and returns a Lindbladian; a jump J = Σ Jₘ expim(m ω t) with partner K = Σ Kₗ expim(l ω t) contributes 𝒟[Jₘ, Kₗ] to harmonic m - l. Only the secular (n = 0) part is again Hermiticity preserving in general.\n\nSee also harmonic, secular.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.harmonics"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.harmonic","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"harmonic(x, ω, t, n::Integer)\n\nThe coefficient of expim(n * ω * t) in x, with the same type as x (a Coeff for a scalar). Every phase of x must be an exact integer multiple of ω * t plus a constant; see harmonics for the precise contract. A component that is not of this form throws an ArgumentError even when n is a different order, because it could not be excluded from harmonic n without assuming a relation between frequencies.\n\nExamples\n\n@variables ω0::Real ωd::Real F::Real t::Real\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:a))\nH = ω0 * a' * a - F * (a + a') * (expim(ωd * t) + expim(-ωd * t)) / 2\nHt = transform(H, Rotation(a, ωd * t, t))\nsecular(Ht, ωd, t)      # (ω0 - ωd) a'a - F/2 (a + a')\nharmonic(Ht, ωd, t, 2)  # -F/2 a'\n\nSee also secular, phase_terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.harmonic"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.secular","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"secular(x, ω, t)\n\nThe time-independent (rotating-wave) part of x, harmonic(x, ω, t, 0). Every oscillating component must be an exact integer harmonic of ω; otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. For a Lindbladian the result is again a Lindbladian. Its rate matrices are time averages of those of L, so they stay positive semidefinite when those of L are at all times. See harmonics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.secular"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.map_coefficients","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"map_coefficients(f, x)\n\nApply f to every coefficient of x and rebuild it in canonical form. x is a QAdd (or a single operator), a SuperOperator or a polynomial Lindbladian. f receives a Coeff and may return a Number, a Symbolics Num or a Coeff; the result is converted exactly. Terms that map to zero are dropped and like terms merge.\n\nf acts per coefficient, so the operators are kept as they are. Summation scopes are kept: a term whose coefficient loses its dependence on a summation index is multiplied by that index's count, as with substitute, so Σᵢ gᵢ a'a with gᵢ ↦ g gives N g a'a. f must not introduce a summation index into a term it does not bind; that throws an ArgumentError.\n\nFor a Lindbladian, f maps the coefficients of the Hamiltonian, of every jump and every rate, and each channel is renormalized. This agrees with mapping the lowered SuperOperator when f is a substitution of real parameters or preserves values, such as simplify; a general f need not commute with lowering, because a lowered coefficient γ c_J conj(c_K) is a product of the stored ones.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:f);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h);\n\njulia> @variables x;\n\njulia> SecondQuantizedAlgebra.map_coefficients(c -> substitute(c, Dict(x => 2)), x * a' * a + a)\na + 2 * a' * a\n\nSee also simplify, substitute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.map_coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/coefficients/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.coefficient","page":"Parameters and coefficients","text":"coefficient(expr, term) -> Coeff\n\nThe coefficient of the operator product term in the polynomial expr, or zero when expr has no such term. term is an operator, a product such as a' * a, or 1 for the scalar part. It must canonicalize to a single product with unit coefficient, so 2a, a + a' and a * a' (which is a'a + 1) are refused. Index inequalities are part of a term: a product that expr stores under other constraints, or under several, throws instead of reading as zero; build term with assume_distinct_index to match a constrained product. Summed expressions are refused.\n\nUnlike coefficient_terms, which splits a scalar into its parameter monomials, coefficient reads the scalar that multiplies an operator product.\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: coefficient\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> H = 3 * a' * a + 2 * a' + 2 * a + 5;\n\njulia> coefficient(H, a' * a), coefficient(H, a'), coefficient(H, 1), coefficient(H, a' * a')\n(3, 2, 5, 0)\n\n\n\n\n\ncoefficient(S::SuperOperator, sandwich::SuperOperator) -> Coeff\n\nThe coefficient of the single sandwich sandwich, such as sprepost(a, a') or spre(a' * a), in the map S, or zero when S has no such sandwich. The rules for operator products apply to both sides: the sandwich must be one canonical term with unit coefficient, index constraints must match, and summed maps are refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.coefficient"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Open-quantum-systems","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A cavity that leaks photons or an atom that emits into free space does not evolve unitarily. For a memoryless environment the density matrix obeys a master equation dotrho = mathcalLrho, whose generator has the Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad form\n\nmathcalLrho = -iH rho + sum gamma mathcalDJ Krho\nqquad\nmathcalDJ Krho = J rho K^dagger - tfrac12 leftK^dagger J rhoright\n\nwith mathcalDJ = mathcalDJ J for an independent decay channel. Consider a two-level atom in a driven cavity,\n\nH = omega_c a^dagger a + omega_a sigma^ee + gleft(a^dagger sigma^ge + sigma^eg aright) + etaleft(e^-iomega_d t a^dagger + e^iomega_d t aright)\n\nwith cavity loss kappa and spontaneous emission gamma.\n\nPages = [\"open_systems.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Open quantum systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Lindblad-generators","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"hamiltonian_action builds the coherent part -iH cdot and dissipator a decay channel, with the rate as a scalar prefactor. The generator keeps its structure and prints as the Hamiltonian and its channels.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\na = Destroy(h, :a, 1)\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\n@variables t::Real ωc::Real ωa::Real ωd::Real g::Real η::Real κ::Real γ::Real\n\nH = ωc * a' * a + ωa * σ(:e, :e) + g * (a' * σ(:g, :e) + σ(:e, :g) * a) +\n    η * (expim(-ωd * t) * a' + expim(ωd * t) * a)\nL = hamiltonian_action(H) + κ * dissipator(a) + γ * dissipator(σ(:g, :e))\n-i[exp(im*t*ωd)*η * a + exp(-im*t*ωd)*η * a' + ωa * σ₂₂ + g * a * σ₂₁ + ωc * a' * a + g * a' * σ₁₂, ·] + κ * 𝒟[a] + γ * 𝒟[σ₁₂]\n\nDifferent spellings of one generator compare equal. A numeric factor of a jump moves into the rate, and a constant in a jump is a coherent drive, mathcalDJ + alpha = mathcalDJ - ilefttfraci2(baralpha J - alpha J^dagger) cdotright, so it moves into the Hamiltonian.\n\ndissipator(2a) == 4 * dissipator(a)\ntrue\n\ndissipator(a + 3)\n-i[3//2im * a + -3//2im * a', ·] + 𝒟[a]","title":"Lindblad generators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Rotating-frame","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"The drive oscillates at omega_d. transform moves the generator into the frame rotating with omega_d (a^dagger a + sigma^ee): the Hamiltonian is transformed and every jump is conjugated. The drive becomes static, the frequencies become the detunings Delta_c = omega_c - omega_d and Delta_a = omega_a - omega_d, and the jumps are unchanged, since a loss channel only picks up a phase.\n\nLrot = transform(L, RotatingFrame(ωd * (a' * a + σ(:e, :e)), t))\n-i[η * a + η * a' + (ωa - ωd) * σ₂₂ + g * a * σ₂₁ + (ωc - ωd) * a' * a + g * a' * σ₁₂, ·] + κ * 𝒟[a] + γ * 𝒟[σ₁₂]","title":"Rotating frame"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Equations-of-motion","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"The transpose of a generator is its Heisenberg-picture form, operatornameTr(AmathcalLrho) = operatornameTr(mathcalL^TArho), so fracddtlangle A rangle = langle mathcalL^T A rangle. The field is damped at kappa2, driven by eta and coupled to the atomic coherence.\n\naverage(transpose(Lrot)(a))\n-\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*⟨\u001b[34ma\u001b[39m⟩*κ - im*η - ⟨\u001b[34mσ₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*g*im + ⟨\u001b[34ma\u001b[39m⟩*im*(-ωc + ωd)\n\nThe coherence decays at gamma2 and is driven by the field times the inversion.\n\naverage(transpose(Lrot)(σ(:g, :e)))\n-\u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*⟨\u001b[34mσ₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*γ + ⟨\u001b[34ma * σ₂₂\u001b[39m⟩*g*im - ⟨\u001b[34ma * σ₁₁\u001b[39m⟩*g*im + ⟨\u001b[34mσ₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*im*(-ωa + ωd)\n\nFor a weak drive the atom stays near its ground state, langle asigma^ggrangle approx langle a rangle and langle asigma^eerangle approx 0, and the two equations close. Their steady state is the vacuum-Rabi response of the cavity field,\n\nlangle a rangle = frac-ietakappa2 + iDelta_c + g^2(gamma2 + iDelta_a)\n\nTrace preservation reads mathcalL^T 1 = 0.\n\ntranspose(Lrot)(1)\n0","title":"Equations of motion"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Numerics","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"to_numeric converts a generator to the backend's superoperator, with the same parameter, time_parameter and indexed sites arguments as for operators. The steady state of the rotating-frame Liouvillian at weak drive reproduces the vacuum-Rabi amplitude.\n\nimport QuantumToolbox as QT\np = Dict(ωc => 1.0, ωa => 1.1, ωd => 0.95, g => 0.2, η => 0.002, κ => 0.1, γ => 0.05)\nbackend = QuantumToolboxBackend()\nLnum = to_numeric(Lrot, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter = p)\nanum = to_numeric(a, h, (10, 2); backend)\nΔc, Δa = 0.05, 0.15\nQT.expect(anum, QT.steadystate(Lnum)), -0.002im / (0.05 + Δc * im + 0.2^2 / (0.025 + Δa * im))\n(0.0079710666045887 - 0.003550538017207381im, 0.007969151670951156 - 0.003547557840616967im)\n\nSolvers that take a Hamiltonian and collapse operators get them from to_numeric_master, here sqrtkappa a and sqrtgamma sigma^ge.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: to_numeric_master\nHnum, c_ops = to_numeric_master(Lrot, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter = p)\nQT.expect(anum, QT.steadystate(Hnum, c_ops))\n0.0079710666045887 - 0.003550538017207381im","title":"Numerics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Displacement-frames","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A damped linear mode is displaced to its steady state. DisplacementFrame takes the amplitude from the fixed point of the full drift, so loss and thermal heating both enter it, and the drive is gone in the displaced frame.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nb = Destroy(FockSpace(:c), :b)\nLth = hamiltonian_action(2 * b' * b + 3 * (b + b')) + (1 // 2) * dissipator(b) + (1 // 10) * dissipator(b')\nU = DisplacementFrame(b, Lth)\nUnitaryTransform with 2 rules:\n  b ↦ (-150//101 + -15//101im) + b\n  b' ↦ (-150//101 + 15//101im) + b'\n\ntranspose(transform(Lth, U))(b)\n(-1//5 + -2im) * b","title":"Displacement frames"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Superoperators","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A Lindbladian is one kind of linear map on operators. The general polynomial map rho mapsto sum c A rho B is a SuperOperator, built from the sandwich sprepost and its one-sided forms spre and spost. SuperOperator(L) lowers a Lindbladian to its sandwiches.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperOperator, spre, spost, sprepost\nD = SuperOperator(dissipator(a))\n-1//2 * (·) ⋅ a' * a + a ⋅ (·) ⋅ a' - 1//2 * a' * a ⋅ (·)\n\nD == sprepost(a, a') - (1 // 2) * spre(a' * a) - (1 // 2) * spost(a' * a)\ntrue\n\nThese names are public but not exported, because QuantumOpticsBase and QuantumToolbox export the same names for their numerical superoperators. With a backend loaded, its spre on a symbolic operator forwards to the symbolic one.\n\nMaps compose with ∘ or *, and the right factor acts first. Composing, transposing or applying a Lindbladian gives a SuperOperator.\n\n(spre(a) ∘ spost(a'))(σ(:e, :e))\nσ₂₂ + a' * a * σ₂₂\n\nEvery Lindbladian preserves the trace. The identity map does not, so it is not a Lindbladian: L^0, like a Lindbladian plus a scalar, is a general map. It prints as the bare placeholder of rho mapsto rho.\n\nL^0\n(·)\n\ntranspose and adjoint are different maps. The adjoint belongs to the Hilbert–Schmidt product operatornameTr(X^dagger Srho) and takes the adjoint of both sides, so the two agree only for maps that preserve Hermiticity.\n\ntranspose(spre(a))(σ(:e, :e)), adjoint(spre(a))(σ(:e, :e))\n(a * σ₂₂, a' * σ₂₂)","title":"Superoperators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#GKSL-form-of-a-map","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A map written in sandwiches, or one that comes out of a composition or a frame change, is read back as a Lindbladian. The extraction is exact in the operator algebra and needs no Hilbert-space cutoff.\n\nLindbladian(κ * (sprepost(a, a') - (1 // 2) * spre(a' * a) - (1 // 2) * spost(a' * a)))\nκ * 𝒟[a]\n\nLindbladian(SuperOperator(Lrot)) == Lrot\ntrue\n\nThe Kossakowski matrix depends on the basis of jumps. In the basis (a a + a^dagger a), independent loss and dephasing become a correlated matrix.\n\nkossakowski(κ * dissipator(a) + γ * dissipator(a' * a), JumpBasis(a, a + a' * a))\n2×2 Matrix{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Coeff}:\n γ + κ  -γ\n -γ     γ\n\nBasis operators are taken modulo the identity and with the completeness relation expanded.\n\nJumpBasis(σ(:g, :g), a + 1)\nJumpBasis with 2 operators:\n  1: -σ₂₂\n  2: a","title":"GKSL form of a map"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Correlated-and-collective-decay","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A cross channel mathcalDJ K with J neq K describes two decay paths into a shared bath, such as the cavity and the atom radiating into the same outgoing mode. liouvillian builds a generator from a list of jumps and a rate matrix in the QuantumOptics convention, where Gamma_ij multiplies J_i rho J_j^dagger. The off-diagonal entries become cross channels.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: liouvillian\n@variables Γc\nliouvillian(0, [a, σ(:g, :e)]; rates = [κ Γc; Γc γ])\nκ * 𝒟[a] + Γc * 𝒟[a, σ₁₂] + Γc * 𝒟[σ₁₂, a] + γ * 𝒟[σ₁₂]\n\nFor an ensemble of emitters, a generator is summed over sites with Σ. Independent emission at rate gamma on every atom drains the total excitation at rate gamma.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nha = NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\n@variables N γ\ni = Index(ha, :i, N, ha)\nj = Index(ha, :j, N, ha)\nk = Index(ha, :k, N, ha)\ns(x, y, n) = IndexedOperator(Transition(ha, :s, x, y), n)\n\nLind = Σ(γ * dissipator(s(:g, :e, i)), i)\ntranspose(Lind)(Σ(s(:e, :e, j), j))\nΣ(i=1:N) -γ * s_i₂₂\n\nEmitters closer than a wavelength radiate collectively, with a rate matrix Gamma_ij over pairs of sites. dissipator(J, Γ, i => j) builds sum_ij Gamma_ij mathcalDJ_i J_j and splits off the diagonal i = j, so the generator shows its local and correlated parts.\n\nLcol = dissipator(s(:g, :e, i), DoubleIndexedVariable(:Γ, i, j), i => j)\nΣ(i=1:N) Γ(i, i) * 𝒟[s_i₁₂] + Σ(i=1:N)Σ(j=1:N)(i≠j) Γ(i, j) * 𝒟[s_i₁₂, s_j₁₂]\n\nThe population of one emitter k then couples to the coherences between k and every other emitter.\n\naverage(transpose(Lcol)(s(:e, :e, k)))\nΣ(i=1:N)(i≠k) \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*⟨\u001b[34ms_i₁₂ * s_k₂₁\u001b[39m⟩*Γ(i, k) + Σ(j=1:N)(j≠k) \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m*⟨\u001b[34ms_j₂₁ * s_k₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*Γ(k, j) - ⟨\u001b[34ms_k₂₂\u001b[39m⟩*Γ(k, k)\n\nA map shares one summation scope between its two sides, as in sum_i J_i rho J_i^dagger. Constructions that would make the scope ambiguous throw instead of guessing, and the algebra never invents an index to resolve them:\n\ncomposing two maps that bind the same index; rename one with change_index;\napplying a map to an operator in which its bound index is free, since the sum would capture it, which is why the drift above is taken on s(:e, :e, k);\na jump that carries its own Σ, since J rho J^dagger with J = sum_i J_i needs two independent copies of the index;\npowers of a map with bound indices, for the same reason.\n\njulia> Lind ∘ Lind\nERROR: ArgumentError: summation index i is bound by both operands. Bound indices of composed maps, or of a map and the operator it acts on, must be distinct; rename one side with `change_index`.\njulia> dissipator(Σ(s(:g, :e, i), i))\nERROR: ArgumentError: a dissipator cannot take an operator with its own bound sum: `J ρ J'` with `J = Σᵢ Jᵢ` needs two independent copies of the summation index. Use `dissipator(Jᵢ, Γ, i => j)` for collective decay or `Σ(dissipator(Jᵢ), i)` for independent decay, and `Σ(sprepost(Aᵢ, Bᵢ), i)` for a summed sandwich.","title":"Correlated and collective decay"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Formal-operator-functions","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"A Hamiltonian or a jump with a formal function such as cos, sin or expim gives a Lindbladian{QExpr}, as for a transmon with its cosine potential and dephasing of the phase. taylor lowers it to a polynomial generator.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nφ = Position(PhaseSpace(:transmon), :φ)\n@variables γφ\nLt = hamiltonian_action(-cos(φ)) + γφ * dissipator(φ)\n-i[-cos(φ), ·] + γφ * 𝒟[φ]\n\ntaylor(Lt, 0:2)\n-i[1//2 * φ * φ, ·] + γφ * 𝒟[φ]\n\nA general map with a formal side is a SuperExpr, and taylor lowers it in the same way.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost\ntaylor(sprepost(cos(φ), φ), 0:2)\n(·) ⋅ φ - 1//2 * φ * φ ⋅ (·) ⋅ φ","title":"Formal operator functions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Inspecting-and-building-maps","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"The parts of a generator are read back with accessors. hamiltonian and channels split a Lindbladian into its Hamiltonian and its Channel => rate pairs, and jump, partner and scope read a channel.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: hamiltonian, channels, jump, partner, scope\nch, rate = first(channels(Lrot))\njump(ch), partner(ch), rate\n(a, a, κ)\n\nIterating a SuperOperator gives its SuperTerm => coefficient pairs, whose sides are read with left_operator and right_operator. A SuperExpr splits into its polynomial part and its formal sandwiches with poly and formal_terms.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: left_operator, right_operator\nterm, c = first(D)\nleft_operator(term), right_operator(term), c\n(a' * a, 1, \u001b[34m(\u001b[39m\u001b[34m-1//2\u001b[39m\u001b[34m)\u001b[39m)\n\nsum over an array of Lindbladians or SuperOperators accumulates in place, like sum over operators, so a generator with many channels costs linear time. A loop uses a builder directly.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra: LindbladianBuilder, QAdd, accumulate!, build\nhm = reduce(⊗, [FockSpace(Symbol(:mode, n)) for n in 1:3])\nmodes = [Destroy(hm, Symbol(:a, n), n) for n in 1:3]\nbuilder = LindbladianBuilder{QAdd}()\nfor m in modes\n    accumulate!(builder, κ * dissipator(m))\nend\nbuild(builder) == sum([κ * dissipator(m) for m in modes])\ntrue","title":"Inspecting and building maps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/open_systems/#Secular-projection","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"When phases remain after the transformation, secular keeps the time-independent part of the Hamiltonian, the rates and the jumps, and returns a Lindbladian. For a quadrature jump this is the rotating-wave dissipator:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n@qnumbers b::Destroy(FockSpace(:secular_mode))\n@variables ω::Real κ::Real ωd::Real t::Real\nimport SecondQuantizedAlgebra: secular\nLx = hamiltonian_action(ω * b' * b) + κ * dissipator(b + b')\nsecular(transform(Lx, Rotation(b, ωd * t, t)), ωd, t)\n-i[(ω - ωd) * b' * b, ·] + κ * 𝒟[b] + κ * 𝒟[b']","title":"Secular projection"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Lindbladian","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"Lindbladian{O}\n\nA Lindblad generator that keeps its Hamiltonian and decay channels.\n\nThe generator is L = -i[H, ·] + Σ γ 𝒟[J, K]. O is QAdd for a polynomial model and QExpr when the Hamiltonian or a jump contains a formal operator function. Build a generator with hamiltonian_action, dissipator and liouvillian, and combine generators with +, scalar * and Σ. L(ρ) applies the generator, and transpose(L)(A) is the Heisenberg drift of the observable A.\n\nA Lindbladian is a form, not a claim of physicality: H may be non-Hermitian and the rates complex or negative. It always preserves the trace. It preserves Hermiticity when H and the rate matrix are Hermitian, and it generates a completely positive evolution when the rate matrix is also positive semidefinite.\n\nEquality is structural. Equal spellings of one channel compare equal, but a cross term is not expanded, so two generators that act alike are compared with SuperOperator(L₁) == SuperOperator(L₂).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> L = hamiltonian_action(a' * a) + 2 * dissipator(a)\n-i[a' * a, ·] + 2 * 𝒟[a]\n\njulia> transpose(L)(a)\n(-1 + -1im) * a\n\nSee also SuperOperator, to_numeric_master.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Lindbladian"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.hamiltonian_action","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"hamiltonian_action(H)\n\nThe coherent part of a Lindblad generator with Hamiltonian H.\n\nIt acts as ρ ↦ -i[H, ρ]. The identity part of H is dropped, since it commutes with every state. A summed H keeps its summation scope.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> hamiltonian_action(a' * a + 1)\n-i[a' * a, ·]\n\nSee also dissipator, Lindbladian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.hamiltonian_action"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.dissipator","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"dissipator(J)\ndissipator(J, K)\ndissipator(J, Γ, i => j, ...)\n\nA decay channel of a Lindblad generator with jump operator J.\n\ndissipator(J) acts as 𝒟[J](ρ) = J ρ J' - (J'J ρ + ρ J'J)/2, and dissipator(J, K) is the cross channel 𝒟[J, K](ρ) = J ρ K' - (K'J ρ + ρ K'J)/2. Rates are scalar prefactors, κ * dissipator(a). A numeric factor of a jump moves into the rate, and a constant in a jump moves into the Hamiltonian as the drive it generates.\n\ndissipator(J, Γ, i => j) is the correlated family Σᵢ Σⱼ Γ(i, j) 𝒟[Jᵢ, Jⱼ], where i is a free index of J and j a second index on the same space and range. The diagonal i = j is split off as Γ(i, i) 𝒟[Jᵢ]. Several pairs give a family over several indices. A jump that carries its own Σ throws.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> dissipator(a)(a' * a)\n1 + 2 * a' * a\n\njulia> dissipator(2a) == 4 * dissipator(a)\ntrue\n\nSee also hamiltonian_action, liouvillian, Lindbladian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.dissipator"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/open_systems/#Base.transpose-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator}","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"transpose(S::SuperOperator)\n\nThe Heisenberg-picture map of a map on operators.\n\nIt satisfies Tr(A * S(ρ)) == Tr(transpose(S)(A) * ρ), and turns each term c * L ρ R into c * R A L. For a generator L, transpose(L)(A) is the drift of the observable A, and average(transpose(L)(A)) its equation of motion.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> transpose(2 * dissipator(a))(a' * a)\n-2 * a' * a\n\nSee also Lindbladian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric_master","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"to_numeric_master(L::Lindbladian, h, dims; backend, parameter, time_parameter, sites, scalar_subs, tol)\n\nThe numerical Hamiltonian and collapse operators of a Lindbladian, as inputs for a master-equation solver.\n\nReturns (hamiltonian = H, collapse_operators = c_ops), which feed QuantumOptics timeevolution.master and QuantumToolbox mesolve without separate rates. backend, parameter, time_parameter and sites work as for to_numeric, and scalar_subs resolves coefficients of unrolled indices, such as g(k). A single channel γ 𝒟[J] gives √γ J. The rate matrix of every block of correlated channels is diagonalized, c_k = √λ_k Σ_m U[m, k] M_m, where M_m are the operator monomials of the jumps.\n\nThe generator must be physical: the rate matrices must be Hermitian and positive semidefinite and H Hermitian, or the conversion throws. Eigenvalues with |λ| ≤ tol * max|λ| are set to zero, with tol = √eps by default. A time-dependent H is checked at a few sample times.\n\nWith a time_parameter, which needs QuantumToolbox, H becomes a QobjEvo, and a single channel with a rate γ(t) gives the collapse operator √γ(t) J; the rate must stay real and nonnegative during the solve. A correlated block with time-dependent rates throws, since the eigenvectors of Γ(t) move with t; to_numeric(L, h, dims; time_parameter) gives the full generator instead.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: to_numeric_master\n\njulia> import QuantumToolbox\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> L = hamiltonian_action(a' * a) + 4 * dissipator(a);\n\njulia> H, c_ops = to_numeric_master(L, h, 4; backend = QuantumToolboxBackend());\n\njulia> only(c_ops) ≈ 2 * to_numeric(a, h, 4; backend = QuantumToolboxBackend())\ntrue\n\nSee also to_numeric, Lindbladian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric_master"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"SuperOperator\nSuperOperator(L::Lindbladian)\nSuperOperator(pairs, indices)\n\nA polynomial linear map on operators.\n\nThe map acts as ρ ↦ Σ c * A * ρ * B. Build maps with sprepost, spre and spost, or lower a Lindbladian with SuperOperator(L). SuperOperator(pairs, indices) rebuilds a map from SuperTerm => coefficient pairs and a summation scope. Maps compose with ∘ or *, the right factor acting first, and S(ρ) applies a map. transpose(S) satisfies Tr(A * S(ρ)) == Tr(transpose(S)(A) * ρ) and is the Heisenberg-picture map, while adjoint(S) is the Hilbert–Schmidt adjoint. Iterating a map gives its SuperTerm => coefficient pairs.\n\nA map is not an operator, so it does not enter operator products or averages. The name is public but not exported, since the numeric backends export a SuperOperator of their own.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperOperator\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> SuperOperator(dissipator(a))\n-1//2 * (·) ⋅ a' * a + a ⋅ (·) ⋅ a' - 1//2 * a' * a ⋅ (·)\n\nSee also Lindbladian, SuperExpr, compose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.sprepost","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"sprepost(A, B)\n\nThe map that multiplies an operator by A from the left and by B from the right.\n\nIt acts as ρ ↦ A * ρ * B. A and B are operators or scalars. A side with a formal operator function gives a SuperExpr. A side that carries its own Σ throws; sum the map instead, Σ(sprepost(Aᵢ, Bᵢ), i).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> S = sprepost(a, a')\na ⋅ (·) ⋅ a'\n\njulia> S(a' * a)\n1 + 3 * a' * a + a' * a' * a * a\n\nSee also spre, spost, SuperOperator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.sprepost"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.spre","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"spre(A)\n\nThe map that multiplies an operator by A from the left, ρ ↦ A * ρ.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: spre\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> spre(a)(a')\n1 + a' * a\n\nSee also spost, sprepost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.spre"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.spost","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"spost(B)\n\nThe map that multiplies an operator by B from the right, ρ ↦ ρ * B.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: spost\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> spost(a)(a')\na' * a\n\nSee also spre, sprepost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.spost"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.compose","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"compose(A, B)\nA ∘ B\nA * B\n\nThe composition of two maps, applying B first.\n\nComposing maps that bind the same summation index throws; rename one with change_index.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: spre, spost, compose\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> compose(spre(a), spost(a'))\na ⋅ (·) ⋅ a'\n\nSee also SuperOperator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.compose"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/open_systems/#Base.adjoint-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator}","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"adjoint(S::SuperOperator)\n\nThe adjoint of a map with respect to the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product.\n\nIt satisfies Tr(X' * S(ρ)) == Tr(S'(X)' * ρ), and turns each term c * A ρ B into conj(c) * A' X B'. It is the Heisenberg-picture map only for maps that preserve Hermiticity; transpose is the Heisenberg-picture map in general.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: spre\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> adjoint(spre(a))(a' * a)\na' * a' * a\n\nSee also transpose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.adjoint"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/open_systems/#TensorCore.:⊗-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator}","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"⊗(A::SuperOperator, B::SuperOperator)\n\nThe map that acts as A and B on two disjoint subsystems.\n\nIt equals A ∘ B, and throws when the two maps act on a common subspace.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperOperator\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:a) ⊗ FockSpace(:b); a = Destroy(h, :a, 1); b = Destroy(h, :b, 2);\n\njulia> SuperOperator(dissipator(a)) ⊗ SuperOperator(dissipator(b)) ==\n           SuperOperator(dissipator(a)) ∘ SuperOperator(dissipator(b))\ntrue\n\nSee also compose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TensorCore.:⊗"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Lindbladian-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperOperator, JumpBasis}","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"Lindbladian(S::SuperOperator[, basis::JumpBasis]; rtol = 0)\n\nA map in GKSL form, read back as a structured generator. The result has the Hamiltonian of S and one channel d_{μν} 𝒟[F_μ, F_ν] for every nonzero entry of the Kossakowski matrix in basis, so SuperOperator(Lindbladian(S, basis)) equals S up to completeness expansion (and up to the terms a positive rtol drops). Without a basis the support_basis of S is used. The Hamiltonian is defined modulo the identity, which hamiltonian_action drops.\n\nThrows and handles floating-point coefficients like kossakowski. The rates are not checked for positivity.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperOperator\n\njulia> Lindbladian(SuperOperator(dissipator(a + 3)))\n-i[3//2im * a + -3//2im * a', ·] + 𝒟[a]\n\nSee also kossakowski, support_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Lindbladian"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.JumpBasis","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"JumpBasis(operators...)\nJumpBasis(operators::AbstractVector)\n\nAn ordered basis of jump operators (F_1  F_q) in which a map is written in GKSL form,\n\nL(ρ) = -i[H, ρ] + Σ_{μν} d_{μν} (F_μ ρ F_ν' - {F_ν' F_μ, ρ}/2),\n\nwith d the Kossakowski matrix returned by kossakowski. Each operator is canonicalized with expand_completeness and taken modulo the identity, since a scalar shift of a jump changes only the Hamiltonian. The operators must be linearly independent modulo the identity, and an ArgumentError is thrown otherwise, including for an operator proportional to the identity; they need not be orthogonal, normalized or complete. Their order fixes the order of d, so bases with the same span in a different order are different. An empty basis is the basis of a coherent map.\n\nA JumpBasis iterates and indexes like a vector of its canonical operators.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> JumpBasis(a + 2, a' * a)\nJumpBasis with 2 operators:\n  1: a\n  2: a' * a\n\nSee also independent_basis, support_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.JumpBasis"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.kossakowski","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"kossakowski(S::SuperOperator, basis::JumpBasis; rtol = 0) -> Matrix{Coeff}\nkossakowski(L::Lindbladian, basis::JumpBasis; rtol = 0) -> Matrix{Coeff}\n\nThe Hermitian Kossakowski matrix d of a map in the ordered basis:\n\nS(ρ) = -i[H, ρ] + Σ_{μν} d_{μν} (F_μ ρ F_ν' - {F_ν' F_μ, ρ}/2).\n\nThe extraction works in the operator algebra and introduces no Hilbert-space cutoff. It throws an ArgumentError when the map has a jump direction outside basis, and a GKSLFormError when the map is not of GKSL form. A Lindbladian is read through its SuperOperator, so its own channels need not lie in basis.\n\nThe extraction is exact. Floating-point coefficients carry the roundoff of building the map, so a float map is usually of GKSL form only up to that roundoff and is rejected; write rates as exact rationals such as 1//10 or as symbolic parameters instead. A positive rtol opts into a heuristic: a leftover float coefficient counts as roundoff when, for every parameter monomial, it is at most rtol times the size of the coefficients it was computed from. No single rtol suits every basis, since roundoff grows as the basis operators approach linear dependence, and genuine terms below rtol are dropped. Exact coefficients are always compared exactly.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> using Symbolics: @variables\n\njulia> @variables κ::Real γ::Real;\n\njulia> kossakowski(κ * dissipator(a) + γ * dissipator(a' * a), JumpBasis(a, a + a' * a))\n2×2 Matrix{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Coeff}:\n γ + κ  -γ\n -γ     γ\n\nSee also Lindbladian, support_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.kossakowski"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.support_basis","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"support_basis(S::SuperOperator) -> JumpBasis\nsupport_basis(L::Lindbladian) -> JumpBasis\n\nThe JumpBasis of the operator monomials on the two-sided sandwiches of a map, after expand_completeness, in term_order_key order. Every jump direction of the map lies in its span, so Lindbladian(S) reads S in this basis.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: support_basis\n\njulia> @qnumbers b::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> support_basis(dissipator(b) + dissipator(b' * b))\nJumpBasis with 2 operators:\n  1: b\n  2: b' * b\n\nSee also JumpBasis, independent_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.support_basis"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.independent_basis","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"independent_basis(candidates::AbstractVector) -> JumpBasis\n\nThe JumpBasis of those candidates that are linearly independent modulo the identity of the candidates before them, kept in order. A candidate proportional to the identity is skipped.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: independent_basis\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> independent_basis([a, 2a + 1, one(a), a' * a])\nJumpBasis with 2 operators:\n  1: a\n  2: a' * a\n\nSee also JumpBasis, support_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.independent_basis"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.GKSLFormError","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"GKSLFormError(message)\n\nThe error thrown when a map has no GKSL form.\n\nThe map either does not preserve the trace, has a non-Hermitian Kossakowski matrix, has a one-sided part that is not a commutator, or has a Hamiltonian that is not Hermitian modulo the identity. The trace is checked first, so a trace defect is reported as such. A Hamiltonian such as x * p is accepted, since it differs from the Hermitian (x * p + p * x) / 2 by a scalar.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: spre\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(FockSpace(:c));\n\njulia> kossakowski(spre(a), JumpBasis(a))\nERROR: the map does not preserve the trace\n[...]\n\nSee also kossakowski, Lindbladian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.GKSLFormError"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.liouvillian","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"liouvillian(H, J; rates = nothing)\n\nThe Lindblad generator of the Hamiltonian H and the jump operators J, in the QuantumOptics convention.\n\nThe generator is -i[H, ·] + Σᵢⱼ Γ[i, j] 𝒟[J[i], J[j]]. rates is nothing for unit rates, a vector of rates, or a matrix Γ whose entry Γ[i, j] multiplies J[i] ρ J[j]'. An empty J gives the coherent part only. The name is public but not exported, since QuantumOpticsBase exports a numerical liouvillian.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: liouvillian\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> liouvillian(a' * a, [a, a']; rates = [1, 2])\n-i[a' * a, ·] + 𝒟[a] + 2 * 𝒟[a']\n\nSee also dissipator, hamiltonian_action.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.liouvillian"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperExpr","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"SuperExpr\nSuperExpr(L::Lindbladian)\n\nA linear map on operators whose sides may contain formal operator functions.\n\nA SuperExpr is a polynomial SuperOperator plus sandwiches c * f ρ g with a QExpr side. It arises from sprepost with a formal side, from operations on a Lindbladian{QExpr}, and from SuperExpr(L). It supports the arithmetic, composition, transpose and application of a SuperOperator, and keeps its type even when no formal sandwich remains. taylor(X, 0:n) lowers every formal side and returns a SuperOperator, and SuperOperator(X) converts an X without formal sandwiches.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> X = sprepost(cos(a' * a), a)\ncos(a' * a) ⋅ (·) ⋅ a\n\njulia> X isa SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperExpr\ntrue\n\nSee also SuperOperator, taylor, poly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperExpr"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.hamiltonian","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"hamiltonian(L::Lindbladian)\nchannels(L::Lindbladian)\n\nThe Hamiltonian of a Lindbladian, and a vector of its Channel => rate pairs in channel_order_key order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: hamiltonian, channels, jump\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> L = hamiltonian_action(a' * a) + 2 * dissipator(a);\n\njulia> hamiltonian(L)\na' * a\n\njulia> ch, rate = first(channels(L)); jump(ch), rate\n(a, 2)\n\nSee also Channel, jump.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.hamiltonian"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Channel","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"Channel{O}\n\nOne decay channel of a Lindbladian, without its rate.\n\nA channel Σ 𝒟[J, K] has a jump J, a partner K and the summation indices of its sum. An independent channel 𝒟[J] has K == J. channels(L) pairs each channel with its rate, and constants and numeric factors of a jump are moved out of the channel when it is built.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: channels\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> first(channels(dissipator(2a)))\n𝒟[a] => 4\n\nSee also jump, hamiltonian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.Channel"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jump","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"jump(c::Channel)\npartner(c::Channel)\nscope(c::Channel)\n\nThe jump operator, the partner and the summation indices of a decay channel.\n\nFor a channel Σ 𝒟[J, K] these are J, K and the indices of the sum. The partner of an independent channel 𝒟[J] is J itself.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: channels, jump, partner, scope\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> ch, rate = first(channels(dissipator(a, a')));\n\njulia> jump(ch), partner(ch), isempty(scope(ch))\n(a, a', true)\n\nSee also Channel, hamiltonian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jump"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperTerm","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"SuperTerm\n\nOne term of a SuperOperator, without its coefficient.\n\nA term is a sandwich ρ ↦ A * ρ * B. Iterating a SuperOperator gives SuperTerm => coefficient pairs. The two sides share their index inequalities, and are read with left_operator and right_operator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> first(sprepost(2a, a'))\na ⋅ (·) ⋅ a' => 2\n\nSee also superterm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperTerm"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superterm","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"superterm(left, right, ne = Tuple{Index, Index}[])\n\nThe key of one term of a SuperOperator, built from two operator products.\n\nThe term is the sandwich ρ ↦ left * ρ * right. left and right are vectors of operators, and ne lists pairs of indices that are distinct. The sides are used as given, so get(S, superterm(left, right), 0) finds a coefficient only for sides in canonical order, such as those of an iterated term.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost, superterm\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> get(sprepost(2a, a'), superterm([a], [a']), 0)\n2\n\nSee also SuperTerm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.superterm"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.left_operator","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"left_operator(t::SuperTerm)\nright_operator(t::SuperTerm)\n\nThe operator acting from the left or from the right in the sandwich t.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost, left_operator, right_operator\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> t, c = first(sprepost(a, a')); left_operator(t), right_operator(t)\n(a, a')\n\nSee also SuperTerm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.left_operator"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.poly","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"poly(X::SuperExpr)\nformal_terms(X::SuperExpr)\n\nThe polynomial part of a SuperExpr, and an iterator over its formal sandwiches with their coefficients.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: sprepost, spre, poly, formal_terms\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> X = sprepost(cos(a' * a), a) + spre(a);\n\njulia> poly(X)\na ⋅ (·)\n\njulia> length(collect(formal_terms(X)))\n1\n\nSee also SuperExpr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.poly"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperBuilder","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"SuperBuilder()\n\nAn accumulator that builds a SuperOperator from many terms in linear time.\n\nAdd maps with accumulate!(b, S) and single sandwiches c * A ρ B with accumulate!(b, c, A, B), and finish with build. sum over an array of maps uses it already.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperBuilder, spre, accumulate!, build\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> b = SuperBuilder();\n\njulia> accumulate!(b, spre(a)); accumulate!(b, 2, a, a');\n\njulia> build(b)\na ⋅ (·) + 2 * a ⋅ (·) ⋅ a'\n\nSee also LindbladianBuilder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.SuperBuilder"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.LindbladianBuilder","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"LindbladianBuilder{O}()\n\nAn accumulator that builds a Lindbladian{O} from many terms in linear time.\n\nAdd generators with accumulate! and finish with build. sum over an array of Lindbladians uses it already.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: LindbladianBuilder, QAdd, accumulate!, build\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> b = LindbladianBuilder{QAdd}();\n\njulia> accumulate!(b, dissipator(a)); accumulate!(b, dissipator(a));\n\njulia> build(b)\n2 * 𝒟[a]\n\nSee also SuperBuilder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.LindbladianBuilder"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.accumulate!","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"accumulate!(b, x)\naccumulate!(b::SuperBuilder, c, A, B)\n\nAdd x to the builder b in place.\n\nA sum of many terms then costs linear time instead of the quadratic cost of a chain of +. A SuperBuilder also takes a single sandwich c * A ρ B. Finish with build.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using SecondQuantizedAlgebra: SuperBuilder, spre, accumulate!, build\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:c); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> b = SuperBuilder();\n\njulia> accumulate!(b, spre(a)); accumulate!(b, spre(a));\n\njulia> build(b)\n2 * a ⋅ (·)\n\nSee also SuperBuilder, LindbladianBuilder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.accumulate!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/open_systems/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.build","page":"Open quantum systems","text":"build(b)\n\nThe expression accumulated in the builder b, in canonical form.\n\nThe result may share storage with b, so b is not used after build. See accumulate! for an example.\n\nSee also SuperBuilder, LindbladianBuilder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.build"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changelog","page":"Changelog","text":"All notable changes to SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on Keep a Changelog, and this project adheres to Semantic Versioning.","title":"Changelog"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.14.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"Adds symbolic dissipative dynamics and exact GKSL extraction, with breaking API changes for type stability.","title":"[v0.14.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added","page":"Changelog","text":"Symbolic Lindbladian, SuperOperator and SuperExpr maps, including collective dissipators, composition, Heisenberg generators, indexed sums and frame transformations. Use to_numeric for backend superoperators and to_numeric_master for Hamiltonian/collapse operators.\nExact GKSL extraction through Lindbladian(S[, basis]) and kossakowski(S, basis), with JumpBasis, support_basis, independent_basis and GKSLFormError. No Hilbert-space cutoff is needed; accepting floating-point roundoff requires an explicit rtol.\ncoefficient reads an operator-product or superoperator-sandwich coefficient; ambiguous constraints and summed expressions throw. map_coefficients transforms coefficients, while coefficient_terms, CoefficientTerm and has_coefficient_terms expose their scalar factors and atom powers.\nharmonics, harmonic and secular extract exact Fourier components from coefficients, operators and dissipative maps. Noninteger harmonics throw.\ndisplacement(U, a) reads a frame's displacement amplitude; UnitaryTransform(G, θ) supports symmetric hopping and squeezing generators.\nIntrospection through get_sum_indices(::QAdd), n_levels, ground_state, transition_levels and operator_axis. Indexed to_numeric accepts parameter and operators keywords.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)","page":"Changelog","text":"index_slot returns 0 instead of nothing for an abstract index; use iszero(index_slot(x)).\nto_numeric, numeric_average and expect default to op_type = EagerOperator(). Custom backends must dispatch numeric_materialize on ::EagerOperator instead of ::Nothing.\nfundamental_operators and find_operators use names = Symbol[] instead of nothing for automatic names. find_operators returns Vector{Union{Op, QAdd}} instead of Vector{QField}.\nchannels(L) returns a sorted vector of Channel => rate pairs in channel_order_key order, replacing unordered dictionary pairs.\nFor custom numeric backends, the numeric_operator(be, ::Val{kind}, op, subbasis) fallback is removed; unsupported roles throw. numeric_assemble_td receives plain (p, t) closures instead of PControlCoeff wrappers.\nnumeric_basis returns a tuple of dimensions for composite QuantumToolbox states.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed","page":"Changelog","text":"Faster symbolic coefficient arithmetic, sparse QuantumOptics conversion on composite bases, LazyKet expectation values and QuantumToolbox time-dependent coefficient evaluation. Runtime-dispatch checks now cover both backend extensions.\nsubstitute rejects parameter values that are neither numeric nor symbolic scalars with ArgumentError.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed","page":"Changelog","text":"Indexed sums preserve site counts after substitution or coefficient simplification, respect excluded sites and count overlapping diagonal cases once, including products of sums and dependent binders.\nCoefficient arithmetic cancels positive numeric factors in roots without changing symbolic root branches, recognizes zero sums across different denominators and accepts bare symbolic scalars alongside phases. More large-prime radicals are canonical ([#291]).\nTime-dependent squeezing requires Squeeze(a, r, ϕ, t); ambiguous Squeeze(a, r * t, t) calls now throw.\nIndexed numeric conversion applies parameters even without sums; numeric_average handles constant imaginary terms. qadjoint and inner_adjoint accept averages and phase coefficients.\nSingle-operator expressions compare correctly (2a - a == a), find_operators removes duplicates, and LaTeX renders zero expressions and brackets complex prefactors correctly.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.13.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.13.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)-2","page":"Changelog","text":"Exactness follows the numeric type: 2.0 stays floating point, including when combined with rationals. ComplexF64 coefficients arise only from floating-point input or division by an exact zero.\nInternal coefficient storage replaces Coeff.z with Coeff.slot, accessed through native_scalar, and makes Monomial parametric in its exact tier.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-2","page":"Changelog","text":"Scalar broadcasting, generic reductions and linear algebra for coefficient arrays, including det, adjoints, transposes, inner products and norms. LinearAlgebra is now a hard dependency.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-2","page":"Changelog","text":"Exact arithmetic promotes beyond Int without overflow or conversion to floats. Raw symbolic rational coefficients and moving-frame conjugation preserve exact complex values.\nExact numeric radicals canonicalize when every prime factor is below 2^32; larger factors remain symbolic ([#291]).\nCoefficient and operator arrays render correctly through latexify and text/latex.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.12.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.12.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-3","page":"Changelog","text":"Exact unitary transformations for bosonic and phase-space displacements, Bogoliubov maps, mode mixing, squeezing, spin/Pauli rotations and N-level basis changes, with composition, inversion, rotating/displacement frames and moving-frame gauges.\nFormal operator functions sin(A), cos(A) and Hermitian-only expim(A), with exact structural rewrites and unitary conjugation. Use taylor(expr, 0:n) to lower them to polynomials before numeric conversion.\nget_variables discovers scalar parameters, including filtered and buffer forms of the Symbolics interface.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)-3","page":"Changelog","text":"Rename prefactor to get_prefactor, and unique_ops / unique_ops! to unique_up_to_adjoint / unique_up_to_adjoint!.\nReplace single-term operators(::QAdd) with expression-wide get_operators, which returns unique operators in canonical order. For repeated factors and their order, iterate the expression and read each QTerm.ops.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-3","page":"Changelog","text":"Type-stable QuantumToolbox conversion and standalone coefficient LaTeX rendering.\nImport tensor and ⊗ from TensorCore for QuantumInterface 0.4.4 compatibility; zero-argument Hilbert-space tensor products throw instead of recursing indefinitely.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.11.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.11.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-4","page":"Changelog","text":"Preserve compact expim phases through simplification, substitution, differentiation, unitary transformations, printing and averaging.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.10.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.10.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-4","page":"Changelog","text":"Exact named unitary transformations for Fock, phase-space, spin/Pauli and N-level operators, including composition, inversion and time-dependent gauges.\nCanonical expim phase arithmetic, with substitution, differentiation and exponential/trigonometric conversion; simplification of exact trigonometric and hyperbolic identities.\nA unitary-transformation guide and moving-frame resonator example.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-5","page":"Changelog","text":"dagger(::QField) extends QuantumInterface.dagger and forwards to qadjoint. Use qadjoint or qconj for scalar and symbolic adjoints.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.10.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"Numeric conversion gains extensible QuantumOpticsBase and QuantumToolbox backends.","title":"[v0.10.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-5","page":"Changelog","text":"QuantumOpticsBackend() and QuantumToolboxBackend(), selected through to_numeric(op, h::HilbertSpace, dims; backend, parameter, time_parameter, operators, adjoint_ops, op_type).\nPublic numeric_* hooks for basis construction, operator assembly, materialization and expectation values, allowing downstream numeric backends.\nQuantumToolbox coefficients of the form (p, t) -> value for differentiable solver controls; QuantumOptics rejects this form.\nExpanded precompilation workloads to reduce first-call latency.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)-4","page":"Changelog","text":"Numeric APIs require explicitly loading QuantumOpticsBase or QuantumToolbox; QuantumOpticsBase is now a weak dependency and QuantumInterface a hard dependency.\nTime-dependent conversion returns native TimeDependentSum or QobjEvo objects instead of t -> op(t) closures. It accepts only op_type = nothing or identity.\nProduct-space dims requires one entry per symbolic subspace. Indexed layouts reject duplicate or out-of-range physical slots.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-2","page":"Changelog","text":"Averages of provably Hermitian operators carry real symbolic type, allowing their conjugates to simplify directly.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-6","page":"Changelog","text":"Coefficients fold elementary functions at literal zero to exact values.\nLeaf commutators distinguish different operator names on the same space, agreeing with a*b - b*a.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.9.4]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.9.4]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-6","page":"Changelog","text":"CollectiveNLevelSpace and CollectiveTransition describe identical multilevel systems exactly in the permutation-symmetric subspace, including QuantumOpticsBase ManyBodyBasis conversion. Collective transitions cannot be site-indexed; their diagonal sum is N I, not I.\nOP_COLLECTIVE_TRANSITION = 7, preserving existing OpKind values.\nPublic indexed-average construction and access through indexed_sum(body, indices; non_equal) and get_sum_body.\nOperator/index accessors and rebuilders: operator_index, acts_on(::Index), set_acts_on and rename.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-3","page":"Changelog","text":"Commutation supports both residual terms of collective-transition algebra.\nConstructors reject string names with a clear ArgumentError; names must be Symbols.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-7","page":"Changelog","text":"Index suffixes such as i_2_1 render as i_{2,1}, avoiding invalid LaTeX double subscripts.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Documentation","page":"Changelog","text":"Indexed versus collective descriptions and a finite-N Tavis-Cummings example.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.9.3]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.9.3]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-8","page":"Changelog","text":"Products crossing indexed sums preserve diagonal exclusions when the summed index occurs only in the coefficient, preventing spurious diagonal contributions.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.9.2]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.9.2]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)-5","page":"Changelog","text":"to_numeric defaults to concrete sparse operators consistently across expression forms. Use op_type = dense for dense output or op_type = identity to retain the previous lazy representation.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.9.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.9.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-9","page":"Changelog","text":"Sums of averages containing different operator types print without comparison errors.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.9.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.9.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-7","page":"Changelog","text":"Simultaneous operator and scalar substitution. Replacements are applied once; adjoint rules are inferred unless replace_adjoint = false.\nto_numeric(op, basis; parameter, time_parameter, operators, adjoint_ops, op_type) and vector conversion. Time-dependent parameters may be constants or functions, producing t -> op(t) closures.\nPublic to_num(::Coeff), Coeff and CNum, with API declarations through SciMLPublic.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-4","page":"Changelog","text":"Expressions display in Hilbert-space order, then operator-kind order.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.8.3]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.8.3]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-5","page":"Changelog","text":"average of a many-term operator is now O(n) instead of O(n²): a 500-term sum drops from about 31 ms to 9.6 ms.\nWide symbolic prefactors build in O(n log n) instead of O(n²), with measured speedups of about 12.5× for sums and 17× for products at 800 terms.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.8.2]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.8.2]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-6","page":"Changelog","text":"Non-indexed commutators allocate less and run about twice as fast on measured many-mode workloads, using about five times less memory.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-10","page":"Changelog","text":"Double conjugation returns the original coefficient, and conjugated expressions that become constants fold numerically.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.8.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.8.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-8","page":"Changelog","text":"In-place sum and reduce(+, …) for Vector{QAdd}, integrated with MutableArithmetics. Use sum([terms...]); bare generators retain the generic fold. Measured many-mode construction is about 4–12× faster with 5–11× less memory.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-7","page":"Changelog","text":"Faster hashing of non-indexed operators reduces product, power, commutator and substitution costs without changing canonical results.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.8.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.8.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-(breaking)-6","page":"Changelog","text":"Operator roles share one concrete Op type. Destroy, Create, Transition, Pauli, Spin, Position and Momentum remain constructors, but type dispatch such as isa Destroy must use role predicates (is_destroy, is_transition, etc.) or optype(op)::OpKind.\nTransition fields i, j, ground_state, n_levels become l1, l2, g, nlev; the Pauli/Spin axis field becomes l1.\nOp and Index use interned IDs. Replace direct .name, .range and .sym reads with operator_name, index_name, index_range and index_sym. Subspace indices are Int32; interned IDs must not be serialized across sessions.","title":"Changed (breaking)"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-8","page":"Changelog","text":"Concrete operator storage and faster coefficient arithmetic reduce dispatch and allocations in products, powers and normal ordering.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.7.2]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.7.2]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-9","page":"Changelog","text":"Complex parameters and scalar functions stay on the native polynomial coefficient path, including conjugation. A measured three-cavity SLH construction is about 18× faster.\nSingle-parameter radicals canonicalize, so sqrt(p)^2 folds to p. Roots of composite expressions remain undistributed to preserve branches.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.7.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.7.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-10","page":"Changelog","text":"Cached term hashes and faster operator-reduction checks improve measured product and power workloads by about 1.5–1.8×, with roughly half the allocations.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.7.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.7.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-11","page":"Changelog","text":"make_time_dependent preserves per-site moments inside indexed sums instead of creating one collective unknown.\nConcrete coefficients keep numeric and parameter-polynomial arithmetic native, expanding polynomials canonically. Measured algebra workloads improve by about 2–3.5×.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-11","page":"Changelog","text":"Averaged indexed sums retain their full body and summation scope through symbolic arithmetic; differently scoped sums no longer cancel incorrectly. Added is_indexed_sum; existing sum metadata accessors remain supported.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.5]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.5]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-9","page":"Changelog","text":"@variables η::Number keeps a complex parameter atomic, with symbolic conj(η) and correct adjoint/numeric conversion, avoiding the real/imaginary expansion of ::Complex parameters.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.4]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.4]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-12","page":"Changelog","text":"change_index zeros DoubleIndexedVariable terms marked identical = false when their indices coincide, including occurrences nested in sums and products.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.3]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.3]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-12","page":"Changelog","text":"Time-dependent averages display as ⟨op⟩(t) in text and LaTeX instead of internal variable names.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.2]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.2]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-10","page":"Changelog","text":"index_slot recovers the position of a per-slot index created with (i::Index)(k) without parsing its name.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Added-11","page":"Changelog","text":"Public order_key, term_order_key and qadd_order_key provide reproducible structural ordering, with equal keys exactly when expressions are isequal.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.6.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.6.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-13","page":"Changelog","text":"Averages use symbolic type Number; make_time_dependent lifts them to time-dependent unknowns while preserving average metadata.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.5.2]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.5.2]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-13","page":"Changelog","text":"Commuting expressions with symbolic rational coefficients cancel exactly instead of leaving spurious terms in derived equations. Disjoint-subspace commutators avoid forming cancelling products.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.5.1]","page":"Changelog","text":"","title":"[v0.5.1]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-14","page":"Changelog","text":"Averaged expressions use angle brackets in LaTeX; averaged indexed sums retain their summation prefix.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Fixed-14","page":"Changelog","text":"Diagonal splitting preserves index-inequality constraints, preventing double-counting in nested and collective sums.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Documentation-2","page":"Changelog","text":"Explain explicit bound-index naming and why alpha-equivalent sums are not automatically collected ([#156]).","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#[v0.5.0]","page":"Changelog","text":"Breaking redesign of the algebraic core. The following changes apply when migrating from SecondQuantizedAlgebra v0.4 or the corresponding QuantumCumulants API.","title":"[v0.5.0]"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Renamed","page":"Changelog","text":"Previous API Replacement\nreorder(expr, pairs) assume_distinct_index(expr, pairs)\nsimplify(expr, h) expand_completeness(simplify(expr))\nnormal_order(expr, h) expand_completeness(normal_order(expr))\n@cnumbers a b @variables a b\n@rnumbers a b @variables a::Real b::Real\nParameter(:x) @variables x","title":"Renamed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Removed","page":"Changelog","text":"Removed construct Migration\nClusterSpace Use Index and Σ; pass truncation order to downstream moment expansion, e.g. meanfield(...; order = k).\nSingleSum, DoubleSum, SpecialIndexedTerm Use Σ, returning a QAdd; inspect bound indices and each term's ne constraints.\nNumberedOperator, insert_index Keep indices symbolic; instantiate sites in the numeric layer.\nIndexedAverageSum, IndexedAverageDoubleSum Averaging handles indexed sums through symbolic metadata.\nQMul Arithmetic returns QAdd; iteration yields QTerm => CNum pairs with operator strings in term.ops and constraints in term.ne.\norder_by_index Declare unresolved index inequalities with assume_distinct_index.","title":"Removed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Changed-15","page":"Changelog","text":"Ground-state projectors remain atomic through arithmetic, normal_order and simplify. Use expand_completeness to expand them explicitly.\nsimplify also simplifies scalar coefficients and removes unused sum indices.\nTransition stores its ground state and level count, inferred from its NLevelSpace.\nFree indices with unknown relationships retain physical order; use assume_distinct_index(expr, pairs) to enable sorting.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"changelog/#Migration-example","page":"Changelog","text":"Use @variables instead of @cnumbers, and specify the atom's subsystem when constructing transitions on a product space:\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nha = NLevelSpace(:atom, 2)\nh = hc ⊗ ha\n@variables N Δ\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1)\ni = Index(h, :i, N, ha)\ng(i) = IndexedVariable(:g, i)\nσ(α, β, k) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, α, β, 2), k)\nH = -Δ * a' * a + Σ(g(i) * (a' * σ(1, 2, i) + a * σ(2, 1, i)), i)\n\nThe final 2 in Transition selects the atom's subsystem in h, not its level count. Other constructor names and the commutator, averaging and numeric-conversion entry points remain available.\n\n<!– Links generated by Changelog.jl –>\n\n[v0.5.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.5.0 [v0.5.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.5.1 [v0.5.2]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.5.2 [v0.6.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.0 [v0.6.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.1 [v0.6.2]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.2 [v0.6.3]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.3 [v0.6.4]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.4 [v0.6.5]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.6.5 [v0.7.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.7.0 [v0.7.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.7.1 [v0.7.2]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.7.2 [v0.8.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.8.0 [v0.8.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.8.1 [v0.8.2]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.8.2 [v0.8.3]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.8.3 [v0.9.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.9.0 [v0.9.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.9.1 [v0.9.2]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.9.2 [v0.9.3]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.9.3 [v0.9.4]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.9.4 [v0.10.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.10.0 [v0.10.1]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.10.1 [v0.11.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.11.0 [v0.12.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.12.0 [v0.13.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.13.0 [v0.14.0]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/releases/tag/v0.14.0 [#156]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/issues/156 [#291]: https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl/issues/291","title":"Migration example"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Parametric-Oscillator-in-the-Phase-Space-Representation","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"The degenerate parametric oscillator (DPO), a single bosonic mode driven by parametric pumping at twice its frequency, is the workhorse of optical squeezing.  In the rotating frame at half the pump frequency its effective Hamiltonian is\n\nH = deltabigl(tfracx^2 + p^22bigr)\n  - tfrackappa2(x^2 - p^2)\n= tfracdelta - kappa2x^2\n  + tfracdelta + kappa2p^2\n\nwhere delta is the detuning from half the pump, kappa is the parametric drive strength, and x p = i are canonical quadrature operators.  Below the threshold kappa = delta the oscillator is stable but its ground state is squeezed: one quadrature variance drops below tfrac12 while the other grows above it, with the product saturating the Heisenberg bound.\n\nThis example showcases the PhaseSpace hilbert space, the canonical-commutator engine, and the squeeze transformation of a quadrature pair: every step uses Position and Momentum directly, without ever invoking Fock ladder operators.","title":"Parametric Oscillator in the Phase-Space Representation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Setup","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"using SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nhp = PhaseSpace(:osc)\n@qnumbers x::Position(hp) p::Momentum(hp)\n\n@variables δ κ\n\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Num}:\n δ\n κ\n\nBuilt-in canonical commutator:\n\ncommutator(x, p)\n\nim","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Hamiltonian-and-Heisenberg-dynamics","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"H = (δ - κ) / 2 * x^2 + (δ + κ) / 2 * p^2\n\n-1im * commutator(x, H)\n\n(δ + κ) * p\n\n-1im * commutator(p, H)\n\n(-δ + κ) * x\n\nReading off,\n\ndot x = (delta + kappa)p qquad\ndot p = -(delta - kappa)x\n\nCombining, ddot x = -(delta^2 - kappa^2)x: oscillation at normal-mode frequency varepsilon = sqrtdelta^2 - kappa^2, which softens to zero at the parametric instability threshold kappa = delta.  Above threshold the system runs away exponentially, and the squeezing parameter diverges.","title":"Hamiltonian and Heisenberg dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Squeezed-state-variances-from-a-squeeze-transformation","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"Below threshold, the ground state is a single-mode squeezed vacuum.  Rather than mapping to ladder operators and quoting the Bogoliubov result, we can build the squeeze that diagonalises H directly on the canonical pair: Squeeze on a (Position, Momentum) pair is e^-i r (x p + p x)2, which stretches one quadrature and shrinks the other.\n\n@variables r\n\nU = Squeeze(x, p, r)\n\nconjugate(x, U), conjugate(p, U)\n\n(exp(r) * x, (1 / exp(r)) * p)\n\nConjugating H rescales the two coefficients in opposite directions:\n\nconjugate(H, U)\n\n((1//2)*(exp(r)^2)*δ - (1//2)*(exp(r)^2)*κ) * x * x + (((1//2)*δ) / (exp(r)^2) + ((1//2)*κ) / (exp(r)^2)) * p * p\n\nThe two agree when e^4r = (delta + kappa)(delta - kappa), leaving a bare oscillator tfracvarepsilon2(x^2 + p^2) at the normal-mode frequency varepsilon = sqrtdelta^2 - kappa^2 found above.  Its ground state is the vacuum, so the ground state of H is U0rangle and the variances follow from conjugating the observables:\n\nconjugate(x * x, U), conjugate(p * p, U)\n\n(exp(r)^2 * x * x, (1 / (exp(r)^2)) * p * p)\n\nWith langle x^2rangle_mathrmvac = langle p^2rangle_mathrmvac = tfrac12 and e^2r = sqrt(delta + kappa)(delta - kappa):\n\nlangle x^2 rangle_0\n  = tfrac12sqrtdfracdelta + kappadelta - kappa qquad\nlangle p^2 rangle_0\n  = tfrac12sqrtdfracdelta - kappadelta + kappa\nqquad\nlangle x^2 rangle_0langle p^2 rangle_0 = tfrac14\n\nThe first variance grows without bound as kappa to delta^- (anti-squeezed quadrature), the second shrinks to zero (squeezed quadrature), and the product stays pinned at the Heisenberg-uncertainty minimum because the squeeze preserves the canonical quadrature pair.","title":"Squeezed-state variances from a squeeze transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Numerical-verification","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"The package's numerical backend maps Position and Momentum onto a Fock basis via x = (a + a^dagger)sqrt 2, p = i(a^dagger - a)sqrt 2.  We diagonalise H on a wide Fock basis and read off the ground-state variances directly.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase, LinearAlgebra, Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\nδ_val = 1.0\nn_max = 60\nb_fock = FockBasis(n_max)\n\nκs = range(0.0, 0.95 * δ_val, length = 30)\nvar_x = Float64[]\nvar_p = Float64[]\nfor κ_val in κs\n    subs = Dict(δ => δ_val, κ => κ_val)\n    H_op = dense(to_numeric(substitute(H, subs), b_fock))\n    F = eigen(Hermitian(H_op.data))\n    ψ = Ket(b_fock, F.vectors[:, 1])\n    push!(var_x, real(numeric_average(x * x, ψ)))\n    push!(var_p, real(numeric_average(p * p, ψ)))\nend\n\nth_x(κ_val) = 0.5 * sqrt((δ_val + κ_val) / (δ_val - κ_val))\nth_p(κ_val) = 0.5 * sqrt((δ_val - κ_val) / (δ_val + κ_val))\n\nfig = plot(; layout = (1, 2), size = (820, 360), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nscatter!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ δ_val, var_x; label = L\"\\langle x^2 \\rangle\", marker = :circle)\nscatter!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ δ_val, var_p; label = L\"\\langle p^2 \\rangle\", marker = :diamond)\nplot!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ δ_val, th_x.(κs); linestyle = :dash, color = :black, label = false)\nplot!(fig[1], collect(κs) ./ δ_val, th_p.(κs); linestyle = :dash, color = :black, label = false)\nhline!(fig[1], [0.5]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dot, label = \"vacuum SQL\")\nplot!(fig[1]; xlabel = L\"\\kappa / \\delta\", ylabel = \"quadrature variance\", title = \"Squeezing approaching threshold\", yscale = :log10, legend = :topleft)\n\nscatter!(fig[2], collect(κs) ./ δ_val, var_x .* var_p; label = \"numeric\")\nhline!(fig[2], [0.25]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dash, label = \"1/4 (min)\")\nplot!(fig[2]; xlabel = L\"\\kappa / \\delta\", ylabel = L\"\\langle x^2 \\rangle\\,\\langle p^2 \\rangle\", title = \"Heisenberg uncertainty product\", ylims = (0.24, 0.26), legend = :topleft)\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nBoth quadrature variances follow their closed-form predictions across the entire below-threshold range, and the uncertainty product (right) sits precisely at the Heisenberg-minimum tfrac14 throughout, so the DPO ground state is a minimum-uncertainty squeezed vacuum.  As kappa approaches delta, langle x^2rangle diverges while langle p^2rangle collapses toward zero, the dynamical signature of the parametric instability.","title":"Numerical verification"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/parametric_oscillator/#Phase-space-portrait","page":"Parametric Oscillator","text":"All of the above is summarised cleanly in a single picture.  The classical phase portrait of the rotated-frame Hamiltonian is the family of level curves H(x p) = mathrmconst, an ellipse below threshold that elongates along the squeezed axis as kappa to delta.  Crossing threshold the coefficient (delta - kappa)2 of x^2 flips sign, and the level curves become hyperbolas: bounded orbits give way to unbounded exponential trajectories.  The symbolic determinant computed earlier makes this transition exact:\n\nmathrmeig(M) = pmsqrt-det M = pmsqrtkappa^2 - delta^2\n= begincases\n  pm isqrtdelta^2 - kappa^2  text(oscillation  kappa  deltatext) \n  0  text(degenerate  kappa = deltatext) \n  pmsqrtkappa^2 - delta^2  text(exponential  kappa  deltatext)\nendcases\n\nBelow threshold the ground-state Wigner function of the minimum-uncertainty squeezed vacuum is the Gaussian whose covariance matrix matches the analytic variances:\n\nW(x p) = frac2pi\n  expleft-fracx^22langle x^2rangle_0\n                 - fracp^22langle p^2rangle_0right\nqquad\nlangle x^2rangle_0 langle p^2rangle_0 = tfrac14\n\nOverlaying both objects in a four-panel sweep across the transition makes the squeezing and the instability immediately visible:\n\nH_class(x_val, p_val, κ_val) =\n    (δ_val - κ_val) / 2 * x_val^2 + (δ_val + κ_val) / 2 * p_val^2\nW_gs(x_val, p_val, κ_val) =\n    (2 / π) * exp(\n    -x_val^2 / (2 * th_x(κ_val)) - p_val^2 / (2 * th_p(κ_val)),\n)\n\nκs_show = [0.0, 0.5, 0.95, 1.2]\nxs_plot = -3:0.04:3\nps_plot = -3:0.04:3\n\nfig2 = plot(; layout = (2, 2), size = (820, 760), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nfor (i, κ_val) in enumerate(κs_show)\n    row, col = ((i - 1) ÷ 2) + 1, ((i - 1) % 2) + 1\n    subplot = fig2[(row - 1) * 2 + col]\n    H_grid = [H_class(xv, pv, κ_val) for xv in xs_plot, pv in ps_plot]\n    if κ_val < δ_val\n        W_grid = [W_gs(xv, pv, κ_val) for xv in xs_plot, pv in ps_plot]\n        heatmap!(subplot, xs_plot, ps_plot, W_grid; color = :viridis, label = false)\n        contour!(subplot, xs_plot, ps_plot, H_grid; levels = 10, color = :white, alpha = 0.6, linewidth = 0.8, colorbar = false, label = false)\n    else\n        contour!(subplot, xs_plot, ps_plot, H_grid; levels = vcat(-3:0.5:-0.5, 0.5:0.5:3), color = :firebrick, linewidth = 0.9, colorbar = false, label = false)\n        contour!(subplot, xs_plot, ps_plot, H_grid; levels = [0.0], color = :black, linewidth = 1.5, colorbar = false, label = false)\n        annotate!(subplot, 0, -2.6, text(\"unstable, no GS\", :firebrick, 10))\n    end\n    plot!(subplot; aspect_ratio = :equal, xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"p\", title = \"κ/δ = $(κ_val)\", xlims = (-3, 3), ylims = (-3, 3), legend = false)\nend\nfig2\n\n(Image: )\n\nTop-left (kappa = 0): vacuum, circular Wigner blob, circular orbits. Top-right and bottom-left: the Wigner ellipse squeezes along p and stretches along x as kappa climbs toward delta, with the classical orbits doing the same.  Bottom-right (above threshold): the saddle separatrix H = 0 (black) splits phase space into four hyperbolic sectors; the orbits run off to infinity along the unstable manifold.  Everything in this figure (the threshold condition, the variances, the Wigner shape) came out of a canonical Position/Momentum pair, the built-in commutator engine and one Squeeze, with no manual translation to ladder operators required.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Phase-space portrait"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Truncated-Wigner-for-a-Driven-Dissipative-Bose-Hubbard-Dimer","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"The truncated Wigner approximation (TWA) maps a driven-dissipative quantum system onto an ensemble of stochastic classical trajectories in phase space. It keeps the leading quantum fluctuations that mean-field theory throws away, at the cost of a classical Langevin equation rather than a full master equation. TWA earns its keep precisely when the Hilbert space is too large to integrate directly: here we take two coupled bosonic modes, whose joint Fock space grows as the product of the per-mode cutoffs, in a regime where the drive populates each mode with many photons.\n\nThe physical system is a Bose-Hubbard dimer: two lossy Kerr modes a_1 a_2 connected by tunneling J, with a coherent drive F on the first site. In the frame rotating at the drive frequency (detuning Delta),\n\nH = -Delta sum_j a_j^dagger a_j\n  + fracU2 sum_j a_j^dagger a_j^dagger a_j a_j\n  - J(a_1^dagger a_2 + a_2^dagger a_1)\n  + F(a_1^dagger + a_1)\n\nwith single-photon loss kappa on each site (Lindblad jump operators L_j = sqrtkappaa_j). This model is bistable: over a range of drives the mean-field equations have two coexisting stable amplitudes. We will see that mean-field, launched from vacuum, gets trapped on the low-amplitude branch, while TWA reveals the quantum fluctuations that drive switching to the high-amplitude branch. That switching is invisible to any single classical trajectory.\n\nThe star of this example is normal_to_symmetric. Wigner-function moments are exactly the symmetrically-ordered operator averages, so the TWA c-number equations follow from one uniform recipe:\n\nrewrite the Heisenberg drift in symmetric order, take the average, factorize into a product of first moments.","title":"Truncated Wigner for a Driven-Dissipative Bose-Hubbard Dimer"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Setup","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"using SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nusing Symbolics, SymbolicUtils\n\nh1 = FockSpace(:site1)\nh2 = FockSpace(:site2)\nh = h1 ⊗ h2\n\n@qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) b::Destroy(h, 2)\n@variables Δ U J F κ\n\nH = -Δ * (a' * a + b' * b) +\n    (U / 2) * (a' * a' * a * a + b' * b' * b * b) -\n    J * (a' * b + b' * a) +\n    F * (a' + a)\n\nF * a + F * a' - J * a * b' - Δ * a' * a - J * a' * b - Δ * b' * b + (1//2)*U * a' * a' * a * a + (1//2)*U * b' * b' * b * b","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#The-Wigner-shift-comes-out-of-normal_to_symmetric","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"The whole approximation hinges on one algebraic fact. The normal-ordered Kerr product, rewritten in symmetric (Weyl) order, picks up a shift:\n\nnormal_to_symmetric(a' * a * a)\n\n-a + a' * a * a\n\nThe trailing -a is the Wigner correction. Under TWA it becomes the famous (alpha^2 - 1)alpha term. The same mechanism corrects observables: the occupation operator a^dagger a maps to alpha^2 - tfrac12,\n\nnormal_to_symmetric(a' * a)\n\n-1//2 + a' * a\n\nso a physical photon number is recovered from the phase-space cloud as langle n_jrangle = langle alpha_j^2rangle - tfrac12.","title":"The Wigner shift comes out of normal_to_symmetric"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#From-operators-to-a-c-number-drift","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"The TWA drift for each mode is the Heisenberg-Langevin equation, symmetrized, averaged, then factorized to first order (the mean-field truncation). The factorization langle a^dagger a arangle to langle a^daggerrangle langle arangle langle arangle is the one step SecondQuantizedAlgebra does not ship as a built-in, so we spell it out: walk the averaged expression and split every multi-operator moment into a product of single-operator averages.\n\nfunction factorize_meanfield(expr)\n    rule = SymbolicUtils.PassThrough(\n        function (x)\n            is_average(x) || return nothing\n            q = undo_average(x)\n            terms = collect(q.arguments)\n            length(terms) == 1 || return nothing\n            ops = first(terms)[1].ops\n            length(ops) <= 1 && return nothing\n            return SymbolicUtils.unwrap(prod(average(op) for op in ops))\n        end,\n    )\n    return SymbolicUtils.Postwalk(rule)(SymbolicUtils.unwrap(expr))\nend\n\nfactorize_meanfield (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe loss enters as the standard drift -tfrackappa2 a_j of a lowering operator. The mean-field and TWA drifts differ only by the normal_to_symmetric call:\n\nheisenberg(o) = -1im * commutator(o, H) - (κ / 2) * o\n\nmeanfield_drift(o) = factorize_meanfield(average(heisenberg(o)))\ntwa_drift(o) = factorize_meanfield(average(normal_to_symmetric(heisenberg(o))))\n\ntwa_drift(a)\n\n-F*im - (1//2)*⟨a⟩*κ + J*⟨b⟩*im + (U + Δ)*⟨a⟩*im - U*⟨a'⟩*(⟨a⟩^2)*im\n\nSubtracting the two confirms the surplus is exactly the Wigner shift +iUlangle arangle:\n\nSymbolics.expand(twa_drift(a) - meanfield_drift(a))\n\nU*⟨a⟩*im","title":"From operators to a c-number drift"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Porting-to-a-ModelingToolkit-system","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"The stochastic variables are the averages themselves. make_time_dependent lifts langle a_jrangle into ModelingToolkit unknowns langle a_jrangle(t), and the conjugate average langle a_j^daggerrangle becomes mathrmconj(langle a_jrangle(t)). We keep the unknowns complex and let StochasticDiffEq carry complex state, so no real/imaginary split is needed.\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\nαa = make_time_dependent(average(a), t)\nαb = make_time_dependent(average(b), t)\n\n⟨b⟩(t)\n\nSecondQuantizedAlgebra represents the imaginary unit inside an average as a symbolic constant; fold it to a numeric im, and map the averages to the time-dependent unknowns, so ModelingToolkit sees a plain complex ODE.\n\nfunction to_rhs(drift)\n    numeric = Symbolics.substitute(drift, Dict(SymbolicUtils.unwrap(Symbolics.IM) => 1im))\n    return Symbolics.substitute(\n        numeric,\n        Dict(\n            average(a) => αa, average(b) => αb,\n            average(a') => conj(αa), average(b') => conj(αb),\n        ),\n    )\nend\n\neqs = [D(αa) ~ to_rhs(twa_drift(a)), D(αb) ~ to_rhs(twa_drift(b))]\n\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Equation}:\n Differential(t, 1)(⟨a⟩(t)) ~ (0 - 1im)*F + (0 + 1im)*J*⟨b⟩(t) + (0 + 1im)*(U + Δ)*⟨a⟩(t) - (1//2)*⟨a⟩(t)*κ + (0 - 1im)*U*(⟨a⟩(t)^2)*conj(⟨a⟩(t))\n Differential(t, 1)(⟨b⟩(t)) ~ (0 + 1im)*J*⟨a⟩(t) + (0 + 1im)*(U + Δ)*⟨b⟩(t) - (1//2)*⟨b⟩(t)*κ + (0 - 1im)*U*(⟨b⟩(t)^2)*conj(⟨b⟩(t))\n\nAssemble the (deterministic) drift system. Its generated right-hand side is reused below as the drift of the stochastic problem.\n\n@named twa = System(eqs, t, [αa, αb], [Δ, U, J, F, κ])\ntwa = mtkcompile(twa)\n\nModel twa:\nEquations (2):\n  2 standard: see equations(twa)\nUnknowns (2): see unknowns(twa)\n  ⟨b⟩(t)\n  ⟨a⟩(t)\nParameters (5): see parameters(twa)\n  Δ\n  U\n  J\n  F\n  κ","title":"Porting to a ModelingToolkit system"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#The-noise","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"Vacuum single-photon loss contributes a diagonal Wigner diffusion. In the complex representation each mode gets an independent complex noise of amplitude sqrtkappa2. This is the one number fixed by convention rather than by the algebra: it is set so that a bare lossy mode relaxes to the vacuum width langlealpha^2rangle = tfrac12, matching the -tfrac12 in the occupation map above.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, StochasticDiffEq\nusing SymbolicIndexingInterface: getp\n\ngetκ = getp(twa, κ)\n\nfunction noise!(du, u, p, t)\n    amp = sqrt(getκ(p) / 2)\n    du[1] = amp\n    du[2] = amp\n    return nothing\nend\n\nnoise! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"The noise"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Mean-field:-two-coexisting-branches","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"The mean-field limit drops both quantum corrections (the Wigner shift and the noise): it is the deterministic drift built from meanfield_drift, one equation per mode. We integrate it to steady state from two different starts, from vacuum and from a large-amplitude seed.\n\nusing Statistics\n\nparams = Dict(Δ => 2.0, U => 0.1, J => 0.6, F => 3.0, κ => 1.0)\ntspan = (0.0, 400.0)\n\nmf_eqs = [D(αa) ~ to_rhs(meanfield_drift(a)), D(αb) ~ to_rhs(meanfield_drift(b))]\n@named mf = System(mf_eqs, t, [αa, αb], [Δ, U, J, F, κ])\nmf = mtkcompile(mf)\n\nmfsolve(α0) = solve(ODEProblem(mf, merge(Dict(αa => α0, αb => α0), params), tspan), Tsit5())\nαlo = mfsolve(0.0im).u[end]        # from vacuum\nαhi = mfsolve(5.0 + 0im).u[end]    # from a bright seed\n(abs2(αlo[2]), abs2(αhi[2]))       # site-2 occupation on each branch\n\n(3.730696238660285, 21.486880674065695)\n\nTwo stable amplitudes coexist for the same parameters. A single classical trajectory, released from vacuum, can only ever find the lower one.","title":"Mean-field: two coexisting branches"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Solving-the-TWA-ensemble","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"TWA replaces that one trajectory by an ensemble. Each realization starts from a vacuum-sampled initial condition (the Wigner distribution of the vacuum is a complex Gaussian with langlealpha^2rangle = tfrac12) and is driven by the loss noise. The drift is reused verbatim from the deterministic problem via SDEFunction.\n\nprob = ODEProblem(twa, merge(Dict(αa => 0.0im, αb => 0.0im), params), tspan)\nsdeprob = SDEProblem(SDEFunction(prob.f.f, noise!), prob.u0, tspan, prob.p)\n\nvacuum_start(prob, ctx) = remake(prob; u0 = randn(ComplexF64, 2) ./ sqrt(2))\n\nntraj = 5_000\nsaveat = 0.0:2.0:400.0\nensemble = EnsembleProblem(sdeprob; prob_func = vacuum_start)\nsim = solve(ensemble, LambaEulerHeun(), EnsembleThreads(); trajectories = ntraj, saveat)\n\nEach trajectory is a phase-space sample (alpha_1(t) alpha_2(t)). Physical occupations are the ensemble means, minus the vacuum tfrac12:\n\ntraj = [reduce(hcat, s.u) for s in sim.u]\nocc(mode, i) = mean(abs2(traj[k][mode, i]) for k in 1:ntraj) - 0.5\nn1_twa = [occ(1, i) for i in eachindex(saveat)]\nn2_twa = [occ(2, i) for i in eachindex(saveat)]","title":"Solving the TWA ensemble"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#Occupations:-TWA-versus-the-mean-field-branches","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"using Plots, LaTeXStrings\ngr()\n\nfig = plot(; layout = (1, 2), size = (720, 330), margin = 5Plots.mm)\nfor (col, (nt, mode, lab)) in enumerate(((n1_twa, 1, \"site 1\"), (n2_twa, 2, \"site 2\")))\n    subplot = fig[col]\n    hline!(subplot, [abs2(αlo[mode])]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dash, label = \"mean-field (lower)\")\n    hline!(subplot, [abs2(αhi[mode])]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dot, label = \"mean-field (upper)\")\n    plot!(subplot, collect(saveat), nt; xlabel = \"t\", ylabel = \"⟨n⟩\", title = lab, color = :firebrick, label = \"TWA ⟨n⟩\", legend = col == 1 ? :right : false)\nend\nfig\n\n(Image: )\n\nMean-field launched from vacuum sits forever on the lower branch (dashed). The TWA ensemble mean (red) climbs well above it: quantum fluctuations kick a growing fraction of trajectories over the barrier onto the upper branch (dotted), and the ensemble average settles between the two. Nothing in the deterministic dynamics from vacuum could produce this.","title":"Occupations: TWA versus the mean-field branches"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/truncated_wigner/#The-phase-space-(Wigner)-distribution","page":"Truncated Wigner Approximation","text":"Because every trajectory is a phase-space point, the ensemble at the final time is a sampled Wigner quasi-distribution. We bin it with FHist and overlay both mean-field fixed points.\n\nusing FHist\n\nre2 = [real(traj[k][2, end]) for k in 1:ntraj]\nim2 = [imag(traj[k][2, end]) for k in 1:ntraj]\nhist = Hist2D((re2, im2); binedges = (-8:0.2:8, -8:0.2:8))\n\nfig2 = plot(; size = (940, 820), xlabel = L\"\\mathrm{Re}\\,\\alpha_2\", ylabel = L\"\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\alpha_2\", aspect_ratio = :equal, title = \"Wigner samples, site 2 (t = 200)\", xlims = (-8, 8), ylims = (-8, 8), xticks = -8:2:8, yticks = -8:2:8, margin = 5Plots.mm)\n\nMakie and Plots use opposite matrix-axis conventions for heatmaps.\n\ncounts = Matrix(adjoint(bincounts(hist)))\nheatmap!(fig2, bincenters(hist)..., counts; color = :magma, colorbar = false, label = false)\nscatter!(fig2, [real(αlo[2])], [imag(αlo[2])]; color = :cyan, marker = :xcross, markersize = 16, label = \"mean-field (lower)\")\nscatter!(fig2, [real(αhi[2])], [imag(αhi[2])]; color = :lime, marker = :cross, markersize = 16, label = \"mean-field (upper)\")\nplot!(fig2; legend = :bottomright)\nfig2\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe distribution is unmistakably bimodal: one lobe sits on each mean-field branch, joined by a bridge of trajectories caught mid-switch. This is the picture mean-field cannot draw. A direct master-equation solve would need a Fock cutoff comfortably above the upper-branch occupation on both modes, a joint space of many hundreds of states, whereas TWA needed only classical trajectories.\n\nEvery ingredient downstream of the Hamiltonian, the drift, the observable map langle nrangle = langlealpha^2rangle - tfrac12, and even the sign of the Kerr shift, came out of symmetric ordering plus average, with no hand conversion from operators to c-numbers.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"The phase-space (Wigner) distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"This page is for anyone who changes code under src/ or ext/. It describes how the package is put together and lists the invariants a change must keep. The invariants matter most: many of them look like missed optimizations or arbitrary choices, and several are not covered by any test.\n\nPages = [\"architecture.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Overview","page":"Architecture","text":"An expression flows through four layers.\n\nconstructors ──► Op ──*,+──► QAdd  (canonical polynomial, Dict{QTerm, Coeff})\n                                │\n                 sin, cos, expim│                     taylor(expr, 0:n)\n                                ▼                            │\n                              QExpr (formal, ordered tree) ──┘ back to QAdd\n                                │\n                    average     ▼          to_numeric / numeric_average\n             QAdd ──────► symbolic scalars           QAdd ──────► backend operator\n                          (SymbolicUtils terms)                   (package extension)\n\nEvery operator is the single concrete struct Op, and its role (annihilation, transition, Pauli, …) is a kind tag. A polynomial expression is a QAdd: a dictionary from terms to coefficients, where a term is an ordered operator product together with the index inequalities that scope it. Products and sums are brought into canonical form the moment they are built. Coefficients are Coeff values: exact numbers, parameter polynomials, or raw SymbolicUtils expressions. Formal operator functions live in a separate tree, QExpr, which never enters the polynomial code. Averages turn a QAdd into SymbolicUtils terms for equation-of-motion packages such as QuantumCumulants.jl, and the numeric layer converts a QAdd to matrices through a backend defined in a package extension. Maps on operators, SuperOperator, Lindbladian and SuperExpr, form a parallel layer whose two sides are canonical operator products.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Code-map","page":"Architecture","text":"Directory Contents\nsrc/operators/ Hilbert spaces, the Op struct and its role constructors, and the operator hooks every algebra rule goes through\nsrc/expressions/ coefficients (exact numbers, polynomials, Coeff), QTerm, QAdd, QExpr, Index and Σ, name interning\nsrc/algebra/ the canonicalization pipeline, simplify/normal_order/substitute, sum accumulation, Weyl ordering, unitary transformations\nsrc/superoperators/ SuperOperator, Lindbladian, SuperExpr, their shared index scope and frame transforms\nsrc/average.jl average, undo_average and the average and sum nodes\nsrc/numeric/ the backend-neutral conversion core, superoperator conversion and the backend interface\next/ the QuantumOpticsBase and QuantumToolbox backends\nsrc/printing/ Unicode and LaTeX rendering","title":"Code map"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Invariants","page":"Architecture","text":"Each invariant states the rule, the reason, and the test that pins it, or \"not tested\" where a break would go unnoticed.","title":"Invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Canonical-form","page":"Architecture","text":"Every product is canonical when it is built. The result of * is already sorted, reduced and commuted, so normal_order and simplify are idempotent on it and like terms collect at once. A new code path that builds terms must end in the canonicalization pipeline. Pinned by test/algebra/.\n\nRewrites come in three classes. Local identities on a provably shared site (transition composition, Pauli products) always fire. Commutation swaps fire only when the site relation is known. Rewrites that multiply the number of terms, such as completeness, fire only on request. This is why the site comparison distinguishes Equal from Undetermined, and why it must keep doing so.\n\nThe pipeline runs reduce, then commute, then reduce. The first reduce composes transitions and Pauli operators, which compose rather than commute. Commutation then handles bosonic, spin and quadrature pairs, and the final reduce catches compositions that a commutator residual creates next to a neighbour.\n\nThe ground-state projector sigma^gg stays atomic. Rewriting it as 1 - sum_k neq g sigma^kk multiplies a product with k such factors into n^k terms before anything can cancel, and a mean-field solver would then carry n - 1 dependent moments instead of one. expand_completeness is the explicit handle. Pinned by test/algebra/simplify_test.jl.\n\nFree indices stay undetermined. Two operators on the same space with different free indices may or may not act on the same site, so no rule fires between them and they keep their written order. Only a Σ or assume_distinct_index resolves them. Pinned by test/fundamentals/indexing_test.jl.\n\nThe canonical form does not depend on declaration order. Operator and index names are interned to integer ids in insertion order, so ordering by id would make the canonical form depend on which name was declared first. All orderings go through the alphabetical rank of the name instead. Not tested.\n\nA pass that forks must copy. The pipeline streams terms through nested passes without materializing intermediate lists. A pass with one output may mutate the operator vector in place; a pass with several outputs copies it for every branch but one. A missed copy gives two terms that share one vector, a silent aliasing bug. Not tested.\n\nDiagonal splitting substitutes before sorting. When a product meets a summed index, the term splits into the off-diagonal part and the part where the two indices coincide. The coinciding part substitutes the index in the unsorted product: sorting first can put two same-site operators in the wrong physical order, so that sigma_j^12sigma_i^21 at i = j would give sigma^22 instead of sigma^11. Pinned by test/fundamentals/indexing_test.jl.\n\nEvery coincidence is counted once. A summed index that may equal several free indices takes the first one it equals, so the diagonal for the k-th excludes the earlier ones. Several binders are split one at a time, each inside the parts of the previous one; splitting each against the original term would count a joint coincidence such as i = k j = l once per binder. Pinned by test/symbolic/index_cancellation_test.jl.","title":"Canonical form"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Terms-and-indices","page":"Architecture","text":"A term is its operators together with its inequalities. Like terms merge only when both the operator product and the inequality constraints match, so sum_i a_i and sum_i neq j a_i remain separate entries. Zero coefficients are never stored. Pinned by test/fundamentals/indexing_test.jl.\n\nEvery stored inequality refers to something the term can see. A constraint must involve an index of one of the term's operators, of its coefficient, or of the enclosing sum. The QAdd constructor drops the others, since they would only prevent like terms from merging. Pinned by test/symbolic/index_cancellation_test.jl.\n\nThe algebra never creates an Index. Every index in an output was constructed by the user, so the user can match on it, substitute it and give it limits. Hence a product of two sums over the same bound index throws instead of renaming one of them, and sum_i sigma_i + sum_j sigma_j stays two terms. Pinned by test/fundamentals/indexing_test.jl.\n\nBinary + copies; reductions do not. Both operands of + are still held by the caller, so + copies the accumulated dictionary and a chain of n additions costs O(n^2). sum and reduce(+, …) over an array instead thread one accumulator through the fold and build the result once; the result must stay identical to the chain of +. Products are left as repeated *, because their cost is the distributive expansion itself, which an accumulator cannot save. Pinned by test/algebra/algebra_test.jl.\n\nInterned ids never leave the session. The name and range tables in intern.jl are written only at construction, from one thread, and their ids follow insertion order. Ids are never serialized, and no SymbolicUtils symbol built from them may be cached at precompile time: it would be a stale copy of the symbol the new session hashconses. Not tested.","title":"Terms and indices"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Expression-layers","page":"Architecture","text":"QExpr never touches the polynomial path. Polynomial work always stays in canonical QAdd leaves, and a formal function is never expanded implicitly. taylor is the only way back to QAdd, and average, to_numeric and numeric_average refuse an unlowered QExpr. A change to QExpr must not cost the polynomial algebra anything. Pinned by test/algebra/operator_function_*.","title":"Expression layers"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Superoperators","page":"Architecture","text":"Maps are not QFields. Were SuperOperator or Lindbladian a QField, every operator method would accept them, and a * L or average(L) would return nonsense instead of an error. The three map types share the internal Union AnyMap, since AbstractSuperOperator belongs to QuantumInterface. Pinned by test/numeric/superoperator_refusal_test.jl.\n\nBoth sides share one scope. In sum_i sigma_i rho sigma_i^dagger the same i binds both sides, so a term stores one inequality list and one summation scope for the pair. The diagonal split runs on the joint unsorted term, and every binder the result no longer mentions is counted, repeatedly, since eliminating one can make another's count known. Without this, transpose(Σ(dissipator(a_i'), i))(1) would be 1 - N instead of zero. Pinned by test/symbolic/superoperator_indexed_test.jl.\n\nAmbiguous indices throw. Composing maps that bind the same index, applying a map to an operator with that index free, or a side carrying its own Σ has no unique reading, and the algebra never mints an index to choose one. Pinned by test/symbolic/superoperator_indexed_test.jl.\n\nLindbladian equality is structural. Normalization moves jump constants into the Hamiltonian, numeric factors and unimodular phases into the rate, and drops the identity part of H, so equal spellings of one generator compare equal. A cross term is not expanded: dissipator(a + b) and its four-channel expansion are unequal as Lindbladians and equal as SuperOperators, which keeps the user's channels through printing and frames. σᵍᵍ stays atomic here as well. Pinned by test/algebra/lindbladian_test.jl and test/algebra/superoperator_structural_test.jl.\n\nGKSL extraction is exact by default. Float maps carry roundoff that grows as the basis approaches linear dependence, so no fixed tolerance both keeps small genuine terms and accepts every valid float map; rtol is an opt-in, measured in map units so that rescaling the basis cannot hide a term. Trace preservation is checked first, so a trace defect is reported as one. Pinned by test/algebra/gksl_coordinates_test.jl.\n\nChannel order is reproducible. channels(L) sorts by channel_order_key, since Dict order depends on hashing and printing and positional access must not. Pinned by test/algebra/gksl_coordinates_test.jl.\n\nThe return type never depends on values. A side with a formal function makes a SuperExpr, and a SuperExpr whose formal part is empty stays one until SuperOperator(X) is called. Pinned by @inferred checks in test/algebra/lindbladian_test.jl.","title":"Superoperators"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Coefficients","page":"Architecture","text":"Complex{Num} exists only at the boundary. Coefficients are Coeff internally; Complex{Num} appears only where values leave the package, as in get_prefactor or to_num. In particular, average must not build a Complex{Num}: Symbolics turns it into an opaque complex(re, im) node that simplify cannot cancel and that generated code cannot call. Pinned by test/symbolic/average_test.jl.\n\nExactness is a type. An exact value is a GaussianRational, and a ComplexF64 is always inexact. Exact with exact stays exact, and anything combined with a float becomes a float, so no operation has to inspect a value. Pinned by test/symbolic/coefficients_test.jl.\n\nOne tier per value. Exact arithmetic runs on Int and is redone on BigInt when it overflows, so its steps must not mutate their inputs. A result returns to Int when it fits, so the same value never exists in both tiers. The redo is not tested for mutation.\n\nRadicals are canonical. A radical is a product of prime square roots (or other roots) with exponents in (0 1), so sqrt6, sqrt2sqrt3 and sqrt242 are the same value with the same hash. Pinned by test/symbolic/coefficients_test.jl.\n\nOnly plain products become compound atoms. A function of an atom, or of a product of real atoms such as cos(ω*t), is a polynomial atom, which keeps its arithmetic on the fast path. Scaled or summed arguments stay raw, since sin(2θ) must reach the trigonometric reduction and exp(-r) must cancel 1/exp(r). Pinned by test/symbolic/reduction_test.jl and test/algebra/unitary_transformations_test.jl.\n\nGaussianRational is not a Number. Methods of a new Number subtype on ==, hash and arithmetic invalidate SymbolicUtils code compiled for abstract numbers, which slowed its simplify by about a fifth. Not tested.","title":"Coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Averages","page":"Architecture","text":"An average is real when its operator is Hermitian. The average and sum nodes override maketerm to derive their type from the operator, since the default rebuild in substitute would silently turn a real average back into a complex one. The index scope of a sum node is an argument, not metadata, because SymbolicUtils ignores metadata in isequal and two sums with different scopes would otherwise cancel. Pinned by test/symbolic/average_test.jl.","title":"Averages"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Orderings","page":"Architecture","text":"Three orderings, three purposes. The site comparison decides which neighbours may swap and is deliberately partial. The display order in sorted_arguments is total but may tie distinct operators. Only order_key is faithful to identity, order_key(a) == order_key(b) exactly when isequal(a, b), and downstream packages use it to choose canonical representatives. None of them is hash, which is not stable across Julia versions. Pinned by test/fundamentals/ordering_test.jl.","title":"Orderings"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Unitary-transformations","page":"Architecture","text":"The affine action is the single representation. A UnitaryTransform stores X mapsto L X + c as blocks, one per algebra: bosonic, phase space, spin and Pauli, finite-level. Each block is inverted with the formula of its own group, so inv never inverts a symbolic matrix, and the rewrite rules are compiled from the action. Caller-supplied matrices are checked for structure but never for unitarity, so no tolerance or projection is applied. Pinned by test/algebra/unitary_*.","title":"Unitary transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"dev/architecture/#Types-and-performance","page":"Architecture","text":"Struct fields are concrete, and Op and Index are bits types. Vector{Op} is then an inline buffer that is hashed and compared on integers. Pinned by test/quality/quality_test.jl.\n\nThe numeric core is backend-neutral. src/numeric/ never names a backend type, and the type of an assembled operator does not depend on the number of terms. Pinned by test/numeric/backend_interface_test.jl and JET.","title":"Types and performance"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 25\">SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Symbolic algebra for quantum operators</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">Build and manipulate second-quantized operator expressions in Julia with canonical arithmetic, indexed sums, and exact transformations.</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"manual/getting_started/\">Get started</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"examples/schrieffer_wolff/\">Examples</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/qojulia/SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl\">View on GitHub</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img src=\"assets/logo.svg\" alt=\"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl logo\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">[·,·]</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Canonical operator algebra</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Apply commutation relations, local identities, normal ordering, and simplification directly to symbolic operator expressions.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⊗</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Multiple quantum algebras</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Combine bosonic, N-level, Pauli, spin, and phase-space operators in composite Hilbert spaces.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">Σ</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Indexed many-body systems</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Work with symbolic sums and indexed operator families, including automatic diagonal splitting and free-index constraints.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🌀</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Unitary transformations</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Construct displacement, rotation, squeezing, Bogoliubov, and generator-derived unitary transformations symbolically.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-feature\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">\n<img src=\"https://sciml.ai/assets/favicon.png\" alt=\"SciML logo\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\">\n</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Numerical bridges</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Convert symbolic operators to QuantumOpticsBase or QuantumToolbox representations when numerical evaluation is needed.</p>\n  </div>\n</section>\n</div>\n\nSecondQuantizedAlgebra.jl provides the noncommutative symbolic layer used to build and transform quantum-operator expressions before numerical simulation. The algebra originated in QuantumCumulants.jl and was separated into a reusable package as its scope expanded (Plankensteiner et al., 2022).","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Install the package with Julia's package manager:\n\npkg> add SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nConstruct a composite cavity–atom space and manipulate its operators directly:\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\n\nhc = FockSpace(:cavity)\nha = NLevelSpace(:atoms, 2)\nh = hc ⊗ ha\n\n@qnumbers b::Destroy(h, 1)\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\n\n@variables g Δ\nH = Δ * b' * b + g * (b * σ(2, 1) + b' * σ(1, 2))\n\ncommutator(H, b)\n-Δ * b - g * σ₁₂","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Averages-and-numerics","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"Measured quantities are expectation values, and the equations of motion of an open or closed system are usually written for them. The Heisenberg equation turns into fracddtlangle X rangle = i langle H X rangle, a relation between averages of operator products. Consider a driven Jaynes–Cummings model,\n\nH = Delta a^dagger a + g left(a^dagger sigma^ge + sigma^eg aright) + eta left(a + a^daggerright)\n\nPages = [\"averages_numerics.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Averages and numerics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Averages","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"average maps an operator expression to its expectation value. Averaging is linear: it distributes over sums and pulls out the coefficients, so the right-hand side of the Heisenberg equation for a becomes a sum of averages.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, (:g, :e))\na = Destroy(h, :a, 1)\nσ(i, j) = Transition(h, :σ, i, j, 2)\n@variables Δ g η t\n\nH = Δ * a' * a + g * (a' * σ(:g, :e) + σ(:e, :g) * a) + η * (a + a')\nda = average(1im * commutator(H, a))\n-im*η - ⟨\u001b[34mσ₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*g*im - ⟨\u001b[34ma\u001b[39m⟩*im*Δ\n\nAn average is an ordinary symbolic scalar, so it takes part in Symbolics arithmetic. undo_average recovers the operator expression.\n\nundo_average(da)\n(-η)im + (-Δ)im * a + (-g)im * σ₁₂","title":"Averages"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Conjugation","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"The equation for langle a^dagger rangle is the complex conjugate of the one for langle a rangle. Taken with conj, the conjugate of an average stays formal. inner_adjoint moves it inside, where it becomes the average of the adjoint operator, langle X rangle^* = langle X^dagger rangle, so the conjugate equation is again written in averages.\n\ninner_adjoint(da)\nim*η + ⟨\u001b[34mσ₂₁\u001b[39m⟩*g*im + ⟨\u001b[34ma'\u001b[39m⟩*im*Δ","title":"Conjugation"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Time-dependence","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"To integrate the equations, every average becomes an unknown function of time.\n\nmake_time_dependent(da, t)\n-im*η - ⟨a⟩(t)*im*Δ - ⟨σ₁₂⟩(t)*g*im","title":"Time dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Operator-bases","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"The equation for langle a rangle involves langle sigma^ge rangle, whose own equation brings in products such as langle a sigma^ee rangle. Closing this hierarchy starts from a set of operators to track. fundamental_operators gives the generators of the algebra on a space, and find_operators all products up to a given order, each counted once together with its adjoint.\n\nfundamental_operators(h)\n3-element Vector{Op}:\n a\n σ₁₂\n σ₂₂\n\nfind_operators(h, 2)\n8-element Vector{Union{Op, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}}:\n a\n σ₁₂\n σ₂₂\n a * a\n a * σ₁₂\n a * σ₂₂\n a' * a\n a * σ₂₁\n\nunique_up_to_adjoint applies the same reduction to a list of your own.\n\nunique_up_to_adjoint([a, a', a' * a, σ(:g, :e), σ(:e, :g)])\n3-element Vector{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}:\n a\n a' * a\n σ₁₂","title":"Operator bases"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Many-emitters","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"With N atoms in the cavity, each with its own coupling g_i, the interaction becomes a Σ over the atoms. The average of a sum is a sum of averages over the same index, with the same excluded sites, so the equations keep N symbolic.\n\nusing SecondQuantizedAlgebra\nh = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2)\na = Destroy(h, :a, 1)\n@variables N\ni = Index(h, :i, N, 2)\nσ(k, l) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l, 2), i)\ng = IndexedVariable(:g, i)\n\nH = Σ(g * (a' * σ(1, 2) + a * σ(2, 1)), i)\naverage(H)\nΣ(i=1:N) (⟨\u001b[34ma * σ_i₂₁\u001b[39m⟩*g(i) + ⟨\u001b[34ma' * σ_i₁₂\u001b[39m⟩*g(i))","title":"Many emitters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Numerical-conversion","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"to_numeric turns an operator expression into a matrix of QuantumOpticsBase.jl or QuantumToolbox.jl, ready for their solvers. The numerical side is an extension: it becomes available once one of the two packages is loaded, and the backend is chosen with QuantumOpticsBackend or QuantumToolboxBackend. The portable form takes the symbolic Hilbert space and one truncation per factor, a photon cutoff for a mode. Every parameter needs a value.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase\nbackend = QuantumOpticsBackend()\nparameter = Dict(Δ => 0.5, g => 0.1, η => 0.2)\nHn = to_numeric(H, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter)\nsize(Hn)\n(22, 22)","title":"Numerical conversion"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Bases-and-levels","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"A basis of the backend can be passed in place of the space and its truncation, and then also fixes the backend. Symbolic level names map to basis indices in the order of the levels of the NLevelSpace, so g is the first basis state and e the second.\n\nbf = FockBasis(10)\nbn = NLevelBasis(2)\nb = bf ⊗ bn\nto_numeric(σ(:g, :e), b) == embed(b, 2, transition(bn, 1, 2))\ntrue","title":"Bases and levels"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Time-dependent-parameters","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"Parameters that change in time go into time_parameter, as functions of time. The result is then the backend's time-dependent operator, a TimeDependentSum for QuantumOptics.\n\nHt = to_numeric(H, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter = Dict(Δ => 0.5, g => 0.1), time_parameter = Dict(η => t -> 0.2 * sin(t)))\nHt isa TimeDependentSum\ntrue\n\nAn explicit time dependence of the coefficients, such as a phase from a rotating frame, needs the time variable itself, given as t => identity. A constant may stand in time_parameter next to it.\n\nHd = η * (expim(Δ * t) * a + expim(-Δ * t) * a')\nto_numeric(Hd, h, (10, 2); backend, parameter = Dict(Δ => 0.5), time_parameter = Dict(t => identity, η => 0.2)) isa TimeDependentSum\ntrue","title":"Time-dependent parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Custom-operators-and-lazy-output","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"operators replaces the matrix of an operator by one of your choice, given on the full basis, and the adjoint is replaced along with it. Replacing a by a + alpha displaces the mode numerically.\n\nα = 0.5\na_displaced = embed(b, 1, destroy(bf)) + α * identityoperator(b)\nto_numeric(a' * a, b; operators = Dict(a => a_displaced)) ≈ to_numeric((a' + α) * (a + α), b)\ntrue\n\nThe default result is a sparse matrix. On a large product space, op_type = identity keeps the backend's lazy sum of tensor products and never forms the full matrix.\n\nto_numeric(H, b; parameter, op_type = identity) isa LazySum\ntrue","title":"Custom operators and lazy output"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Indexed-systems","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"A sum over an index is written out over explicit copies of the subsystem, so the basis fixes the number of atoms. It has one factor per copy, and sites maps the position of each subsystem in the symbolic space to the positions of its copies. Parameters that depend on the site are keyed by site, obtained by substituting a number for the index.\n\nusing QuantumOpticsBase\nb = FockBasis(3) ⊗ NLevelBasis(2) ⊗ NLevelBasis(2)\ncouplings = Dict(substitute(g, Dict(index_sym(i) => k)) => 0.1k for k in 1:2)\nHn = to_numeric(H, b, Dict(1 => [1], 2 => [2, 3]); parameter = couplings)\nsize(Hn)\n(16, 16)","title":"Indexed systems"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#Expectation-values","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"numeric_average evaluates an operator expression, or an expression of averages, in a state. For a coherent state with amplitude alpha and the atom in its ground state, langle sigma^ge rangle vanishes and the right-hand side of the equation for langle a rangle is -i(Deltaalpha + eta) = -045i.\n\nψ = coherentstate(bf, α) ⊗ nlevelstate(bn, 1)\nnumeric_average(substitute(da, parameter), ψ)\n0.0 - 0.4499999999999477im\n\nA vector of states, such as the output of a time evolution, gives a vector of values. expect is the same for operator expressions, under the name used by QuantumOptics.jl and QuantumToolbox.jl.\n\nψs = [coherentstate(bf, β) ⊗ nlevelstate(bn, 1) for β in (0.1, 0.5)]\nnumeric_average(a' * a, ψs)\n2-element Vector{ComplexF64}:\n 0.009999999999999998 + 0.0im\n  0.24999999999994768 + 0.0im\n\nexpect(a' * a, ψ)\n0.24999999999994768 + 0.0im\n\nOn a large product space, a LazyKet holds the state of each factor separately and avoids the full state vector.\n\nψl = LazyKet(b, (coherentstate(bf, 0.5), nlevelstate(bn, 2)))\nnumeric_average(average(a' * a + σ(:e, :e)), ψl)\n1.2499999999999432 + 0.0im","title":"Expectation values"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.average","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"average(expr)\n\nExpectation value of an operator expression, as a symbolic scalar.\n\nThe average is linear: it distributes over sums and takes coefficients out, so the result is a sum of averages of operator products, printed as ⟨…⟩. It takes part in ordinary symbolic arithmetic with Symbolics.jl. The average of a Hermitian operator is real. A sum over an index becomes a sum of averages over the same index.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω;\n\njulia> average(ω * a' * a + a)\n⟨a⟩ + ⟨a' * a⟩*ω\n\nSee also undo_average, numeric_average, make_time_dependent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.average"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.undo_average","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"undo_average(expr)\nundo_average(lhs ~ rhs)\n\nOperator expression whose average is expr, the inverse of average.\n\nSums over indices are restored, and so are averages turned into functions of time by make_time_dependent. An equation gives the pair lhs => rhs of operator expressions.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables ω;\n\njulia> undo_average(average(ω * a' * a + a))\na + ω * a' * a\n\nSee also average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.undo_average"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_average","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"is_average(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is the average of an operator expression, as made by average.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> is_average(average(a)), is_average(a)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also average, is_indexed_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.is_average"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.inner_adjoint","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"inner_adjoint(x)\n\nComplex conjugate of an expression of averages, written again as averages.\n\nThe conjugate of langle X rangle is written as langle X^dagger rangle rather than as conj(⟨X⟩), so that equations for averages stay expressed in averages of operators. Coefficients are conjugated as by qadjoint.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables g::Number;\n\njulia> inner_adjoint(g * average(a))\n⟨a'⟩*conj(g)\n\nSee also qadjoint, average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.inner_adjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.make_time_dependent","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"make_time_dependent(expr, t)\n\nReplace every average in expr by an unknown function of time t.\n\nEach average langle X rangle becomes langle X rangle(t), which can be differentiated with respect to t and serves as an unknown of a differential equation, for instance in ModelingToolkit.jl. The unknown is real when X is Hermitian and complex otherwise. undo_average recovers the operators.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> @variables t;\n\njulia> make_time_dependent(average(a' * a) + average(a), t)\n⟨a⟩(t) + ⟨a' * a⟩(t)\n\nSee also average, undo_average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.make_time_dependent"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.fundamental_operators","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"fundamental_operators(h::HilbertSpace; names = Symbol[])\n\nSmallest set of operators from which every operator on h is built by products and adjoints.\n\nA bosonic mode contributes its annihilation operator; a finite-level system its transitions sigma^ij with i  j and its projectors except the ground state; a CollectiveNLevelSpace all of its collective transitions; Pauli and spin systems their three components; and a pair of quadratures position and momentum. On a product space the sets of the factors are joined. names sets the operator names, one per factor; otherwise they are generated.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 3);\n\njulia> fundamental_operators(h; names = [:b, :s])\n6-element Vector{Op}:\n b\n s₁₂\n s₁₃\n s₂₂\n s₂₃\n s₃₃\n\nSee also find_operators, unique_up_to_adjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.fundamental_operators"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.find_operators","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"find_operators(h::HilbertSpace, order::Int; names = Symbol[])\n\nAll distinct products of up to order operators on h, counting adjoints only once.\n\nThe products are built from fundamental_operators and their adjoints, brought into canonical form, and filtered with unique_up_to_adjoint. They are the averages a set of moment or cumulant equations of that order is written in. names is passed on to fundamental_operators.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> find_operators(FockSpace(:cavity), 2)\n3-element Vector{Union{Op, SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd}}:\n a\n a * a\n a' * a\n\nSee also fundamental_operators, unique_up_to_adjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.find_operators"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.unique_up_to_adjoint","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"unique_up_to_adjoint(ops)\n\nOperators of ops with duplicates removed, counting an operator and its adjoint as the same.\n\nThe first of each pair is kept. Equations for averages need only one of langle X rangle and langle X^dagger rangle, since the other is its complex conjugate.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> unique_up_to_adjoint([a, a', a' * a])\n2-element Vector{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField}:\n a\n a' * a\n\nSee also find_operators, fundamental_operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.unique_up_to_adjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"to_numeric(op, h::HilbertSpace, dims; backend, parameter, time_parameter, operators, adjoint_ops, op_type)\nto_numeric(op, basis; kwargs...)\nto_numeric(op, state; kwargs...)\nto_numeric(op, basis_or_state, operators)\nto_numeric(ops::AbstractVector, args...; kwargs...)\n\nMatrix of an operator expression on a truncated basis, for a numerical backend.\n\nThe first form builds the basis from the symbolic space h and works with every backend. dims holds one entry per factor: the photon cutoff for a mode, so 5 gives six states, and the spin length for a spin. The backend is QuantumOpticsBackend() or QuantumToolboxBackend(), and may be left out when only one of the two packages is loaded. The other forms take a basis of the backend, or a state whose basis is used; QuantumToolbox also accepts its own dimension tuple, which counts states rather than photons. The third argument operators replaces operators by given matrices, as the keyword does with adjoint_ops = false. A vector of expressions gives a vector of matrices.\n\nKeywords:\n\nparameter: values of the symbolic parameters. A coefficient that does not reduce to a number throws an ArgumentError.\ntime_parameter: values that depend on time, as functions t -> value or numbers; the time variable itself is given as t => identity. A non-empty time_parameter makes the result time dependent, a TimeDependentSum for QuantumOptics and a QobjEvo for QuantumToolbox. With QuantumToolbox, a function (p, t) -> value of the solver parameters p makes the QobjEvo differentiable in p, for optimal control, and may be mixed with the other entries.\noperators: matrices on the full basis to use for given operators in place of the standard ones.\nadjoint_ops = true: also replace the adjoint of each operator in operators by the adjoint matrix.\nop_type: the default gives a sparse matrix. identity keeps the backend's lazy representation, which saves memory on large product spaces, and another function, such as dense or QuantumToolbox.to_dense, is applied to the result. A time-dependent result is always the backend's time-dependent operator, so only the default and identity are accepted.\n\nFormal operator functions throw an ArgumentError; expand them with taylor first.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using QuantumOpticsBase\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> @qnumbers a::Destroy(h, 1) σ::Transition(h, 1, 2, 2);\n\njulia> @variables g;\n\njulia> Hn = to_numeric(g * (a' * σ + σ' * a), h, (5, 2); backend = QuantumOpticsBackend(), parameter = Dict(g => 0.1));\n\njulia> size(Hn)\n(12, 12)\n\nSee also numeric_average, QuantumOpticsBackend, QuantumToolboxBackend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QuantumOpticsBackend","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"QuantumOpticsBackend()\n\nNumerical backend built on QuantumOpticsBase.jl.\n\nAvailable after using QuantumOpticsBase. Operators become Operators on QuantumOptics bases, and time-dependent ones TimeDependentSums, ready for the solvers of QuantumOptics.jl.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using QuantumOpticsBase\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> to_numeric(a' * a, h, 4; backend = QuantumOpticsBackend()) ≈ number(FockBasis(4))\ntrue\n\nSee also QuantumToolboxBackend, to_numeric.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QuantumOpticsBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QuantumToolboxBackend","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"QuantumToolboxBackend()\n\nNumerical backend built on QuantumToolbox.jl.\n\nAvailable after using QuantumToolbox. Operators become QuantumObjects, and time-dependent ones QobjEvos, ready for the solvers of QuantumToolbox.jl.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> import QuantumToolbox\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity); a = Destroy(h, :a);\n\njulia> to_numeric(a' * a, h, 4; backend = QuantumToolboxBackend()) ≈ QuantumToolbox.num(5)\ntrue\n\nSee also QuantumOpticsBackend, to_numeric.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QuantumToolboxBackend"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QAdd, QuantumInterface.Basis, AbstractDict{Int64, Vector{Int64}}}","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"to_numeric(expr, basis, sites::Dict{Int, Vector{Int}}; parameter, operators)\nto_numeric(expr, basis, sites::Dict{Int, Vector{Int}}[, operators[, parameter]])\n\nMatrix of an expression with sums over indices, on a basis with one factor per site.\n\nEach sum is written out over the sites. sites maps the position of each subsystem in the symbolic space to the positions of its copies in basis; a subsystem without an index has a single copy. Pairs of indices that must differ skip their coinciding sites. parameter gives the values of all parameters. A site-dependent parameter g_i is keyed by site, with the value of g_1 under substitute(g, Dict(index_sym(i) => 1)). operators maps operators to matrices as for the other forms. This form is available for QuantumOpticsBase only.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using QuantumOpticsBase\n\njulia> h = FockSpace(:cavity) ⊗ NLevelSpace(:atom, 2);\n\njulia> a = Destroy(h, :a, 1); i = Index(h, :i, 2, 2);\n\njulia> σ(k, l) = IndexedOperator(Transition(h, :σ, k, l, 2), i);\n\njulia> g = IndexedVariable(:g, i); H = Σ(g * (a' * σ(1, 2) + a * σ(2, 1)), i);\n\njulia> b = FockBasis(3) ⊗ NLevelBasis(2) ⊗ NLevelBasis(2);\n\njulia> gs = Dict(substitute(g, Dict(index_sym(i) => k)) => 0.1k for k in 1:2);\n\njulia> size(to_numeric(H, b, Dict(1 => [1], 2 => [2, 3]); parameter = gs))\n(16, 16)\n\nSee also Σ, index_sym.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.to_numeric"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_average","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"numeric_average(x, state[, operators])\nnumeric_average(x, states::AbstractVector[, operators])\n\nExpectation value of an operator expression or of an expression of averages in a numerical state.\n\nx is either an operator expression or a result of average, with every parameter already replaced by a number. The state is a ket or a density matrix of the backend, and its basis fixes the truncation; a vector of states gives a vector of values. operators maps operators to the matrices to use in place of the standard ones.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using QuantumOpticsBase\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> ψ = coherentstate(FockBasis(20), 0.5);\n\njulia> numeric_average(a' * a, ψ) ≈ 0.25\ntrue\n\njulia> numeric_average(average(a) + average(a'), ψ) ≈ 1\ntrue\n\nSee also expect, to_numeric, average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SecondQuantizedAlgebra.numeric_average"},{"category":"method","location":"manual/averages_numerics/#QuantumInterface.expect-Tuple{SecondQuantizedAlgebra.QField, Any}","page":"Averages and numerics","text":"expect(op, state[, operators])\n\nExpectation value of an operator expression, the same as numeric_average.\n\nThe name matches expect of QuantumOptics.jl and QuantumToolbox.jl. Unlike numeric_average, it does not accept expressions of averages.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using QuantumOpticsBase\n\njulia> a = Destroy(FockSpace(:cavity), :a);\n\njulia> expect(a' * a, fockstate(FockBasis(5), 2)) ≈ 2\ntrue\n\nSee also numeric_average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumInterface.expect"}]
}
